const FINAL_ANSWERS = {"answers":{"acute-transient-psychotic-disorder-course-outcome":{"title":"Course and outcome of acute and transient psychotic disorders (ATPD)","status":"draft","updated":"2026-10-20","reviewer":[],"covers":["p1-cee74827"],"exam":"<p><strong>Definition.</strong> Acute and transient psychotic disorder (ICD-11 <strong>6A23</strong>; ICD-10 F23) is a psychosis with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>acute onset</strong> (from non-psychotic to clearly psychotic within 2 weeks);</li>\n<li><strong>polymorphic, rapidly changing</strong> positive symptoms (delusions, hallucinations, perceptual disturbance, disorganisation) with emotional turmoil and perplexity;</li>\n<li><strong>full remission within 3 months</strong>, usually within days to 1 month.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is not explained by mood disorder, substances or an organic cause [1,2]. ICD-11 dropped ICD-10&#39;s four subtypes and kept only single vs recurrent episode [1]. In DSM-5-TR, the equivalent is <strong>brief psychotic disorder</strong> (remits within 1 month) or <strong>schizophreniform disorder</strong> (1–6 months) [1].</p>\n<h3>Mechanism</h3>\n<p>Stress–vulnerability model: lower genetic loading + acute psychosocial stress (marriage, migration, job loss) or physiological stress (fever, childbirth) → transient hyperarousal (P300 latency shorter than controls even in remission; frontotemporal <strong>hypermetabolism</strong> on FDG-PET, the opposite of the hypofrontality of schizophrenia) and a pro-inflammatory state (raised IL-6, TNF-α, TGF-β) → brief polymorphic psychosis → rapid recovery [1,2]. Patients with a family history of ATPD need less stress to become ill, and vice versa [1,2].</p>\n<h3>Course and outcome — key data</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Dimension</th>\n<th>Findings</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Recovery from episode</td>\n<td>Most remit within 4 weeks of treatment; minimal residual or negative symptoms [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chandigarh 5-year follow-up</td>\n<td>75% complete recovery with no residual symptoms [2]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>WHO CAP cohort, 20-year follow-up (Chandigarh)</td>\n<td>Up to 82% excellent outcome, no relapse, no residual symptoms [2]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recurrence</td>\n<td>22% at 5 years (DOSMeD); 35% at 5 years (Rozario); 46.6% at 8 years (Malhotra); 30–38% in 2 years, up to 54% later (meta-analysis) [1,2]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Diagnostic stability</td>\n<td>Retained in 30–73% across studies; pooled ≈0.56 over 4.5 years; 87% at 8 years in Jørgensen [1,2]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Change to schizophrenia</td>\n<td>15% at 1 year in one series; 1 in 17 at 12 years in the Chandigarh DOSMeD cohort [2]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Change to affective disorder</td>\n<td>~28% at 1 year in one series [2]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Medication-free remission</td>\n<td>Up to ~60% stay well without medication [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mortality and suicide</td>\n<td>SMR similar to schizophrenia and bipolar disorder; suicidal behaviour 36–55% in the acute polymorphic phase [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compared with first-episode schizophrenia</td>\n<td>Better long-term prognosis (meta-analysis of 82 studies) [1]</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Predictors</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Good outcome</th>\n<th>Poor outcome / conversion to schizophrenia</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Female sex [2]</td>\n<td>Male sex [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Acute stress at onset [2]</td>\n<td>Younger age at onset [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Polymorphic picture <strong>without</strong> schizophrenic symptoms [1,2]</td>\n<td>First-rank symptoms, thought disorder [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Good premorbid adjustment [1]</td>\n<td>Family history of schizophrenia [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Developing-country setting, rural, low SES [2]</td>\n<td>Predominantly delusional or schizophrenia-like subtype [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Postpartum or febrile precipitant [2]</td>\n<td>Longer episode duration</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Assessment</h3>\n<p>Rule out organic causes in every case: fever, delirium, NMDA-receptor and other <strong>autoimmune encephalitis</strong> (acute onset, catatonia, autonomic instability, seizures; check anti-neuronal antibodies), substance use, postpartum medical causes, epilepsy. Assess stressors, premorbid function, family history and suicide risk. Use longitudinal follow-up at 3 months to confirm the diagnosis [1].</p>\n<h3>Management</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Brief hospitalisation if risk is high.</li>\n<li>Low-dose antipsychotic: haloperidol, risperidone or olanzapine give dramatic improvement within about 4 weeks [1].</li>\n<li>Benzodiazepines for agitation.</li>\n<li>Address the stressor; psychoeducation for the family.</li>\n<li>Continue medication for about 3 months after remission, then taper with monitoring. Long-term maintenance is not routinely needed, except for recurrent cases or emerging schizophrenia.</li>\n<li>Follow up for recurrence and change of diagnosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Key evidence</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>WHO IPSS, DOSMeD and CAP</strong> studies established non-affective acute remitting psychosis (NARP), which is about 10 times more common in developing countries and more common in women [2].</li>\n<li><strong>Chandigarh studies (Malhotra):</strong> 75–82% excellent outcome; stress, female sex and absence of schizophrenic symptoms predict good outcome [2].</li>\n<li><strong>Fusar-Poli 2016 meta-analysis:</strong> brief psychotic episodes have better prognosis than remitted first-episode schizophrenia [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conclusion.</strong> ATPD is a common, mostly <strong>benign</strong> psychosis in India and other low- and middle-income countries: most patients recover fully, often stay well without medication, and keep the diagnosis. A substantial minority, however, recur (about 1 in 3) or convert to schizophrenia or bipolar disorder (up to about 1 in 3 in some series), and acute-phase suicide risk is high. Treat briefly, rule out organic and autoimmune causes, and follow up long enough to catch the minority with a poor course.</p>\n","depth":"<h3>Background</h3>\n<p>Acute remitting &quot;third psychoses&quot; were described in many countries before ICD-10 [2]:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>France: <em>bouffée délirante</em> (Magnan).</li>\n<li>Germany: cycloid psychosis (Kleist, Leonhard); motility psychosis.</li>\n<li>Scandinavia: psychogenic/reactive psychosis; Langfeldt&#39;s schizophreniform psychosis.</li>\n<li>USA: good-prognosis schizophrenia; hysterical psychosis.</li>\n<li>Japan: atypical psychosis (Mitsuda).</li>\n<li>Africa and the West Indies: acute psychotic reactions.</li>\n<li>India: &quot;acute psychosis of uncertain origin&quot;, hysterical psychosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Common features were sudden onset, fluid polymorphic symptoms, prominent affect and fear, a precipitant, good premorbid adjustment and complete recovery [2].</p>\n<p>WHO studies (IPSS 1968–70, DOSMeD 1978–80, CAP 1980–82) confirmed this group, which led to F23 in ICD-10 (1992). ICD-10 itself acknowledged that &quot;the nomenclature of these acute disorders is as uncertain as their nosological status&quot; [1,2]. Savita Malhotra&#39;s DLN Murthy Rao Oration (2007) summarised the extensive Indian validation work [2].</p>\n<h3>Epidemiology</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Annual incidence 3.9–9.6 per 100,000 in UK and Danish data; 5.8–20% of clinic and hospital psychosis samples [1].</li>\n<li>In the CAP study (n=1,004), 41.7% had stress close to onset and two-thirds were well with no relapse at 1 year [2].</li>\n<li>ICMR multicentre study (Bikaner, Goa, Patiala, Vellore; n=323): psychological stress in 50% and physiological stress (fever, childbirth) in another 20% [2].</li>\n<li>Female preponderance (in most but not all studies), low SES, rural background, migrants, a summer peak in onset (Chandigarh), childbirth within 3 months before onset, and short non-specific fever beforehand [1,2].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Classification and criteria</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>System</th>\n<th>Category</th>\n<th>Duration rule</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>ICD-10 F23</td>\n<td>Acute polymorphic without (F23.0) / with (F23.1) schizophrenic symptoms; acute schizophrenia-like (F23.2); predominantly delusional (F23.3); 5th digit for associated acute stress</td>\n<td>Remission within 3 months; schizophrenic symptoms ≤1 month (else F20) [2]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ICD-11 6A23</td>\n<td>Single episode / multiple episodes; polymorphic picture required</td>\n<td>Remission within 3 months; typically days to 1 month [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DSM-5-TR</td>\n<td>Brief psychotic disorder (with/without marked stressor; with postpartum onset)</td>\n<td>1 day to &lt;1 month</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DSM-5-TR</td>\n<td>Schizophreniform disorder</td>\n<td>1–6 months</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Mismatch example: a 6-week psychosis with schizophrenic symptoms is schizophrenia in ICD-11 but schizophreniform disorder in DSM-5. This lack of harmony has limited research [1].</p>\n<h3>Mechanism in detail</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Genetics:</strong> family history of ATPD is 3 times higher, and of schizophrenia 4 times lower, in relatives of ATPD probands than schizophrenia probands. Schizophrenia in relatives is seen mainly when the proband has schizophrenic symptoms. Affective illness rates are similar. A Danish register found an RR of 1.93 with any family psychiatric history. A Japanese GWAS of &quot;atypical psychosis&quot; found partial overlap with schizophrenia but not bipolar disorder [1,2].</li>\n<li><strong>Neurophysiology:</strong> in remission, ATPD showed higher P300 amplitude and shorter latency than schizophrenia, and shorter latency even than healthy controls, which suggests hyperarousal (Indian study, 25 per group) [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Imaging:</strong> FDG-PET within 2 weeks of onset showed global (frontal and temporal) hypermetabolism in ATPD vs frontal hypometabolism in schizophrenia (PGIMER; n=17 vs 12) [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Immunology:</strong> raised IL-6, TNF-α, TGF-β; TNF-α still higher at follow-up (n=41) [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Biochemistry:</strong> altered amino-acid pathways, raised bilirubin; in cycloid psychosis, raised glycine and low tryptophan [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Autoimmune overlap:</strong> some &quot;ATPD&quot; cases may be NMDAR or VGKC antibody encephalitis. Screen first-episode acute psychosis with atypical features [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Neurocognition:</strong> impaired acutely but processing speed recovers within 6 months; brief psychotic disorder is the least impaired first-episode subgroup (Ayesa-Arriola) [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Clinical features in detail</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Polymorphic:</strong> delusions and hallucinations that change from day to day, emotional turmoil (ecstasy, terror), perplexity, confusion without clouding, and fluctuating anxiety [1,2].</li>\n<li><strong>Schizophrenia-like:</strong> first-rank symptoms with a stable picture over &lt;1 month.</li>\n<li><strong>Predominantly delusional.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>With acute stress:</strong> onset within 2 weeks of a stressor.</li>\n<li><strong>Postpartum:</strong> onset within weeks after delivery.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Negative symptoms are minimal or absent [1].</p>\n<h3>Assessment in detail</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Area</th>\n<th>Purpose</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Temperature, vitals, sensorium</td>\n<td>Exclude delirium and infection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CBC, electrolytes, glucose, LFT/RFT, thyroid</td>\n<td>Organic screen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Urine drug screen</td>\n<td>Substances</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HIV, VDRL</td>\n<td>Infectious causes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>EEG</td>\n<td>Seizures, encephalopathy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MRI brain, CSF, anti-NMDAR/VGKC antibodies</td>\n<td>If catatonia, dyskinesia, seizures, autonomic instability or reduced consciousness [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Postpartum: anaemia, sepsis, thyroiditis</td>\n<td>Postpartum causes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stress and premorbid assessment</td>\n<td>Prognosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Suicide risk</td>\n<td>High in acute polymorphic phase [1]</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Differential diagnosis</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Condition</th>\n<th>Distinguishing feature</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Schizophrenia / schizophreniform</td>\n<td>Longer duration; insidious onset; negative symptoms; hypofrontality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bipolar mania or mixed state</td>\n<td>Sustained elevated or irritable mood, overactivity; family history of BPAD</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Psychotic depression</td>\n<td>Pervasive low mood; mood-congruent delusions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Delirium</td>\n<td>Clouded consciousness, fluctuating attention, medical cause</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Autoimmune encephalitis</td>\n<td>Catatonia, seizures, dyskinesias, autonomic instability [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Substance-induced psychosis</td>\n<td>Temporal link; toxicology</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dissociative trance/possession</td>\n<td>Culturally patterned, often episodic, with amnesia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>BLIPS (clinical high risk)</td>\n<td>Under 1 week, spontaneous resolution; ~70% of BLIPS also meet ATPD criteria [1]</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Management details</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Acute:</strong> a safe environment; IM lorazepam or haloperidol + promethazine for severe agitation; low-dose oral haloperidol, risperidone or olanzapine; ECT if catatonic or life-threatening [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Duration:</strong> no formal guidelines exist [1]. Clinical practice is 1–3 months after remission, then taper over weeks. Many patients stay well without medication [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Recurrent ATPD:</strong> consider longer prophylaxis; review the diagnosis (bipolar or cycloid course); lithium if there is an affective component.</li>\n<li><strong>Postpartum ATPD:</strong> manage as postpartum psychosis (mother–baby unit, check breastfeeding compatibility of medication).</li>\n<li><strong>Psychosocial:</strong> address stressors (marital, migration); family psychoeducation; avoid stigmatising labels; plan follow-up visits at 3 and 6 months, then yearly.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Evidence table</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Study</th>\n<th>Design</th>\n<th>n / follow-up</th>\n<th>Finding</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>WHO CAP (Cooper 1990) [2]</td>\n<td>14 centres, 7 countries</td>\n<td>1,004 / 1 year</td>\n<td>41.2% schizophrenic symptoms; two-thirds well at 1 year</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DOSMeD (Susser &amp; Wanderling) [2]</td>\n<td>First-onset psychosis</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>NARP 10× more common in developing countries; bimodal duration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chandigarh DOSMeD cohort [2]</td>\n<td>Follow-up</td>\n<td>17 / 12 years</td>\n<td>14 of 17 remained well; 1 developed schizophrenia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Malhotra et al. [2]</td>\n<td>ATPD cohort</td>\n<td>8 years</td>\n<td>Recurrence 46.6%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rozario et al. [2]</td>\n<td>ATPD cohort</td>\n<td>5 years</td>\n<td>75% complete recovery; 35% recurrence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Malhotra 2000 [2]</td>\n<td>WHO CAP cohort</td>\n<td>20 years</td>\n<td>Up to 82% excellent outcome</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Jørgensen [2]</td>\n<td>Cohort</td>\n<td>8 years</td>\n<td>87% diagnostic stability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fusar-Poli et al., 2016 [1]</td>\n<td>Meta-analysis</td>\n<td>82 studies</td>\n<td>Brief psychotic episodes have better prognosis than remitted first-episode schizophrenia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rutigliano et al., 2018 [1]</td>\n<td>Meta-analysis, first-episode psychosis</td>\n<td>42 studies, 4.5 years</td>\n<td>ATPD diagnostic stability ≈0.56</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Critical appraisal</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Validity:</strong> ATPD passes several Robins–Guze/Feighner validators (distinct clinical picture, follow-up, family studies, early biological markers), but biological data come from small, often single-centre Indian studies [1,2].</li>\n<li><strong>Heterogeneity:</strong> ICD-10 subtypes had poor validity in field trials; ICD-11 simplified this. Schizophrenia-like and delusional forms behave more like schizophrenia [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Duration criteria are arbitrary.</strong> DOSMeD data suggest natural episodes may last up to 28 weeks, longer than the 3 months ICD allows [2].</li>\n<li><strong>Culture and context:</strong> higher incidence and better outcome in LMICs may reflect true biology, social factors, or diagnostic habits.</li>\n<li><strong>Neuroinflammatory hypothesis:</strong> some ATPD may be undiagnosed autoimmune or infective encephalitis [1].</li>\n<li><strong>CHR overlap:</strong> BLIPS and ATPD overlap diagnostically and prognostically, which raises the question of whether ATPD belongs in a high-risk framework [1].</li>\n<li><strong>No treatment guidelines or RCTs</strong> exist specifically for ATPD [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Indian context</h3>\n<p>India is the most important source of ATPD research: the PGIMER Chandigarh studies, the ICMR multicentre study, and NIMHANS neurobiology [1,2]. ATPD is common in Indian emergency and inpatient settings, especially among women, rural patients and postpartum mothers. Clinicians must avoid labelling these patients &quot;schizophrenia&quot; too early, because that label brings stigma and consequences for marriage and employment. MHCA 2017 provisions on supported admission and emergency treatment (72 hours) apply in the acute phase.</p>\n<h3>Recent advances</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Established:</strong> ICD-11 simplification (6A23, single vs multiple episode).</li>\n<li><strong>Emerging:</strong><ul>\n<li>PET hypermetabolism, P300 hyperarousal and inflammatory markers as candidate biomarkers [1].</li>\n<li>Routine autoimmune screening in atypical acute psychosis [1].</li>\n<li>Integration with the CHR-P framework [1].</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Investigational:</strong> immunomodulatory treatment in antibody-positive acute psychosis; dimensional (HiTOP/RDoC) approaches across ATPD, schizophrenia and bipolar disorder.</li>\n</ul>\n","refs":"<ol>\n<li>Malhotra S, Sahoo S, Balachander S. Acute and transient psychotic disorders: newer understanding. <em>Curr Psychiatry Rep</em> 2019; 21:113.</li>\n<li>Malhotra S. Acute and transient psychosis: a paradigmatic approach (DLN Murthy Rao Oration). <em>Indian J Psychiatry</em> 2007; 49(4):233–243.</li>\n<li>World Health Organization. <em>ICD-11 for Mortality and Morbidity Statistics</em> (6A23 Acute and transient psychotic disorder). 2022.</li>\n<li>American Psychiatric Association. <em>DSM-5-TR</em>. 2022; Brief psychotic disorder, Schizophreniform disorder.</li>\n<li>Fusar-Poli P, Cappucciati M, Bonoldi I, et al. Prognosis of brief psychotic episodes: a meta-analysis. <em>JAMA Psychiatry</em> 2016; 73:211–220.</li>\n</ol>\n"},"antipsychotics-in-clinical-high-risk-for-psychosis":{"title":"Antipsychotics for people at clinical high risk (at-risk mental state) for psychosis — pros and cons","status":"draft","updated":"2026-10-20","reviewer":[],"covers":["p1-ab084c9a"],"exam":"<p><strong>Definition.</strong> The <strong>at-risk mental state (ARMS)</strong>, also called ultra-high risk (UHR) or clinical high risk (CHR), describes help-seeking young people with subthreshold psychotic features and declining function who have a markedly raised short-term risk of developing a psychotic disorder [1]. The <strong>Melbourne UHR criteria</strong> (PACE clinic; Yung 2004) require age 15–25, referral for help, a drop in function (or sustained low function) for ≥1 month in the past year, <strong>and</strong> one of the following [1]:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Attenuated psychotic symptoms (APS):</strong> subthreshold ideas of reference, odd beliefs, perceptual disturbance, paranoid ideation or odd speech, several times a week, for 1 week to 5 years.</li>\n<li><strong>Brief limited intermittent psychotic symptoms (BLIPS):</strong> frank psychotic symptoms lasting under 1 week that resolve spontaneously.</li>\n<li><strong>Trait vulnerability:</strong> schizotypal personality disorder, or a first-degree relative with psychosis, plus functional decline.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Mechanism (why antipsychotics were tried)</h3>\n<p>Rising presynaptic striatal dopamine synthesis capacity during transition (Howes 2011) → aberrant salience → psychosis. The hypothesis: early D2 blockade could prevent sensitisation and transition, and limit the &quot;toxic&quot; effects of untreated psychosis [1].</p>\n<h3>The risk to be treated</h3>\n<p>Transition rates (Fusar-Poli 2012 meta-analysis): <strong>18% at 6 months, 22% at 1 year, 29% at 2 years, 36% at 3 years</strong>. That is roughly 400 times the population risk, but <strong>most do not convert</strong>, and rates have fallen in established clinics [1].</p>\n<h3>Pros and cons</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pros (arguments for)</th>\n<th>Cons (arguments against)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Psychosis is disabling; preventing or delaying even a minority of cases is valuable [1]</td>\n<td><strong>Most (over 60%) are false positives</strong> at 3 years; many would be treated unnecessarily [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Early trials: McGorry 2002 (risperidone + CBT) cut transition at 6 months; McGlashan 2006 (olanzapine) showed a trend to benefit [1]</td>\n<td>Benefit <strong>not sustained</strong> after stopping drug (Phillips 2007); effects may just delay onset [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>May reduce DUP and the severity of first episode</td>\n<td><strong>Adverse effects:</strong> weight gain with olanzapine, EPS with risperidone [1]; metabolic and prolactin effects in adolescents</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Useful when APS rapidly worsen with risk to self or others (Australian guideline exception) [1]</td>\n<td><strong>Iatrogenic dopamine supersensitivity</strong>, rebound on withdrawal, brain volume changes [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Low dose may be acceptable to some families</td>\n<td><strong>Stigma and labelling</strong> of young people; self-stigma [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>Non-specific interventions (supportive therapy, monitoring, CBT) do as well on transition; NNT for any intervention ≈ 9 at 12 months [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>ARMS youth have high rates of depression, anxiety and substance use, which have their own effective treatments [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td><strong>NICE (2014):</strong> &quot;Do not offer antipsychotic medication&quot; to people at increased risk [1]</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Assessment</h3>\n<p>CAARMS (Comprehensive Assessment of At-Risk Mental States) or SIPS/SOPS; basic symptoms (SPI-A); comorbidity (depression, anxiety, substance use); function (SOFAS/GAF); risk (suicide); family history; cannabis; physical health; organic exclusion where indicated [1].</p>\n<h3>Management (recommended approach)</h3>\n<p>Engage and psychoeducate (non-stigmatising language) → case management, monitoring of mental state and risk → <strong>CBT</strong> (stress–vulnerability model) ± family intervention → treat comorbid depression/anxiety (SSRIs) and substance use → address stressors (education, work, housing) → regular review for transition (CAARMS) → <strong>low-dose atypical antipsychotic only if</strong> psychotic symptoms rapidly worsen with significant functional decline and risk, with close monitoring → if transition occurs, treat as first-episode psychosis (early intervention service) [1].</p>\n<h3>Key evidence</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>McGorry 2002 (PACE):</strong> risperidone 1–2 mg + CBT vs needs-based intervention; transition 10% vs 36% at 6 months; difference lost by 12 months in non-adherent patients [1].</li>\n<li><strong>McGlashan 2006 (PRIME):</strong> olanzapine vs placebo; transition 16% vs 35% (not significant); weight gain about 9 kg [1].</li>\n<li><strong>McGorry 2013:</strong> CBT + risperidone, CBT + placebo and supportive therapy + placebo all had similar low transition [1].</li>\n<li><strong>van der Gaag 2013 meta-analysis:</strong> NNT 9 across interventions; CBT best supported [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Amminger 2010 (omega-3):</strong> promising but not replicated (NEURAPRO) [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conclusion.</strong> Routine antipsychotic treatment of people at clinical high risk is <strong>not justified</strong>. The predictive value is too low, benefits do not last, harms are real, and safer psychological and case-management approaches are as effective. Antipsychotics should be reserved for rapid worsening with risk, at low dose and with close review. In practice, this means &quot;treat the person and the comorbidity, monitor the psychosis&quot;.</p>\n","depth":"<h3>Background</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Sullivan (1927) proposed early intervention; Chapman (1966) described early symptoms retrospectively [1].</li>\n<li>Huber&#39;s Bonn school described <strong>basic symptoms</strong>: subjective disturbances of thought, perception and drive [1].</li>\n<li>Yung and McGorry set up the PACE clinic in Melbourne (1994) and defined the UHR criteria prospectively, moving away from the retrospective DSM-III prodrome [1].</li>\n<li>In the USA, Miller and McGlashan developed SIPS/SOPS (2003) and the PRIME trial [1].</li>\n<li>DSM-5 (2013) placed <strong>attenuated psychosis syndrome</strong> in Section III (conditions for further study) after major debate. In practice it can be coded as &quot;other specified schizophrenia spectrum disorder&quot; [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Epidemiology</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Transition rates have fallen: Melbourne&#39;s early cohorts converted at about 40% in 12 months, later cohorts at about 15% (the &quot;dilution effect&quot; from earlier and wider referral) [1].</li>\n<li>The risk continues long term; in Nelson 2013, about 35% converted by 10 years.</li>\n<li>Among non-transitioned UHR cases, many have persistent or new non-psychotic disorders and poor function (Lin 2015) [1].</li>\n<li>Indian data are limited; studies from NIMHANS and PGIMER suggest similar psychopathology but lower help-seeking for attenuated symptoms.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Classification and criteria</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Approach</th>\n<th>Criteria / tool</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>UHR (Melbourne)</td>\n<td>APS, BLIPS, trait + decline; CAARMS</td>\n<td>Most used in UK, Australia [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CHR (North America)</td>\n<td>SIPS/SOPS; Criteria of Prodromal Syndromes</td>\n<td>Similar groups [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Basic symptoms</td>\n<td>COPER, COGDIS; SPI-A</td>\n<td>Earlier stage; sensitivity 98%, specificity 59% (Klosterkötter 2001) [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Self-disturbance</td>\n<td>EASE (Parnas 2005)</td>\n<td>Phenomenological; needs training [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DSM-5 attenuated psychosis syndrome</td>\n<td>Section III</td>\n<td>Not an official diagnosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ICD-11</td>\n<td>No specific category; schizotypal disorder (6A22) is placed in the psychosis grouping</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Mechanism in detail</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dopamine:</strong> progressive rise in striatal dopamine synthesis capacity in those who transition (Howes 2011) [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Glutamate:</strong> altered thalamic and hippocampal glutamate; lower thalamic glutamate predicts poor functional outcome (Allen 2015) [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Structure:</strong> parahippocampal grey matter reduction; multi-site imaging classifiers (Koutsouleris 2015) [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Electrophysiology:</strong> mismatch negativity (MMN), P300 and sensory gating deficits; MMN predicts onset [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Blood markers:</strong> a 15-analyte assay (inflammation, oxidative stress, hormones) showed predictive potential (Perkins 2015) [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Clinical features in detail — predictors of transition</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Melbourne (Yung 2004b):</strong> APS plus genetic risk, long symptom duration, poor social function, poor attention; at least one of these gave PPV 80.8% [1].</li>\n<li><strong>NAPLS (Cannon 2008, n=291):</strong> five predictors (genetic risk with decline, unusual thought content, suspiciousness, poor social function, substance use); combined PPV up to 81% [1].</li>\n<li><strong>EPOS (Ruhrmann 2010):</strong> six-variable prognostic index (positive symptoms, bizarre thinking, sleep disturbance, schizotypy, GAF, education); PPV 83.3% [1].</li>\n<li>BLIPS converts more often than APS.</li>\n<li>Clinician gestalt (&quot;praecox feeling&quot;) had PPV of only 32% (Nelson 2010) [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Assessment in detail</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Domain</th>\n<th>Tool / investigation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Risk status</td>\n<td>CAARMS (intensity, frequency, duration of positive symptoms) or SIPS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Basic symptoms</td>\n<td>SPI-A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Function</td>\n<td>SOFAS, GAF</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Comorbidity</td>\n<td>Structured interview for depression, anxiety, OCD, substance use</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cognition</td>\n<td>Brief battery (attention, working memory, verbal fluency)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Physical</td>\n<td>Weight, BMI, glucose, lipids, prolactin before any antipsychotic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Organic screen</td>\n<td>Only when indicated: thyroid, drugs, EEG/MRI for atypical features</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Differential diagnosis</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Condition</th>\n<th>Distinguishing feature</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>First-episode psychosis</td>\n<td>Full-threshold symptoms over 1 week (CAARMS threshold)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Schizotypal personality disorder</td>\n<td>Long-standing, stable traits without recent decline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Depression with psychotic-like experiences</td>\n<td>Mood-congruent, accompanies mood episode</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Substance-induced experiences</td>\n<td>Clear temporal link with cannabis or stimulants</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>OCD with poor insight</td>\n<td>Intrusive, ego-dystonic, ritual-linked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Autism spectrum</td>\n<td>Lifelong social difficulty, no decline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Normal adolescent unusual experiences</td>\n<td>No distress, help-seeking or decline</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Management details</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Psychological:</strong> CBT (normalising, stress management, appraisal of unusual experiences); family work; vocational and educational support [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Omega-3</strong> (1.2 g/day EPA+DHA): safe, but the evidence is weak after NEURAPRO [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Antidepressants:</strong> naturalistic data (Cornblatt 2007) suggested benefit; treat depression and anxiety on their own merits [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Antipsychotics if used:</strong> low-dose second-generation drug (e.g., aripiprazole 2–5 mg, risperidone 0.5–2 mg); avoid olanzapine in youth because of weight; set a review date and a stopping plan; obtain informed consent including uncertainty [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Adolescents:</strong> involve parents; consider the effect on school; metabolic sensitivity.</li>\n<li><strong>Monitoring:</strong> at least monthly in the first year; urgent pathway if transition occurs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Evidence table</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Trial</th>\n<th>Intervention</th>\n<th>n</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>McGorry et al., 2002 [1]</td>\n<td>Risperidone + CBT vs needs-based</td>\n<td>59</td>\n<td>Transition 10% vs 36% at 6 months; lost at 12 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>McGlashan et al., 2006 (PRIME) [1]</td>\n<td>Olanzapine vs placebo</td>\n<td>60</td>\n<td>16% vs 35% at 1 year (NS); weight gain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ruhrmann et al., 2007 [1]</td>\n<td>Amisulpride + needs-focused (open)</td>\n<td>124</td>\n<td>Symptom improvement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Morrison et al., 2004 / 2012 (EDIE) [1]</td>\n<td>CBT vs monitoring</td>\n<td>58 / 288</td>\n<td>Positive small trial; larger trial no difference in transition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>van der Gaag et al., 2012 [1]</td>\n<td>CBT</td>\n<td>201</td>\n<td>Transition halved at 18 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>McGorry et al., 2013 [1]</td>\n<td>CBT+risperidone vs CBT+placebo vs supportive+placebo</td>\n<td>115</td>\n<td>No difference</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Amminger et al., 2010 [1]</td>\n<td>Omega-3 vs placebo</td>\n<td>81</td>\n<td>4.9% vs 27.5% at 12 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>McGorry et al., 2017 (NEURAPRO)</td>\n<td>Omega-3 vs placebo</td>\n<td>304</td>\n<td>No difference</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fusar-Poli et al., 2012 [1]</td>\n<td>Meta-analysis of transition</td>\n<td>2,502</td>\n<td>18%→36% over 6 months→3 years</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Critical appraisal</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Prediction:</strong> PPV of UHR criteria depends on the base rate. In non-help-seeking or community samples, PPV falls sharply [1]. Prognostic models (EPOS, NAPLS calculators) improve PPV but need external validation.</li>\n<li><strong>Trial quality:</strong> small samples, falling transition rates mean underpowered trials, and short follow-up. Active &quot;control&quot; arms may themselves be effective [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Outcome choice:</strong> transition is a dichotomous threshold. Functional outcome and comorbidity may matter more to young people [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Ethics:</strong><ul>\n<li>Labelling and stigma.</li>\n<li>The right not to know the prognosis.</li>\n<li>Tension between reassurance and monitoring, where monitoring itself may raise anxiety.</li>\n<li>Legal implications of an &quot;attenuated psychosis&quot; label [1].</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Counter-argument:</strong> if any intervention can delay or soften psychosis, even for a minority, it is worth offering with care [1]. Antipsychotics are simply not the best first choice.</li>\n<li><strong>The pharmacological case</strong> may revive with non-D2 agents (muscarinic agonists, glycine-transporter inhibitors), which might avoid D2-related harms; these are still investigational.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Indian context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Few dedicated ARMS services exist. Some early intervention programmes, such as NIMHANS early psychosis clinics and SCARF Chennai&#39;s first-episode services, see help-seeking youth.</li>\n<li>Pathways to care often go first to faith healers, so attenuated symptoms may be interpreted spiritually and DUP is long. Community awareness and training of general practitioners and school counsellors matter more than specialist ARMS clinics.</li>\n<li>The risk of over-prescribing antipsychotics is real in busy outpatient settings where psychological therapy is scarce. The NICE/Australian caution is directly relevant [1].</li>\n<li>MHCA 2017: informed consent and the rights of minors (guardian involvement) apply; avoid coercive &quot;preventive&quot; treatment.</li>\n<li>Cannabis (bhang, ganja) use in youth is an important modifiable risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Recent advances</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Established:</strong> NICE recommends CBT ± family intervention for ARMS, not antipsychotics [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Emerging:</strong> the &quot;clinical staging&quot; model (McGorry), with transdiagnostic youth services (headspace, Orygen) treating early distress regardless of eventual diagnosis; individualised risk calculators (NAPLS-2, PSYCHS).</li>\n<li><strong>Investigational:</strong> ulotaront and other non-D2 agents in CHR; machine-learning multimodal prediction (PRONIA); sequential multiple assignment trials (STEP, Australia); the AMP SCZ consortium.</li>\n</ul>\n","refs":"<ol>\n<li>Thompson A, Marwaha S, Broome MR. At-risk mental state for psychosis: identification and current treatment approaches. <em>BJPsych Advances</em> 2016; 22:186–193.</li>\n<li>Fusar-Poli P, Bonoldi I, Yung AR, et al. Predicting psychosis: meta-analysis of transition outcomes in individuals at high clinical risk. <em>Arch Gen Psychiatry</em> 2012; 69:220–229.</li>\n<li>National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. <em>Psychosis and schizophrenia in adults: prevention and management</em> (CG178). 2014.</li>\n<li>McGorry PD, Nelson B, Markulev C, et al. Effect of ω-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids in young people at ultrahigh risk for psychotic disorders: the NEURAPRO randomized clinical trial. <em>JAMA Psychiatry</em> 2017; 74:19–27.</li>\n<li>Taylor DM, Barnes TRE, Young AH. <em>The Maudsley Prescribing Guidelines in Psychiatry</em>. 15th ed. Wiley, 2025; Chapter 1 and Chapter 5 (Psychosis in children and adolescents).</li>\n<li>American Psychiatric Association. <em>DSM-5</em>. 2013; Section III: Attenuated psychosis syndrome.</li>\n</ol>\n"},"applied-schizophrenia-olanzapine-metabolic-syndrome":{"title":"Applied case — schizophrenia with olanzapine-induced weight gain and relapse after stopping","status":"draft","updated":"2026-10-20","reviewer":[],"covers":["p1-7bcebc12"],"exam":"<p><strong>Case summary.</strong> A 35-year-old man with 8 years of <strong>paranoid schizophrenia</strong> (persecutory and referential delusions, somatic passivity, second-person auditory hallucinations; no mood symptoms) responded well to olanzapine 20 mg/day. He developed increased sleep and appetite and his BMI rose from <strong>24.8 to 32.1 kg/m²</strong> (a gain of about 29%, now in the obese range by both WHO and Asian cut-offs). He stopped the drug because of these effects and relapsed.</p>\n<h3>(a) What is metabolic syndrome? What assessments and investigations would you do in this patient?</h3>\n<p><strong>Definition.</strong> A cluster of central obesity, dyslipidaemia, hypertension and dysglycaemia that predicts type 2 diabetes and cardiovascular disease. Harmonised/IDF criteria (South Asian): waist ≥90 cm (men), plus two or more of: triglycerides ≥150 mg/dL; HDL &lt;40 mg/dL; BP ≥130/85 or on treatment; fasting glucose ≥100 mg/dL or diabetes [1,4].</p>\n<p><strong>Assessment in this patient</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Domain</th>\n<th>Specific points</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>History</td>\n<td>Time course of weight gain and its relation to olanzapine dose; appetite, night eating, sugary drinks; activity level; sleep (snoring, daytime sleepiness — obstructive sleep apnoea); smoking and alcohol; polyuria/polydipsia; sexual dysfunction; family history of diabetes, premature CVD; other drugs (valproate, mirtazapine)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Psychiatric</td>\n<td>Current relapse severity (PANSS), risk, insight, attitude to medication, reasons for stopping (weight was the main one); previous drug trials and responses</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Examination</td>\n<td>Weight, height, BMI, <strong>waist circumference</strong>, BP (both arms), pulse; acanthosis nigricans; fundus; peripheral pulses; thyroid; signs of liver disease</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Investigations</td>\n<td>Fasting glucose and <strong>HbA1c</strong>; fasting lipid profile; LFT (NAFLD; ALT); renal function and eGFR (before metformin); TSH; serum prolactin; ECG (QTc before any switch); urine albumin if diabetic; vitamin B12 (baseline before metformin); consider uric acid; sleep study if OSA is suspected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk score</td>\n<td>10-year CVD risk (QRISK3 or WHO/ISH chart)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>(b) Relationship between metabolic syndrome and psychosis. What lifestyle modifications would you recommend?</h3>\n<p><strong>The relationship is bidirectional and multifactorial:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Illness-related:</strong> possible intrinsic insulin resistance and impaired glucose tolerance in drug-naïve first-episode patients; HPA overactivity (cortisol) from stress; inflammation; genetic overlap.</li>\n<li><strong>Lifestyle-related:</strong> negative symptoms and amotivation → inactivity; poor diet; smoking (60–65%); alcohol; poverty; disrupted sleep [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Drug-related:</strong> H1 and 5-HT2C blockade → hyperphagia; M3 blockade → impaired insulin secretion; direct insulin resistance and dyslipidaemia (olanzapine, clozapine), sometimes before weight gain [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Consequences:</strong> weight gain → non-adherence (as here) → relapse; stigma and low self-esteem; CVD → 15–20-year mortality gap.</li>\n<li>Metabolic syndrome is associated with worse cognition in schizophrenia [2].</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Lifestyle plan for this patient</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Psychoeducation:</strong> explain that weight gain is a side effect that can be managed, so he does not need to stop treatment. Use motivational interviewing.</li>\n<li><strong>Diet</strong> (culturally adapted Indian diet): regular meals, smaller portions of rice/roti, more dal, vegetables and protein; cut sugar, sweets, fried snacks, sugary tea and soft drinks; avoid late-night eating; involve the family member who cooks.</li>\n<li><strong>Physical activity:</strong> brisk walking 30 minutes, 5 days a week (150 minutes/week), building up gradually; yoga; group exercise.</li>\n<li><strong>Sleep:</strong> regular schedule; reduce daytime napping (sedation also reduces as the drug is changed).</li>\n<li><strong>Smoking and alcohol:</strong> cessation support (NRT, varenicline). If he smokes and is later on clozapine/olanzapine, monitor drug levels when he quits [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Structure:</strong> a behavioural lifestyle programme with regular weighing and booster sessions; BAP data show about −3 kg vs control, with benefits fading unless sustained [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Targets:</strong> 5–10% weight loss over 6 months; waist under 90 cm.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>(c) Relationship between antipsychotics and metabolic syndrome. Which antipsychotic would you prefer?</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Liability</th>\n<th>Drugs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Clozapine, olanzapine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Quetiapine, risperidone, paliperidone, iloperidone, asenapine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Aripiprazole, brexpiprazole, cariprazine, lurasidone, ziprasidone, amisulpride, haloperidol</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>None/minimal (non-D2)</td>\n<td>Xanomeline–trospium (≥7% weight gain RR 0.46 vs placebo; lowers LDL; GI effects and raised heart rate) [3]</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Sources: [1,3,5].</p>\n<p><strong>Preferred choice for this patient: aripiprazole</strong> (10–15 mg/day, maximum 30 mg).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Good evidence for weight-neutral effect and improved lipids and glucose after switching from olanzapine [1].</li>\n<li>Effective for positive symptoms; low prolactin; available as a monthly or 2-monthly LAI. This matters because he has relapsed after non-adherence.</li>\n<li><strong>Alternatives:</strong> lurasidone 40–160 mg with food (weight-neutral); amisulpride (low metabolic risk, but raises prolactin); cariprazine.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Plan</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Re-start an antipsychotic now: he is relapsing.</li>\n<li>If he needs rapid control, consider cross-titration; otherwise start aripiprazole directly.</li>\n<li>Once stable, offer <strong>aripiprazole LAI</strong> (400 mg monthly after 14 days of oral overlap, or a 2-day initiation regimen).</li>\n<li><strong>Add metformin</strong> given his BMI gain (&gt;3% in a year and BMI ≥30): 500 mg once → 1 g twice daily over 6 weeks, checking eGFR [4].</li>\n<li>If BMI stays ≥30 despite this, a <strong>GLP-1 agonist</strong> (semaglutide/liraglutide) [4].</li>\n<li>Treat each metabolic component (statin, antihypertensive) according to risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>If aripiprazole fails:</strong> olanzapine was effective, so consider lurasidone or amisulpride. Treatment resistance is not established here, so clozapine is not yet indicated. If olanzapine is truly the only effective drug, restart it at the lowest effective dose with co-prescribed metformin from the start, or adjunctive aripiprazole (about −2 kg) [1]. In countries where it is available, olanzapine–samidorphan is another option.</p>\n<p><strong>Conclusion.</strong> This is a classic, preventable cycle: effective drug → metabolic harm → non-adherence → relapse. The answer is to keep antipsychotic treatment but change to a metabolically benign drug (aripiprazole, ideally as an LAI), add metformin and a structured lifestyle programme, and monitor regularly. Making metabolic health part of the psychiatric treatment plan prevents the next relapse.</p>\n","depth":"<h3>Background</h3>\n<p>The olanzapine dilemma is well known: olanzapine is among the most effective non-clozapine antipsychotics in network meta-analyses (CATIE: longest time to discontinuation) but causes the most weight gain after clozapine. This trade-off drove the development of metformin co-prescription guidance (2025) and combination products (olanzapine–samidorphan) [1,4].</p>\n<h3>Epidemiology</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Olanzapine: mean gain about 4–5 kg at 10 weeks and about 10 kg over 1 year in some studies. Youth gain more (SATIETY: +8.5 kg in 11 weeks).</li>\n<li>About 80% of people on antipsychotics gain weight; low and normal BMI at baseline predicts larger gain [4].</li>\n<li>About 30–35% of patients on long-term antipsychotics meet metabolic syndrome criteria.</li>\n<li>Non-adherence due to weight gain: weight gain is one of the most-cited reasons for stopping; about 50% are non-adherent by 1 year [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Classification and criteria (applied)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Clinical:</strong> ICD-11 6A20 Schizophrenia, multiple episodes, currently symptomatic; symptom specifiers (positive). DSM-5-TR schizophrenia, multiple episodes, currently in acute episode.</li>\n<li><strong>Metabolic:</strong> ICD-11 5A44 Metabolic syndrome; 5B81 Obesity; drug-induced codes for adverse effects.</li>\n<li><strong>Indian BMI categories:</strong> 23–24.9 overweight; ≥25 obese. This patient is obese class II by Asian criteria.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Mechanism in detail</h3>\n<p>Olanzapine has high affinity for H1 (Ki ≈ 0.1–7 nM), 5-HT2C and M1–M5. It causes rapid insulin resistance (euglycaemic clamp studies show this within 1–2 weeks), increases hepatic lipogenesis (SREBP) and raises leptin with leptin resistance. Aripiprazole is a D2/5-HT1A partial agonist with weak H1 affinity, which accounts for its metabolic neutrality. Xanomeline acts through M1/M4 agonism with no D2 or H1 blockade [3].</p>\n<h3>Clinical features in detail — the relapse</h3>\n<p>Relapse after stopping is typical: discontinuation leads to relapse in about 60–80% within a year in multi-episode illness [2]. Abrupt cessation can also cause withdrawal-associated relapse (dopamine supersensitivity) and cholinergic rebound (insomnia, agitation, nausea) [2]. Assess suicide risk, violence and need for admission. Under MHCA 2017, independent admission is preferred; use supported admission (Sec 89) if criteria are met.</p>\n<h3>Assessment in detail</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Adherence and attitude:</strong> Drug Attitude Inventory (DAI-10), Brief Adherence Rating Scale.</li>\n<li><strong>Side-effect burden:</strong> GASS or LUNSERS, to show him that side effects are taken seriously.</li>\n<li><strong>Sexual dysfunction and prolactin</strong> before switching (aripiprazole lowers prolactin).</li>\n<li><strong>Eating behaviour:</strong> screen for binge eating and night eating.</li>\n<li><strong>Physical:</strong> fasting labs as above; ECG before ziprasidone or high doses.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Differential diagnosis</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Of the psychosis:</strong> schizophrenia (most likely); schizoaffective disorder (no mood symptoms reported, so unlikely); delusional disorder (excluded by hallucinations and passivity); substance-induced relapse (check cannabis and alcohol); organic causes if the presentation changes.</li>\n<li><strong>Of the weight gain:</strong> drug effect; hypothyroidism; binge eating; reduced activity; Cushing&#39;s (unlikely). See also the metabolic syndrome answer for the full differential.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Management details</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Switch technique:</strong> with olanzapine stopped and the patient relapsing, start aripiprazole 10 mg and increase to 15–20 mg. Add short-term lorazepam for agitation and sleep. Watch for akathisia (propranolol 10–20 mg TDS if needed).</li>\n<li><strong>LAI:</strong> aripiprazole monohydrate 400 mg monthly (300 mg if poor CYP2D6 metaboliser or on CYP2D6 inhibitors); aripiprazole lauroxil; 2-monthly aripiprazole (960 mg).</li>\n<li><strong>Metformin:</strong> see dose schedule above; stop it if he develops acute illness or dehydration (sick-day rule); check B12 yearly [4].</li>\n<li><strong>GLP-1:</strong> semaglutide 0.25 mg weekly, titrated to 1–2.4 mg. Counsel on nausea.</li>\n<li><strong>Monitoring:</strong> weight monthly for 3 months then 3-monthly; HbA1c and lipids at 3 months then yearly [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Psychosocial:</strong> family psychoeducation; relapse signature plan; CBTp for persistent delusions; vocational support.</li>\n<li><strong>If adherence remains poor despite a good drug:</strong> consider the reasons (insight, stigma), use adherence therapy, and involve the nominated representative.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Evidence table</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Study</th>\n<th>Design</th>\n<th>n</th>\n<th>Finding</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Newcomer et al., 2008</td>\n<td>RCT switch olanzapine → aripiprazole</td>\n<td>173</td>\n<td>Weight −1.8 kg vs +1.4 kg; better lipids</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fleischhacker et al., 2010</td>\n<td>RCT adjunctive aripiprazole to clozapine</td>\n<td>207</td>\n<td>Weight −2.5 kg; LDL improved</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wu et al., 2008</td>\n<td>RCT metformin ± lifestyle</td>\n<td>128</td>\n<td>Metformin + lifestyle −4.7 kg; metformin alone −3.2 kg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stahl et al., 2013</td>\n<td>Open switch olanzapine → lurasidone</td>\n<td>240</td>\n<td>−1.9 kg at 6 months; 29% lost &gt;7% [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Correll et al., 2020 (ENLIGHTEN-2)</td>\n<td>RCT olanzapine–samidorphan vs olanzapine</td>\n<td>561</td>\n<td>Less weight gain (4.2% vs 6.6%)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Larsen et al., 2017</td>\n<td>RCT liraglutide in clozapine/olanzapine-treated</td>\n<td>103</td>\n<td>−5.3 kg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fabiano et al., 2025 [3]</td>\n<td>Meta-analysis xanomeline–trospium</td>\n<td>690</td>\n<td>Weight-neutral; no EPS</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Critical appraisal</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Switching from an effective drug risks relapse. Evidence suggests switching to aripiprazole maintains symptom control in most patients, but some lose response. Monitor closely [1].</li>\n<li>Metformin evidence is moderate, with heterogeneous trials, many from China [1]. The 2025 guideline makes a strong practical recommendation for co-commencement [4].</li>\n<li>GLP-1 agonists are promising but expensive in India; generic liraglutide and semaglutide are becoming available.</li>\n<li>Lifestyle programmes work modestly and fade without boosters [1].</li>\n<li>Patient choice and dignity: he stopped because of weight, a rational concern that should be respected and addressed rather than labelled as &quot;lack of insight&quot;.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Indian context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>High background diabetes prevalence (ICMR-INDIAB ≈11%) and central obesity at lower BMI.</li>\n<li>Aripiprazole and metformin are inexpensive generics in India. Aripiprazole LAI is available but costly; generic versions are increasing.</li>\n<li>Community health workers and NCD clinics (Ayushman Arogya Mandirs) can support BP and glucose monitoring.</li>\n<li>Family involvement in diet (the family member who cooks) is key.</li>\n<li>Rights under MHCA 2017: informed consent for the switch and the LAI; advance directive if he wishes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Recent advances</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>2025 metformin guideline: co-commence metformin with high-risk antipsychotics [4].</li>\n<li>Xanomeline–trospium (approved in the US 2024; check Indian status) offers a weight-neutral option [3].</li>\n<li>Olanzapine–samidorphan.</li>\n<li>GLP-1 receptor agonists and tirzepatide (GIP/GLP-1) in severe mental illness: trials ongoing.</li>\n</ul>\n","refs":"<ol>\n<li>Cooper SJ, Reynolds GP, Barnes TRE, et al. BAP guidelines on the management of weight gain, metabolic disturbances and cardiovascular risk associated with psychosis and antipsychotic drug treatment. <em>J Psychopharmacol</em> 2016; 30(8):717–748.</li>\n<li>Taylor DM, Barnes TRE, Young AH. <em>The Maudsley Prescribing Guidelines in Psychiatry</em>. 15th ed. Wiley, 2025; Chapter 1 (Antipsychotic prophylaxis; Stopping antipsychotics; Switching antipsychotics; Treatment of antipsychotic-induced weight gain); McCutcheon RA, et al. <em>Mol Psychiatry</em> 2023; 28:1902–1918 (metabolic syndrome and cognition).</li>\n<li>Fabiano N, Wong S, Zhou C, et al. Efficacy, tolerability, and safety of xanomeline-trospium chloride for schizophrenia: a systematic review and meta-analysis. <em>Eur Neuropsychopharmacol</em> 2025; 92:62–73.</li>\n<li>Guideline on metformin for prevention of antipsychotic-induced weight gain. <em>Schizophr Bull</em> 2025; 51(5):1201 (guideline algorithm).</li>\n<li>Pillinger T, McCutcheon RA, Vano L, et al. Comparative effects of 18 antipsychotics on metabolic function in patients with schizophrenia. <em>Lancet Psychiatry</em> 2020; 7:64–77.</li>\n<li>Alberti KG, et al. Harmonizing the metabolic syndrome. <em>Circulation</em> 2009; 120:1640–1645.</li>\n</ol>\n"},"applied-schizophrenia-woman-galactorrhoea-refusing-admission":{"title":"Applied case — young obese woman with chronic psychosis, suicidality, refusing admission, past galactorrhoea","status":"draft","updated":"2026-10-20","reviewer":[],"covers":["p1-ef058f3c"],"exam":"<p><strong>Case summary.</strong> A 27-year-old obese woman has 6 years of suspiciousness and self-absorbed behaviour. She now presents with severe agitation, suspiciousness, hallucinations and <strong>intense suicidal ideas</strong>. She refuses admission and medication because of past <strong>galactorrhoea</strong>, which was most likely antipsychotic-induced hyperprolactinaemia. The likely diagnosis is <strong>schizophrenia</strong> (paranoid, multiple episodes or continuous), with high risk, impaired capacity, a valid adverse-effect concern, and sex-specific issues (obesity, prolactin, reproductive health).</p>\n<h3>(a) What additional information is needed to make the diagnosis?</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Area</th>\n<th>Information required</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Onset and course</td>\n<td>Insidious or acute; continuous or episodic; interepisode function; deterioration in work/self-care</td>\n<td>Schizophrenia vs ATPD vs mood disorder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Phenomenology</td>\n<td>Nature of voices (third-person, running commentary), delusions (persecutory, reference, control), thought disorder, negative symptoms (&quot;self-absorbed&quot; = autism/withdrawal)</td>\n<td>ICD-11/DSM criteria</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mood symptoms</td>\n<td>Depressive or manic episodes, their timing relative to psychosis; post-psychotic depression</td>\n<td>Schizoaffective, bipolar or psychotic depression; suicide risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Suicide</td>\n<td>Intent, plan, means, past attempts, command hallucinations, hopelessness, access to pesticides or drugs</td>\n<td>Risk management</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Menstrual and reproductive history</td>\n<td>Menstrual relation of symptoms (perimenstrual worsening), menarche, pregnancy and <strong>postpartum</strong> episodes, contraception, current pregnancy possibility, lactation</td>\n<td>Women&#39;s psychosis course; drug choice [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Galactorrhoea details</td>\n<td>Which drug, dose, duration; amenorrhoea, sexual dysfunction; whether it resolved on stopping</td>\n<td>Prolactin-elevating drug (risperidone, amisulpride, FGA) [2]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Treatment history</td>\n<td>Drugs, doses, duration, adherence, response, side effects (weight gain with olanzapine?), LAI use</td>\n<td>Treatment resistance; choice of drug</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Substance use</td>\n<td>Cannabis, alcohol, stimulants</td>\n<td>Substance-induced psychosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Medical</td>\n<td>Thyroid, epilepsy, head injury, autoimmune symptoms; <strong>causes of obesity and hyperprolactinaemia</strong> (PCOS, hypothyroidism, pituitary tumour — headache, visual field defects)</td>\n<td>Organic exclusion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Family history</td>\n<td>Psychosis, mood disorder, suicide</td>\n<td>Genetic risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Premorbid personality and social context</td>\n<td>Schizoid/schizotypal traits; marital status, stressors, abuse, support</td>\n<td>Prognosis; safeguarding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Insight and capacity</td>\n<td>Understanding of illness and treatment; ability to weigh information</td>\n<td>MHCA 2017 decisions</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Investigations:</strong> CBC, glucose/HbA1c, lipids, LFT/RFT, TSH, <strong>serum prolactin</strong> (morning, minimal stress), <strong>pregnancy test (β-hCG)</strong>, urine drug screen, ECG; MRI pituitary if prolactin is very high (&gt;3,000 mIU/L) without a causative drug, or if there are visual symptoms [2].</p>\n<h3>(b) Acute and long-term management</h3>\n<p><strong>Acute phase</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Safety first:</strong> risk of suicide and agitation. Do not discharge an acutely suicidal, psychotic patient without a safety plan. Observe and remove means.</li>\n<li><strong>Legal (MHCA 2017):</strong><ul>\n<li>If she lacks capacity and has severe illness with risk of self-harm, use <strong>supported admission</strong> (Section 89, up to 30 days; nominated representative involved).</li>\n<li><strong>Emergency treatment</strong> (Section 94, up to 72 hours) for imminent risk.</li>\n<li>Respect any <strong>advance directive</strong> where valid. Document capacity assessment. She has the right to information and to be heard about galactorrhoea.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Engagement:</strong> acknowledge her galactorrhoea experience as real and offer a <strong>prolactin-sparing drug</strong>. This turns refusal into negotiation.</li>\n<li><strong>Rapid tranquillisation if needed:</strong> oral lorazepam 1–2 mg, or oral aripiprazole/quetiapine. IM: lorazepam ± promethazine. Avoid IM haloperidol if possible (raises prolactin, EPS) but use it if necessary for safety [2].</li>\n<li><strong>Antipsychotic choice:</strong> <strong>aripiprazole</strong> (10–30 mg) is prolactin-sparing, weight-neutral and available as an LAI. Alternatives: quetiapine (prolactin-sparing but weight gain), cariprazine, brexpiprazole. <strong>Avoid</strong> risperidone, paliperidone, amisulpride and FGAs (high prolactin) and olanzapine/clozapine first (obesity) [2,3].</li>\n<li><strong>Suicidality:</strong> close observation. ECT is an option if suicide risk is extreme or catatonia develops. Treat comorbid depression. Clozapine reduces suicidality in schizophrenia (InterSePT) if resistance is established.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Long-term</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Maintenance antipsychotic at the lowest effective dose; consider <strong>aripiprazole LAI</strong> if adherence is poor.</li>\n<li>Prolactin monitoring: baseline, at 3 months, then yearly; ask about menstrual and sexual symptoms [2].</li>\n<li>Metabolic monitoring and management: weight, waist, HbA1c, lipids; lifestyle programme; <strong>metformin</strong> if weight rises or BMI ≥30 [3].</li>\n<li>Bone health: calcium/vitamin D, weight-bearing exercise; DEXA if there was long-standing hyperprolactinaemia [2].</li>\n<li>Reproductive health: contraception counselling (unplanned pregnancy rates 24–48% in women with schizophrenia); pre-conception planning; folate [1].</li>\n<li>Psychosocial: CBTp, family psychoeducation, vocational rehabilitation, suicide prevention plan, crisis contacts (Tele-MANAS 14416).</li>\n<li>Hormonal course: watch for perimenstrual, postpartum and perimenopausal worsening. Consider adjunctive <strong>raloxifene</strong> (60–120 mg) or oestradiol in women with persisting symptoms, especially peri- or post-menopausal (investigational/off-label) [1].</li>\n<li>If two adequate trials fail → <strong>clozapine</strong> (with metformin co-commenced).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>(c) Long-term course and outcome</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Schizophrenia in women:</strong> later onset (about 5 years later than men; second peak after 40), more affective symptoms, fewer negative symptoms, better social and occupational functioning, and better treatment response before menopause. Worsening occurs perimenstrually, postpartum and after menopause [1].</li>\n<li><strong>General course:</strong> heterogeneous. Madras 20-year data showed only about 8% continuously ill and 8% fully recovered; most had relapses with full or partial remission; no sex difference in course; GAF &gt;60 in about 75% [4].</li>\n<li><strong>Poor prognostic factors here:</strong> 6-year duration with insidious onset (suspiciousness, self-absorption → long DUP), current suicidality, poor adherence due to side effects, obesity and metabolic risk, possible poor insight.</li>\n<li><strong>Good prognostic factors:</strong> female sex, a specific and correctable reason for refusal, response to past treatment (if any).</li>\n<li><strong>Mortality:</strong> suicide (about 5% lifetime) and cardiovascular disease (obesity) are the main threats. In Madras, 7 of 16 deaths were suicides in patients under 35 [4].</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conclusion.</strong> Manage the immediate suicide and agitation risk using MHCA 2017 provisions where necessary. Engage her by validating the galactorrhoea complaint and choosing a prolactin-sparing, weight-neutral drug such as aripiprazole, preferably later as an LAI. Then build long-term care around metabolic, bone and reproductive health and the hormonal life course specific to women.</p>\n","depth":"<h3>Background</h3>\n<p>Sex differences in schizophrenia were noted by Kraepelin. Seeman (1980s) and Häfner developed the <strong>oestrogen protection hypothesis</strong>: later onset in women and a second peak at menopause suggest oestrogen buffers vulnerability. Kulkarni&#39;s group (from 1996) tested adjunctive oestradiol and SERMs [1].</p>\n<h3>Epidemiology</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Male:female incidence ≈1.4:1. Peak onset 20–29 in men; women about 5 years later (20–39), with higher incidence than men after 40 [1].</li>\n<li>Women attempt suicide more often; men complete suicide more often [1].</li>\n<li>20–32% of women report perimenstrual exacerbation; admissions rise in the perimenstrual phase [1].</li>\n<li>More women than men need admission after age 45, coinciding with perimenopause (Sommer 2023) [1].</li>\n<li>Hyperprolactinaemia in 30–70% of patients on antipsychotics [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Classification and criteria</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>ICD-11 6A20 Schizophrenia:</strong> at least 1 month of two or more core symptoms (persistent delusions, persistent hallucinations, disorganised thinking, experiences of influence/passivity, negative symptoms, grossly disorganised behaviour, psychomotor disturbance), at least one from the first four. Specifiers: episode course; symptom domains including positive, negative, depressive, cognitive.</li>\n<li><strong>DSM-5-TR:</strong> 6-month duration including at least 1 month of active symptoms; functional decline.</li>\n<li><strong>Hyperprolactinaemia:</strong> above the laboratory upper limit (typically &gt;25 ng/mL in women); symptomatic vs asymptomatic.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Mechanism in detail</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Oestrogen and psychosis:</strong> oestradiol modulates D2 sensitivity, serotonergic and glutamatergic systems, and hippocampal plasticity. It is neuroprotective (anti-excitotoxic, antioxidant, mitochondrial). Falling levels (perimenstrual, postpartum, menopause) lead to symptom worsening. Low oestrogen also reduces CYP activity, changing drug levels [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Antipsychotic hyperprolactinaemia:</strong> D2 blockade in the tuberoinfundibular pathway removes dopamine&#39;s inhibition of lactotrophs → raised prolactin → GnRH suppression → low oestrogen (amenorrhoea, infertility, bone loss) and galactorrhoea. <strong>Low oestrogen may itself worsen psychosis</strong>, creating a vicious cycle [1,2].</li>\n<li><strong>Stress hyperprolactinaemia:</strong> drug-naïve patients may have raised prolactin from stress; this may be stronger in women [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Clinical features in detail</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Women: more affective symptoms, comorbid depression and anxiety, better verbal memory [1].</li>\n<li>Hyperprolactinaemia features: galactorrhoea, oligo/amenorrhoea, breast enlargement, sexual dysfunction, reduced libido, infertility, possibly delusions of pregnancy; long-term osteoporosis and possibly breast cancer [2].</li>\n<li>Obesity in schizophrenia: from illness, lifestyle and drugs. Women with PCOS may also have raised prolactin.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Assessment in detail</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Prolactin interpretation:</strong> take blood in the morning with minimal stress. Exclude pregnancy, hypothyroidism, renal failure, seizures and prolactinoma. A raised level on a prolactin-sparing drug suggests another cause [2].</li>\n<li><strong>Capacity assessment (MHCA Sec 4):</strong> can she understand information relevant to the decision, appreciate foreseeable consequences, and communicate a decision?</li>\n<li><strong>Suicide risk:</strong> structured assessment (C-SSRS) plus clinical formulation.</li>\n<li><strong>Bone:</strong> DEXA if amenorrhoea lasted more than 6–12 months [2].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Differential diagnosis</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Condition</th>\n<th>Points for/against</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Paranoid schizophrenia</td>\n<td>Long duration, suspiciousness, hallucinations, withdrawal (most likely)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Schizoaffective disorder</td>\n<td>Requires concurrent prominent mood episodes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Psychotic depression</td>\n<td>Suicidal ideas prominent; check pervasive low mood</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bipolar mixed state</td>\n<td>Agitation + suicidality; past mania?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Delusional disorder</td>\n<td>Hallucinations not prominent; better functioning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Organic (autoimmune, thyroid, epilepsy, prolactinoma with psychosis)</td>\n<td>Atypical features, neurological signs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Substance-induced</td>\n<td>Temporal relation, toxicology</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Schizotypal disorder</td>\n<td>Long-term oddness without frank psychosis (not the case now)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Management details</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Strategy for hyperprolactinaemia</th>\n<th>Detail</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Switch</td>\n<td>To aripiprazole, brexpiprazole, cariprazine, quetiapine, clozapine (prolactin-sparing) [2]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Adjunct (first choice)</td>\n<td>Aripiprazole 5 mg/day (3–6 mg effective) [2]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Second choice</td>\n<td>Vitamin B6 600 mg/day [2]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Third choice</td>\n<td>Cabergoline, bromocriptine, amantadine (may worsen psychosis), metformin 2.5–3 g/day [2]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Avoid high-prolactin drugs</td>\n<td>Under 25 years, young women, osteoporosis, hormone-dependent breast cancer [2]</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Oestrogen strategies (investigational/adjunctive):</strong> transdermal oestradiol 100 µg (Kulkarni trials); raloxifene 60–120 mg/day (meta-analysis positive for PANSS total, positive and negative symptoms; Brand 2023 suggests benefit for negative symptoms only in women); bazedoxifene under trial [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Contraception:</strong> prefer LARC (IUD, implant, depot) over daily pills when adherence is uncertain; older ethinylestradiol pills may worsen mood [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Pregnancy plans:</strong> aripiprazole and quetiapine have reasonable safety data; avoid valproate; plan postpartum relapse prevention.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Evidence table</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Study</th>\n<th>Design</th>\n<th>n</th>\n<th>Finding</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Kulkarni et al., 2015 [1]</td>\n<td>RCT transdermal oestradiol 200 µg</td>\n<td>183</td>\n<td>Reduced PANSS vs placebo</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weiser et al., 2019 [1]</td>\n<td>RCT oestradiol in women of childbearing age</td>\n<td>200</td>\n<td>Benefit, especially for negative symptoms and age &gt;38</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>de Boer et al., 2018 [1]</td>\n<td>Meta-analysis raloxifene</td>\n<td>8 RCTs</td>\n<td>Improved total, positive, negative symptoms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Brand et al., 2023 [1]</td>\n<td>RCT raloxifene</td>\n<td>102</td>\n<td>No overall benefit; negative symptoms improved in women</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Meng et al., 2015 / 2022 NMA [2]</td>\n<td>Adjunctive aripiprazole for hyperprolactinaemia</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Most effective adjunct</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Meltzer et al., 2003 (InterSePT)</td>\n<td>RCT clozapine vs olanzapine</td>\n<td>980</td>\n<td>Clozapine reduced suicidal behaviour</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thara, 2004 (Madras) [4]</td>\n<td>20-year cohort</td>\n<td>90</td>\n<td>No sex difference in course</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Critical appraisal</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Coercion vs autonomy:</strong> her refusal has a rational basis. MHCA 2017 favours least-restrictive options; coercion should be the last resort and time-limited, with reasons recorded.</li>\n<li><strong>Sex differences:</strong> some are robust (age of onset); others vary across cultures. The Madras cohort found no sex difference in course [4].</li>\n<li><strong>Oestrogen therapy:</strong> promising, but risks (thrombosis, breast and endometrial effects) and heterogeneous response limit routine use. Raloxifene trials are mixed [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Prolactin:</strong> many patients have asymptomatic hyperprolactinaemia. Treat according to symptoms and long-term risk, not the number alone [2].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Indian context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>MHCA 2017:</strong><ul>\n<li>Supported admission (Sec 89/90).</li>\n<li>Emergency treatment (Sec 94).</li>\n<li>Nominated representative (Sec 14).</li>\n<li>Advance directive (Sec 5).</li>\n<li>Decriminalisation of attempted suicide (Sec 115), which places a duty on government to care for people who attempt suicide.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>National Suicide Prevention Strategy 2022;</strong> Tele-MANAS (14416).</li>\n<li>Social context: stigma around marriage for women with schizophrenia; illness often concealed at marriage (Madras) [4]; caregiver burden falls on parents. Safeguarding against domestic violence and abandonment matters.</li>\n<li>Pesticide access is the major means of suicide in rural India; counsel the family on restricting means.</li>\n<li>Aripiprazole is affordable as a generic; aripiprazole LAI is costly.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Recent advances</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Prolactin-sparing, weight-neutral options are expanding: cariprazine, brexpiprazole, lumateperone, and xanomeline–trospium (prolactin-sparing; US-approved) [2].</li>\n<li><strong>SERMs:</strong> bazedoxifene trials in women with schizophrenia [1].</li>\n<li>Women&#39;s mental health services and perinatal psychiatry units are expanding in India (NIMHANS perinatal service).</li>\n</ul>\n","refs":"<ol>\n<li>Mu E, Gurvich C, Kulkarni J. Estrogen and psychosis — a review and future directions. <em>Arch Womens Ment Health</em> 2024; 27:877–885.</li>\n<li>Taylor DM, Barnes TRE, Young AH. <em>The Maudsley Prescribing Guidelines in Psychiatry</em>. 15th ed. Wiley, 2025; Chapter 1 (Hyperprolactinaemia, pp. 190–194; Acutely disturbed or violent behaviour; Antipsychotic depots).</li>\n<li>Cooper SJ, et al. BAP guidelines on the management of weight gain, metabolic disturbances and cardiovascular risk associated with psychosis and antipsychotic drug treatment. <em>J Psychopharmacol</em> 2016; 30(8):717–748; and metformin guideline, <em>Schizophr Bull</em> 2025; 51(5):1201.</li>\n<li>Thara R. Twenty-year course of schizophrenia: the Madras Longitudinal Study. <em>Can J Psychiatry</em> 2004; 49(8):564–569.</li>\n<li>Government of India. <em>The Mental Healthcare Act, 2017</em> (Sections 4, 5, 14, 89, 94, 115).</li>\n<li>Meltzer HY, Alphs L, Green AI, et al. Clozapine treatment for suicidality in schizophrenia: International Suicide Prevention Trial (InterSePT). <em>Arch Gen Psychiatry</em> 2003; 60:82–91.</li>\n</ol>\n"},"cognitive-impairment-in-schizophrenia":{"title":"Cognitive (neuropsychological) impairment in schizophrenia","status":"draft","updated":"2026-10-20","reviewer":[],"covers":["p1-2a7319ea","p1-90793542"],"exam":"<p><strong>Definition.</strong> Cognitive impairment is a <strong>core, trait-like feature</strong> of schizophrenia, separate from positive and negative symptoms. It is a deficit in neurocognition (attention, memory, processing speed, executive function) and social cognition. On average, patients perform about <strong>1 SD below</strong> controls on individual domains and <strong>1.5–2 SD below</strong> on composite scores. It is present before psychosis begins, largely stable afterwards, and is the strongest predictor of functional outcome. Antipsychotics barely improve it [1,2].</p>\n<h3>Mechanism</h3>\n<p>Genetic liability (strong negative genetic correlation between schizophrenia risk and cognitive ability) + environment (obstetric complications, childhood trauma, deprivation, cannabis) → neurodevelopmental disruption → <strong>cortical excitation/inhibition (E/I) imbalance</strong>: parvalbumin GABA interneuron deficit (low GAD67), NMDA hypofunction, <strong>cortical hypodopaminergia</strong> (D1), and reduced muscarinic M1/M4 signalling → disrupted gamma oscillations and network function → impaired working memory, learning and executive function [1].</p>\n<h3>Profile of deficits (MATRICS domains)</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Domain</th>\n<th>Typical deficit</th>\n<th>Example test</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Processing speed</td>\n<td>Large (partly drug-related)</td>\n<td>BACS symbol coding, Trail Making A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Attention/vigilance</td>\n<td>Moderate–large</td>\n<td>Continuous Performance Test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Working memory</td>\n<td>Large</td>\n<td>Letter–number span</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Verbal learning and memory</td>\n<td><strong>Largest; best distinguishes patients</strong> (Bilder 2000)</td>\n<td>Hopkins Verbal Learning Test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Visual learning and memory</td>\n<td>Moderate</td>\n<td>Brief Visuospatial Memory Test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reasoning/problem-solving (executive)</td>\n<td>Moderate–large</td>\n<td>NAB Mazes, WCST</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Social cognition</td>\n<td>Moderate–large (theory of mind, emotion recognition, attribution bias)</td>\n<td>MSCEIT</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The first-episode study by <strong>Bilder 2000</strong> (n=94) found a generalised deficit of <strong>1.53 SD</strong>, with additional relative deficits in memory and executive function. Executive and attention deficits were most linked to poor functional outcome [3].</p>\n<h3>Course</h3>\n<p>Present in childhood (global deficits, delayed milestones) → widening in adolescence (slower development) → present in the clinical high-risk state (worst in those who convert) → at first episode, ~1.5 SD → <strong>stable</strong> thereafter (Heaton 2001, n=142, mean follow-up 3 years and up to 10) → earlier age-related decline and higher dementia risk after 65, linked to smoking, obesity, diabetes and inactivity [1,3].</p>\n<h3>Assessment</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>MATRICS Consensus Cognitive Battery (MCCB)</strong> (7 domains); BACS (brief, ~35 minutes); MoCA as a screen.</li>\n<li>Social cognition battery; functional capacity (UCSD Performance-based Skills Assessment, UPSA).</li>\n<li>Exclude confounders: anticholinergics, benzodiazepines, sedation, depression, substance use, metabolic disease.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Management</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Remove harmful contributors: reduce anticholinergic drugs (improves cognition) and benzodiazepines; use the lowest effective antipsychotic dose; avoid polypharmacy [1].</li>\n<li>Treat depression, sleep problems and physical illness; stop smoking; exercise (aerobic exercise improves cognition).</li>\n<li><strong>Cognitive remediation therapy (CRT)</strong>, combined with vocational rehabilitation: moderate effect on cognition and small–moderate effect on function [1].</li>\n<li>Social cognition training (SCIT); IPS supported employment with cognitive adaptations.</li>\n<li>No licensed drug. Investigational options: xanomeline–trospium (M1/M4), TAAR1 agonists, cannabidiol, memantine. Cholinesterase inhibitors and glutamate agents have failed [1].</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Key evidence</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Heinrichs &amp; Zakzanis 1998:</strong> meta-analysis; large effect sizes across domains.</li>\n<li><strong>Bilder 2000:</strong> first-episode generalised deficit of 1.5 SD; memory most discriminating [3].</li>\n<li><strong>Heaton 2001:</strong> deficits stable over 1.6 and 5 years [3].</li>\n<li><strong>Green 1996/2000:</strong> cognition predicts functional outcome more than positive symptoms do.</li>\n<li><strong>Wykes 2011; Vita 2021:</strong> CRT meta-analyses; effect on cognition d ≈ 0.45, on function d ≈ 0.4.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conclusion.</strong> Cognitive impairment is a central, enduring and disabling dimension of schizophrenia. It is neurodevelopmental in origin and largely untouched by D2-blocking drugs. Routine cognitive assessment and remediation should be part of standard care. New drugs that act on muscarinic and E/I mechanisms are the most promising pharmacological avenue.</p>\n","depth":"<h3>Background</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Kraepelin (1919)</strong> named the illness <em>dementia praecox</em>, placing cognitive decline at its centre. <strong>Bleuler</strong> emphasised loosening of associations and attention.</li>\n<li>From the 1980s, neuropsychology (Saykin 1991, Heinrichs 1998) showed deficits across domains.</li>\n<li>Green (1996) linked cognition to function.</li>\n<li>The <strong>NIMH MATRICS initiative (2003–2008)</strong> produced the MCCB and a regulatory path for cognitive drugs. TURNS (Treatment Units for Research on Neurocognition in Schizophrenia) tested candidate drugs.</li>\n<li>DSM-5 considered, but did not add, cognitive impairment as a diagnostic criterion because it is not specific enough (it also occurs in bipolar disorder) [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Epidemiology</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>About 80% of patients perform below their expected premorbid level. About 25–30% have scores in the &quot;normal&quot; range but below what would be predicted for them (&quot;neuropsychologically normal&quot; subgroup).</li>\n<li>Unaffected first-degree relatives show milder deficits (an endophenotype).</li>\n<li>80–90% of people with schizophrenia are unemployed for most of adult life; cognitive and negative symptoms explain much of this [1].</li>\n<li>About 65% smoke. Nicotine briefly improves sensory gating and attention, but long-term smoking worsens cognition and late-life decline [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Classification and criteria</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>ICD-11 includes a <strong>&quot;cognitive symptoms&quot; specifier</strong> among the symptom specifiers for primary psychotic disorders (6A25.5), rated as none, mild, moderate or severe.</li>\n<li>DSM-5-TR uses the Clinician-Rated Dimensions of Psychosis Symptom Severity, which includes impaired cognition (Section III).</li>\n<li><strong>Neurocognition</strong> (attention, memory, speed, executive) vs <strong>social cognition</strong> (emotion processing, theory of mind, social perception, attributional style). Social cognition is separable and has its own effect on function [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Relation to PANSS factors:</strong> cognition is distinct from positive and negative factors and only modestly related to the disorganisation factor [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Mechanism in detail</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>System</th>\n<th>Abnormality</th>\n<th>Cognitive effect</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Dopamine</td>\n<td>Striatal hyperdopaminergia (positive symptoms); <strong>cortical hypodopaminergia</strong> (D1) [1]</td>\n<td>Working memory, executive dysfunction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Glutamate</td>\n<td>NMDA hypofunction on interneurons (ketamine model; anti-NMDAR encephalitis) [1]</td>\n<td>Disinhibition, impaired plasticity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GABA</td>\n<td>Low GAD65/67, fewer parvalbumin interneurons [1]</td>\n<td>Abnormal gamma oscillations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Acetylcholine</td>\n<td>Reduced M1/M4 receptors (post-mortem, PET); anticholinergic drugs worsen cognition [1]</td>\n<td>Attention, memory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Synaptic</td>\n<td>Reduced dendritic spines and synaptic density (SV2A PET) [1]</td>\n<td>Pyramidal hypofunction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Networks</td>\n<td>Aberrant resting gamma power, reduced task-induced gamma; reduced MMN; altered fMRI networks [1]</td>\n<td>Working memory precision</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>There is an open question about whether interneuron loss is primary (so boost inhibition) or compensatory for weak pyramidal activity (so boosting inhibition would harm). The answer will shape drug development [1].</p>\n<p><strong>Genetics:</strong> polygenic risk for schizophrenia correlates negatively with cognitive ability; bipolar-specific alleles do not (they may even be linked to better cognition). Within patients, cognition tracks polygenic scores for IQ and educational attainment rather than schizophrenia polygenic score [1].</p>\n<h3>Clinical features in detail</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Subjective vs objective deficits:</strong> weakly correlated. Poor insight and disorganisation reduce self-awareness of deficits. Subjective complaints are more common in people at clinical high risk and in those with depression [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Heterogeneity:</strong> variability between and within individuals is greater than in controls. Intellectually &quot;preserved&quot; and &quot;deteriorated&quot; subgroups exist [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Premorbid vs current IQ:</strong> patients matched on current IQ score higher on premorbid-sensitive tests (e.g., reading) but lower on working memory, which suggests decline from their premorbid level [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Functional consequences:</strong> cognition correlates with UPSA functional capacity (r = 0.4–0.8) more than with interview-based measures (r = 0.1–0.3). It also predicts adherence, admission and length of stay [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Assessment in detail</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tool</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>MCCB</td>\n<td>Gold standard; 10 tests, 7 domains, ~65 minutes; Indian norms are limited</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>BACS</td>\n<td>~35 minutes; sensitive; Hindi and Kannada versions available</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NIMHANS Neuropsychology Battery</td>\n<td>Indian norms; widely used in Indian practice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Schizophrenia Cognition Rating Scale (SCoRS)</td>\n<td>Interview-based, informant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MoCA / ACE-III</td>\n<td>Screening, especially in older patients</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>UPSA</td>\n<td>Functional capacity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Social cognition: TASIT, Hinting Task, Faux Pas, facial emotion recognition (e.g., TRENDS, Indian)</td>\n<td>Social cognition</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Medical work-up when decline is atypical: thyroid, B12, HIV/VDRL, MRI (vascular, other), sleep apnoea and metabolic syndrome.</p>\n<h3>Differential diagnosis</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Cause</th>\n<th>Clue</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Primary schizophrenia cognitive deficit</td>\n<td>Premorbid onset, stable, generalised</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Drug effect</td>\n<td>Anticholinergic load, benzodiazepines, high-dose antipsychotics; improves on reduction [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Depression (post-psychotic)</td>\n<td>Subjective complaints, slowness, low mood</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Negative symptoms</td>\n<td>Apathy affects effort on tests; effort testing helps</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Substance use</td>\n<td>Cannabis, alcohol, inhalants</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Comorbid neurological disease or dementia</td>\n<td>Progressive late decline, focal signs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metabolic/vascular</td>\n<td>Diabetes, hypertension, metabolic syndrome worsen cognition [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Intellectual disability with psychosis</td>\n<td>Lifelong low IQ, adaptive deficits from childhood</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Management details</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Antipsychotic choice:</strong> all antipsychotics have similarly small cognitive effects. Early claims for &quot;atypicals&quot; were trial artefacts. Cariprazine showed some advantage on PANSS cognitive items [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Anticholinergic burden:</strong> calculate (Anticholinergic Cognitive Burden scale); taper trihexyphenidyl where possible [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Cognitive remediation:</strong> computer-assisted or therapist-led (e.g., NEAR, CIRCuiTS); 20–40 sessions; best combined with psychosocial rehabilitation and active strategy coaching.</li>\n<li><strong>Exercise:</strong> aerobic exercise 2–3 times a week improves global cognition (Firth 2017 meta-analysis).</li>\n<li><strong>Physical health:</strong> metformin and weight control, diabetes and BP control, smoking cessation (which improves cognition) [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Sleep:</strong> treat insomnia and sleep apnoea [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Older adults:</strong> screen for dementia; avoid anticholinergics.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Evidence table</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Study</th>\n<th>Design</th>\n<th>n</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Heinrichs &amp; Zakzanis, 1998</td>\n<td>Meta-analysis</td>\n<td>204 studies</td>\n<td>Effect sizes ~0.6–1.4 across domains</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bilder et al., 2000 [3]</td>\n<td>First-episode cross-sectional</td>\n<td>94 vs 36</td>\n<td>Generalised deficit 1.53 SD; memory and executive relative deficits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Heaton et al., 2001 [3]</td>\n<td>Longitudinal</td>\n<td>142 vs 206</td>\n<td>Stable over up to 10 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reichenberg et al., 2010 (Dunedin)</td>\n<td>Birth cohort</td>\n<td>1,037</td>\n<td>Childhood deficits; widening developmental lag age 7–13</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wykes et al., 2011</td>\n<td>CRT meta-analysis</td>\n<td>40 RCTs</td>\n<td>Cognition d 0.45; function d 0.42</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Brannan et al., 2021 (EMERGENT-1)</td>\n<td>RCT xanomeline–trospium</td>\n<td>182</td>\n<td>Positive symptom effect; cognitive post hoc benefit in impaired patients</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>McCutcheon et al., 2023 [1]</td>\n<td>Expert review</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>E/I balance model; treatment options</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Critical appraisal</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Practice effects</strong> inflate apparent improvement in longitudinal and CRT studies [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Global vs specific:</strong> the deficit may be mostly generalised; current tests may be too coarse to isolate specific processes [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Processing speed</strong> is partly a drug effect. In drug-naïve samples it is no worse than verbal or working memory deficits [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Failed drug development:</strong> cholinesterase inhibitors, nicotinic agonists (varenicline), mGlu2/3 agonists, glycine agents and memantine have not worked in large trials [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Measurement:</strong> cognition is rarely assessed in routine care because of time and training constraints [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Specificity:</strong> cognitive deficits are more severe and earlier in schizophrenia than in bipolar disorder or depression, but not unique [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Indian context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>NIMHANS and PGIMER have contributed neuropsychological norms and studies; the <strong>NIMHANS Neuropsychology Battery</strong> is the Indian standard.</li>\n<li>Cognitive remediation adapted for Indian settings has been trialled (NIMHANS); group and family-assisted formats suit limited resources.</li>\n<li>Disability certification under the <strong>RPwD Act 2016</strong> uses IDEAS. Cognitive disability can support benefit claims.</li>\n<li>Low education and literacy affect test norms; use culturally adapted tests.</li>\n<li>Better employment outcomes in Indian cohorts (Madras: 76% of men working at 20 years) show that informal jobs can accommodate cognitive limits [4].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Recent advances</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Established:</strong> cognitive remediation as a recommended intervention (EPA 2021 guidance).</li>\n<li><strong>Emerging:</strong><ul>\n<li><strong>Xanomeline–trospium</strong> (approved for schizophrenia, 2024), with cognitive signals in post hoc analyses.</li>\n<li><strong>Iclepertin (BI 425809)</strong>, a GlyT1 inhibitor. <em>Check current status</em>: the phase 3 CONNEX programme reported negative results in 2025.</li>\n<li>TAAR1 agonists (ulotaront, ralmitaront), whose development is uncertain after phase 3 failures.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Investigational:</strong> cannabidiol, M1-selective positive allosteric modulators, rTMS (minimal effect) [1,5], digital cognitive training, and E/I biomarker-guided treatment.</li>\n</ul>\n","refs":"<ol>\n<li>McCutcheon RA, Keefe RSE, McGuire PK. Cognitive impairment in schizophrenia: aetiology, pathophysiology, and treatment. <em>Mol Psychiatry</em> 2023; 28:1902–1918.</li>\n<li>Gupta S, Kulhara P. What is schizophrenia: a neurodevelopmental or neurodegenerative disorder or a combination of both? <em>Indian J Psychiatry</em> 2010; 52(1):21–27.</li>\n<li>Bilder RM, Goldman RS, Robinson D, et al. Neuropsychology of first-episode schizophrenia: initial characterization and clinical correlates. <em>Am J Psychiatry</em> 2000; 157:549–559; Heaton RK, Gladsjo JA, Palmer BW, et al. Stability and course of neuropsychological deficits in schizophrenia. <em>Arch Gen Psychiatry</em> 2001; 58:24–32 (user&#39;s notes).</li>\n<li>Thara R. Twenty-year course of schizophrenia: the Madras Longitudinal Study. <em>Can J Psychiatry</em> 2004; 49(8):564–569.</li>\n<li>Tikka SK, et al. Clinical practice guidelines for the therapeutic use of rTMS in neuropsychiatric disorders. <em>Indian J Psychiatry</em> 2023; 65(2):270–288.</li>\n<li>Wykes T, Huddy V, Cellard C, et al. A meta-analysis of cognitive remediation for schizophrenia: methodology and effect sizes. <em>Am J Psychiatry</em> 2011; 168:472–485.</li>\n<li>Green MF, Kern RS, Braff DL, Mintz J. Neurocognitive deficits and functional outcome in schizophrenia. <em>Schizophr Bull</em> 2000; 26:119–136.</li>\n</ol>\n"},"early-intervention-in-psychosis":{"title":"Early intervention in psychosis — concept, evidence and the Indian context","status":"draft","updated":"2026-10-20","reviewer":[],"covers":["p1-6d5b01b1","p1-19591112","p1-40ad35dc"],"exam":"<p><strong>Definition.</strong> Early intervention in psychosis (EIP) is a service model and treatment philosophy aimed at two goals:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Early detection:</strong> identifying people at clinical high risk, and those with first-episode psychosis (FEP) as soon as possible, to shorten the <strong>duration of untreated psychosis (DUP)</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Intensive, phase-specific treatment</strong> during the <strong>critical period</strong> (first 3–5 years), when most deterioration, relapse and suicide occur.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>It is delivered by specialised, youth-friendly multidisciplinary teams (McGorry, EPPIC Melbourne 1992; Birchwood&#39;s critical-period hypothesis 1998).</p>\n<h3>Mechanism (rationale)</h3>\n<p>Long DUP → psychosocial damage (loss of education, job and relationships) + possible biological &quot;toxicity&quot; (sensitisation, neuroprogression) → poorer response and outcome. Earlier and intensive treatment in the critical period → better remission, fewer relapses, preserved social roles → better long-term trajectory [1,2].</p>\n<h3>Components of EIP</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Stage</th>\n<th>Interventions</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Community detection</td>\n<td>Public awareness, GP/school training, rapid-access pathways (TIPS Norway reduced DUP from 16 to 5 weeks)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Clinical high risk</td>\n<td>Monitoring, CBT, treatment of comorbidity; antipsychotics not first-line [3]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>First episode</td>\n<td>Low-dose second-generation antipsychotic (&quot;start low, go slow&quot;), clozapine early if two failures; CBTp; family psychoeducation; IPS supported employment/education; case management (caseload ~1:15); physical health and substance use care</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Critical period (2–5 years)</td>\n<td>Relapse prevention, LAI option, suicide prevention, recovery and transition planning</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Assessment</h3>\n<p>DUP (time from first psychotic symptom to adequate treatment); pathways to care; CAARMS/SIPS for high risk; PANSS; function; suicide risk; substance use; baseline metabolic profile; organic screen (thyroid, drugs, MRI or EEG if atypical); family burden.</p>\n<h3>Evidence that early detection and treatment improve outcome</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Evidence</th>\n<th>Finding</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>DUP and outcome (Marshall 2005; Perkins 2005; Penttilä 2014)</td>\n<td>Longer DUP → poorer symptoms, function and response; correlation r ≈ 0.2–0.3 [2]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TIPS (Norway; Melle 2008; Hegelstad 2012)</td>\n<td>Early detection reduced DUP; fewer negative symptoms and better recovery at 10 years (31% vs 15%)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>OPUS (Denmark; Petersen 2005)</td>\n<td>Better outcomes at 2 years; <strong>benefits lost by 5 and 10 years</strong> after transfer to standard care</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lambeth Early Onset (LEO; Craig 2004)</td>\n<td>Fewer relapses and admissions at 18 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Correll 2018 meta-analysis (10 RCTs, n=2,176)</td>\n<td>EIP superior to usual care on discontinuation, hospitalisation, symptoms, function, school/work</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RAISE-ETP (Kane 2016)</td>\n<td>NAVIGATE better quality of life, especially with DUP under 74 weeks</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Management principles</h3>\n<p>Rapid assessment (target ≤2 weeks) → engagement → lowest effective dose → psychosocial package → family work → supported employment/education → relapse prevention → extended EIP (5 years) or a planned step-down.</p>\n<p><strong>Conclusion.</strong> The first statement, &quot;early detection and treatment has significantly improved outcome&quot;, is <strong>partly true</strong>. EIP clearly improves short- and medium-term outcomes and reduced DUP is associated with better outcome. But many gains fade when care stops at 2 years, and the causal role of DUP is still debated. In India, the concept is valid and urgently needed because DUP is long, but it must be adapted rather than copied: community- and family-based detection, task-sharing and integration with the DMHP are more feasible than Western standalone teams.</p>\n","depth":"<h3>Background</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Sullivan (1927) argued that schizophrenia should be treated at its earliest stage [3].</li>\n<li>Falloon&#39;s Buckingham project (1980s) used GP-based detection.</li>\n<li><strong>1992:</strong> EPPIC in Melbourne. <strong>1994:</strong> the PACE clinic for UHR [3].</li>\n<li><strong>1998:</strong> Birchwood&#39;s critical-period hypothesis.</li>\n<li>2000s: national rollouts (UK NHS Plan 2000; 2016 NHS waiting standard of treatment within 2 weeks, including ARMS) [3].</li>\n<li><strong>2005:</strong> WHO and the International Early Psychosis Association (IEPA) Declaration.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Epidemiology</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Mean DUP is about 1–2 years in high-income countries, with a median of about 3–6 months. It is often longer in LMICs: Indian studies report mean DUPs of 6–30 months, depending on setting.</li>\n<li>The first 5 years carry the highest suicide risk. In the Madras cohort, 7 of 16 deaths over 20 years were suicides, all in people under 35 [1].</li>\n<li>Over 80% relapse within 5 years of a first episode [4].</li>\n<li><strong>Indian pathway-to-care data:</strong> most families first consult faith healers or general practitioners. In the Madras cohort, insidious onset (54.5%) was common [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Classification and criteria</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>First-episode psychosis:</strong> first presentation with full-threshold psychotic symptoms for over 1 week, regardless of eventual diagnosis (schizophrenia, schizophreniform, ATPD, affective psychosis).</li>\n<li><strong>DUP:</strong> onset of first positive symptom to start of adequate antipsychotic treatment. <strong>DUI</strong> (duration of untreated illness) includes the prodrome.</li>\n<li><strong>Clinical staging (McGorry):</strong> 0 (at risk, asymptomatic), 1a (non-specific distress), 1b (UHR), 2 (FEP), 3 (incomplete remission or relapse), 4 (persistent, unremitting).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Mechanism in detail — why DUP might matter</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Neurotoxicity hypothesis:</strong> glutamatergic excitotoxicity and oxidative stress during active psychosis cause progressive change. Gupta &amp; Kulhara cite longer DUP predicting worse response and higher relapse as evidence of an active pathological process [2].</li>\n<li><strong>Psychosocial hypothesis:</strong> untreated psychosis disrupts development (education, work, relationships) and family relationships (expressed emotion), and leads to trauma (coercive admission).</li>\n<li><strong>Confound hypothesis:</strong> insidious onset and negative symptoms cause both long DUP and poor outcome. Long DUP may be a marker rather than a cause.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The truth is likely a mix of the three.</p>\n<h3>Clinical features in detail — the critical period</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Most functional decline happens in the first 2–5 years, then plateaus.</li>\n<li>The risk of relapse after stopping medication is highest early. Maudsley notes that withdrawing antipsychotics after 1–2 years led to almost 80% relapse at 1 year [4].</li>\n<li>Suicide, violence and substance misuse cluster here.</li>\n<li>Developmental tasks (completing education, starting work) are at stake.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Assessment in detail</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Domain</th>\n<th>Tool</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>DUP</td>\n<td>Nottingham Onset Schedule; Interview for Retrospective Assessment of Onset of Schizophrenia (IRAOS)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pathways to care</td>\n<td>WHO Encounter Form</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High risk</td>\n<td>CAARMS, SIPS [3]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Symptoms</td>\n<td>PANSS, CDSS (depression)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Function</td>\n<td>SOFAS, PSP</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cognition</td>\n<td>Brief Assessment of Cognition in Schizophrenia (BACS)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Physical</td>\n<td>Weight, waist, BP, glucose, lipids, ECG, prolactin</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Differential (in first-episode work)</h3>\n<p>Substance-induced psychosis, ATPD (good prognosis; avoid premature schizophrenia label), affective psychosis (bipolar disorder in youth), organic psychosis (autoimmune encephalitis, epilepsy, HIV, thyroid, Wilson&#39;s), autism with stress-related psychotic features, and trauma-related dissociation.</p>\n<h3>Management details</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Antipsychotics:</strong> first-episode patients respond to lower doses and are more sensitive to side effects. Prefer aripiprazole, risperidone (low dose) or amisulpride; avoid olanzapine as first choice in young people where possible because of weight gain. Start metformin early if weight rises [4].</li>\n<li><strong>Clozapine early:</strong> after two adequate trials; delays in clozapine use are associated with poorer response.</li>\n<li><strong>LAIs in first episode:</strong> large benefit over oral risperidone in first episode [4].</li>\n<li><strong>Duration:</strong> at least 1–2 years; dose reduction only with care and monitoring [4].</li>\n<li><strong>Psychosocial:</strong> CBTp, family psychoeducation, IPS, cognitive remediation, peer support.</li>\n<li><strong>Physical health:</strong> smoking cessation, exercise; prevention of metabolic syndrome from the start.</li>\n<li><strong>Special groups:</strong> adolescents (Maudsley Chapter 5), pregnancy, and comorbid cannabis use (the strongest predictor of relapse after discontinuation) [4].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Evidence table</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Study</th>\n<th>Design</th>\n<th>n</th>\n<th>Main result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Marshall et al., 2005</td>\n<td>Systematic review of DUP</td>\n<td>26 studies</td>\n<td>Longer DUP → worse outcome at 6 and 12 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Perkins et al., 2005 [2]</td>\n<td>Meta-analysis</td>\n<td>43 studies</td>\n<td>Longer DUP → poorer response</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Melle et al., 2008; Hegelstad 2012 (TIPS)</td>\n<td>Quasi-experimental</td>\n<td>281</td>\n<td>Shorter DUP; better 10-year recovery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Petersen et al., 2005; Secher 2015 (OPUS)</td>\n<td>RCT</td>\n<td>547</td>\n<td>2-year benefit lost at 5/10 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Craig et al., 2004 (LEO)</td>\n<td>RCT</td>\n<td>144</td>\n<td>Fewer relapses and admissions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kane et al., 2016 (RAISE-ETP)</td>\n<td>Cluster RCT</td>\n<td>404</td>\n<td>Better QoL; larger effect with short DUP</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Correll et al., 2018</td>\n<td>Meta-analysis</td>\n<td>10 RCTs, 2,176</td>\n<td>EIP superior on all main outcomes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chang et al., 2015 (Hong Kong EASY)</td>\n<td>RCT extended care</td>\n<td>160</td>\n<td>Extending EIP to 3 years kept gains</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thara, 2004 (Madras) [1]</td>\n<td>Cohort</td>\n<td>90</td>\n<td>Relapse common but good functional outcome in an LMIC setting</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Critical appraisal</h3>\n<p><strong>For the statement &quot;early detection and treatment have significantly improved outcome&quot;:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>RCT and meta-analytic evidence of better short-term outcomes (Correll 2018).</li>\n<li>Shorter DUP linked to better response.</li>\n<li>Cost-effective: savings from fewer admissions; UHR services were cost-effective in the UK [3].</li>\n<li>Hope and engagement for young people and families.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Against, or limitations:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Durability:</strong> OPUS showed benefits fade after care ends. Outcomes depend on continued care, not on &quot;early&quot; alone.</li>\n<li><strong>DUP causality</strong> is not established; lead-time bias and confounding by mode of onset are possible.</li>\n<li><strong>Population-level outcomes</strong> (mortality, long-term recovery rates) have not clearly changed. Jääskeläinen 2013 found no improvement in recovery rates over decades.</li>\n<li>High-risk detection has low PPV, and antipsychotic prevention is not supported [3].</li>\n<li>Most evidence comes from high-income countries with resources that do not exist in most LMICs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Verdict:</strong> the statement holds for short- and medium-term outcomes and for service experience. It is overstated for long-term outcomes unless EIP is sustained.</p>\n<h3>Indian context (critical evaluation)</h3>\n<p><strong>Why EIP is needed in India</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>DUP is long: delayed help-seeking, first contact with faith healers, stigma, and the distance to services.</li>\n<li>Psychiatrists are scarce (about 0.75 per 100,000), and the treatment gap for psychosis is high (NMHS 2016: about 75%).</li>\n<li>Young population; suicide is a major cause of death in youth.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What is different</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Better natural outcomes in some Indian cohorts (WHO IPSS/DOSMeD; Madras: 76% of men employed, 73.7% married, only 5 of 61 continuously ill), so the added value of Western-style EIP may be smaller here, or differently located [1].</li>\n<li>Strong family involvement can serve as a resource for detection, adherence and rehabilitation.</li>\n<li>Informal employment allows role recovery without formal IPS [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Barriers</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Few specialised teams; the cost of multidisciplinary staff; urban concentration.</li>\n<li>Poor continuity; out-of-pocket costs.</li>\n<li>Mental health literacy is low; stigma around marriage leads to hiding illness [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Feasible Indian model</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Integrate early detection into the <strong>DMHP</strong>, primary care (Ayushman Bharat health and wellness centres), schools and colleges.</li>\n<li>Task-sharing with trained community health workers (ASHAs).</li>\n<li>Collaborate with faith healers (the Dawa-Dua programme at Erwadi, Tamil Nadu).</li>\n<li>Use Tele-MANAS and telepsychiatry for follow-up.</li>\n<li>Family psychoeducation; affordable generic antipsychotics and depots.</li>\n<li>Examples: SCARF first-episode programmes, NIMHANS early psychosis services, and COPSI community care (Chatterjee 2014).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Legal framework:</strong> MHCA 2017 (rights, advance directives, supported admission); the National Mental Health Policy 2014; the National Suicide Prevention Strategy 2022.</p>\n<h3>Recent advances</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Established:</strong> EIP as a standard of care in many high-income countries; NICE 2-week access standard.</li>\n<li><strong>Emerging:</strong><ul>\n<li>Extended EIP (5 years).</li>\n<li>Transdiagnostic youth services (headspace model; Orygen).</li>\n<li>Digital tools (smartphone relapse prediction, online peer support such as Horyzons).</li>\n<li>Clozapine earlier in the pathway.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Investigational:</strong> biomarker-guided staging; non-D2 drugs (xanomeline–trospium) for first episode; LMIC adaptations, for example in the NIHR-funded global early psychosis networks.</li>\n</ul>\n","refs":"<ol>\n<li>Thara R. Twenty-year course of schizophrenia: the Madras Longitudinal Study. <em>Can J Psychiatry</em> 2004; 49(8):564–569.</li>\n<li>Gupta S, Kulhara P. What is schizophrenia: a neurodevelopmental or neurodegenerative disorder or a combination of both? <em>Indian J Psychiatry</em> 2010; 52(1):21–27.</li>\n<li>Thompson A, Marwaha S, Broome MR. At-risk mental state for psychosis: identification and current treatment approaches. <em>BJPsych Advances</em> 2016; 22:186–193.</li>\n<li>Taylor DM, Barnes TRE, Young AH. <em>The Maudsley Prescribing Guidelines in Psychiatry</em>. 15th ed. Wiley, 2025; Chapter 1 (Antipsychotic prophylaxis; First-episode schizophrenia).</li>\n<li>Correll CU, Galling B, Pawar A, et al. Comparison of early intervention services vs treatment as usual for early-phase psychosis: a systematic review, meta-analysis, and meta-regression. <em>JAMA Psychiatry</em> 2018; 75:555–565.</li>\n<li>Marshall M, Lewis S, Lockwood A, et al. Association between duration of untreated psychosis and outcome in cohorts of first-episode patients. <em>Arch Gen Psychiatry</em> 2005; 62:975–983.</li>\n<li>Secher RG, Hjorthøj CR, Austin SF, et al. Ten-year follow-up of the OPUS specialized early intervention trial for patients with a first episode of psychosis. <em>Schizophr Bull</em> 2015; 41:617–626.</li>\n<li>Hegelstad WtV, Larsen TK, Auestad B, et al. Long-term follow-up of the TIPS early detection in psychosis study: effects on 10-year outcome. <em>Am J Psychiatry</em> 2012; 169:374–380.</li>\n<li>Kane JM, Robinson DG, Schooler NR, et al. Comprehensive versus usual community care for first-episode psychosis: 2-year outcomes from the NIMH RAISE Early Treatment Program. <em>Am J Psychiatry</em> 2016; 173:362–372.</li>\n<li>Gururaj G, Varghese M, Benegal V, et al. <em>National Mental Health Survey of India, 2015–16</em>. NIMHANS, 2016.</li>\n</ol>\n"},"maintenance-treatment-schizophrenia":{"title":"Maintenance treatment of schizophrenia — critical evaluation","status":"draft","updated":"2026-10-20","reviewer":[],"covers":["p1-3597bec2"],"exam":"<p><strong>Definition.</strong> Maintenance treatment is the continued, long-term use of antipsychotic medication <strong>plus psychosocial interventions</strong> after an acute episode remits. Its aims are to prevent relapse and rehospitalisation, consolidate recovery, reduce suicide and violence, and maximise functioning and quality of life, while limiting long-term adverse effects. The phases are acute → stabilisation (about 6 months) → stable/maintenance (years).</p>\n<h3>Mechanism</h3>\n<p>Continued D2 blockade (60–80% occupancy) → prevents re-emergence of presynaptic striatal dopamine dysregulation under stress → fewer relapses → less relapse-related functional decline and treatment resistance. Against this: long-term blockade → D2 up-regulation/supersensitivity → withdrawal-associated relapse, tardive dyskinesia, metabolic harm [1].</p>\n<h3>Core evidence on relapse</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Situation</th>\n<th>Relapse on antipsychotic</th>\n<th>Relapse off antipsychotic</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>First episode, 6–12 months (placebo-controlled meta-analysis)</td>\n<td>26%</td>\n<td>61% [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>First episode, 18–24 months (8 RCTs, 2018)</td>\n<td>35%</td>\n<td>61% [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>First episode, stopping after 1–2 years (Gitlin 2001)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>~80% at 1 year, 98% at 2 years [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Multi-episode (Leucht 2012, 65 RCTs)</td>\n<td>27% at 1 year</td>\n<td>64% at 1 year</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Depot vs oral (meta-analyses)</td>\n<td>Relative risk of relapse 30% lower, absolute risk 10% lower with depot [1]</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Assessment during maintenance</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Symptoms and relapse signatures:</strong> PANSS/BPRS, early warning signs (sleep loss, suspiciousness, withdrawal).</li>\n<li><strong>Function:</strong> Personal and Social Performance scale (PSP), GAF.</li>\n<li><strong>Adherence:</strong> up to 25% non-adherent 10 days after discharge, 50% at 1 year, 75% at 2 years [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Physical monitoring:</strong> weight, BMI and waist (monthly for 3 months, then 3-monthly in year 1, then yearly), BP, fasting glucose/HbA1c, lipids, prolactin, ECG where indicated, AIMS for tardive dyskinesia every 6–12 months.</li>\n<li><strong>Substance use, suicidality, depression.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Management</h3>\n<p><strong>1. Duration</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>First episode: at least <strong>1–2 years</strong> after remission. Consider very gradual, guided dose reduction only in a stable, well-supported patient who understands the risks [1,2].</li>\n<li>Multi-episode (≥2 episodes): <strong>at least 2–5 years</strong>, often indefinite, especially with prior serious suicide attempt, violence or rapid relapse [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. Drug and dose</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Continue the drug and dose that worked acutely [1].</li>\n<li>Prophylactic efficacy starts to be lost below about <strong>5 mg/day risperidone equivalent</strong>; higher doses add only side effects [1].</li>\n<li>Targeted/intermittent treatment (drug only when symptoms return) is inferior and may raise TD risk [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>3. Long-acting injectables (LAIs)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Offer early, not only after non-adherence: risperidone, paliperidone 1-/3-/6-monthly, aripiprazole, olanzapine pamoate, haloperidol and fluphenazine decanoate, flupentixol, zuclopenthixol [1].</li>\n<li>Real-world data favour LAIs and clozapine for preventing rehospitalisation (Tiihonen 2017) [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>4. Clozapine</strong> for treatment-resistant illness (failure of two antipsychotics); it also reduces suicide (InterSePT) and mortality.</p>\n<p><strong>5. Psychosocial treatment</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Family psychoeducation (reduces relapse by about 50%).</li>\n<li>CBT for psychosis; adherence work; cognitive remediation.</li>\n<li>Supported employment (IPS model); social skills training; assertive community treatment for those at high risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>6. Physical health</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Metabolic monitoring, smoking cessation, exercise, metformin for antipsychotic weight gain.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>7. Stopping, when chosen</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperbolic taper over months to years. Abrupt withdrawal doubles relapse in the first 6 months [1].</li>\n<li>Agree a relapse plan with patient and family.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Key evidence</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Leucht 2012 (Lancet):</strong> antipsychotic vs placebo, 1-year relapse 27% vs 64%; rehospitalisation 10% vs 26%.</li>\n<li><strong>Wunderink 2013 (7-year follow-up):</strong> guided dose reduction/discontinuation group had double the recovery rate (40% vs 18%) despite more early relapses; criticised and not replicated [1].</li>\n<li><strong>HAMLETT/RADAR (2023):</strong> dose reduction increased relapse without improving function in the short term [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Tiihonen 2017 (Swedish cohort, n=29,823):</strong> clozapine and LAIs had the lowest rehospitalisation; longer treatment gave lower risk [1].</li>\n<li><strong>CATIE 2005:</strong> 74% discontinued their assigned drug within 18 months.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conclusion.</strong> Evidence strongly supports continuous antipsychotic maintenance, preferably at the lowest effective dose, with early use of LAIs and clozapine where indicated. It must be combined with family and psychosocial interventions and physical health care. The open question is not whether to maintain treatment but <strong>for whom and for how long</strong>. A minority (perhaps 18–30% of first-episode patients) may stay well without long-term drugs [1]. We cannot yet identify them, so decisions about reduction should be shared, slow and closely monitored.</p>\n","depth":"<h3>Background</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Before chlorpromazine (1952), most patients with schizophrenia stayed in asylums. Depot fluphenazine (1960s) allowed community maintenance.</li>\n<li>Hogarty&#39;s 1970s studies showed that drug plus &quot;major role therapy&quot; was additive, which established combined maintenance.</li>\n<li>The 1990s–2000s second-generation antipsychotic era was followed by CATIE and CUtLASS, which showed no broad effectiveness advantage for newer drugs.</li>\n<li>The 2010s brought debate about long-term harms and recovery-oriented deprescribing (Wunderink, Moncrieff), and the 2020s brought guided dose-reduction trials (RADAR, HAMLETT) and hyperbolic tapering [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Epidemiology</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Over 80% of first-episode patients relapse within 5 years (Robinson 1999), and the risk is about five times higher after stopping medication.</li>\n<li>Madras Longitudinal Study: more than 80% relapsed over 20 years, with nearly half of relapses in years 10–20; yet only 22 of 61 were ever hospitalised and nearly half were not on treatment at 20 years [3].</li>\n<li>Suicide risk (lifetime ~5–10%) is concentrated in the first decade [1]. In the Madras cohort, 7 of 16 deaths were suicides, all in people aged under 35 [3].</li>\n<li>Non-adherence: 50% at 1 year, 75% at 2 years [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Classification and criteria</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Relapse definitions</strong> vary: hospitalisation; a rise in PANSS of ≥25%, or ≥10 points if baseline is low; specific positive items ≥4 (moderate); suicidal or violent behaviour. The Maudsley notes that these focus on positive symptoms and miss cognitive and negative domains [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Remission (Andreasen 2005):</strong> 8 PANSS items (P1, P2, P3, N1, N4, N6, G5, G9) ≤3 (mild) for ≥6 months.</li>\n<li>ICD-11 course specifiers (first episode, multiple episodes, continuous; currently symptomatic, partial or full remission) guide maintenance decisions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Mechanism in detail</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Relapse biology:</strong> stress → dopamine release in a sensitised system → aberrant salience. Maintenance D2 blockade raises the threshold.</li>\n<li><strong>Supersensitivity:</strong> chronic blockade raises D2/D3 receptor availability. Imaging shows increased D2/D3 availability in medicated but not drug-naïve patients. This may persist for months to years after stopping, which explains why relapses cluster in the first 3 months after abrupt cessation (60% of relapses over 4 years in one analysis) and why slow tapers help [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Neurotoxicity of relapse vs of drugs:</strong> longer DUP and more relapses are linked with worse outcomes. Antipsychotic exposure is linked with grey matter loss in some cohorts. Causality is unresolved.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Clinical features in detail — predictors of relapse</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Non-adherence (strongest), substance use (especially cannabis), high expressed emotion in the family, residual symptoms, poor insight, male sex, adverse effect burden, a negative attitude to treatment, and stressful life events [1].</li>\n<li>Relapse signature: insomnia, irritability, social withdrawal, suspiciousness and preoccupation usually precede florid psychosis by 1–4 weeks.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Assessment in detail</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Domain</th>\n<th>Tool</th>\n<th>Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Psychopathology</td>\n<td>PANSS/BPRS; CDSS for depression</td>\n<td>Every visit / 3-monthly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Function</td>\n<td>PSP, SOFAS, GAF</td>\n<td>6-monthly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Adherence</td>\n<td>Pill counts, LAI records, plasma levels (TDM)</td>\n<td>Every visit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Side effects</td>\n<td>GASS/LUNSERS, AIMS, Barnes Akathisia Rating Scale, Simpson–Angus</td>\n<td>6-monthly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metabolic</td>\n<td>Weight, waist, BP, glucose/HbA1c, lipids</td>\n<td>Baseline, 12 weeks, then yearly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Endocrine</td>\n<td>Prolactin; sexual function; menstrual history in women</td>\n<td>Baseline, then if symptomatic [2]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cardiac</td>\n<td>ECG (QTc) for high-risk drugs and combinations</td>\n<td>Baseline, after dose changes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bone</td>\n<td>DEXA if long-term hyperprolactinaemia</td>\n<td>As indicated [2]</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Differential — apparent relapse vs other causes</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Presentation</th>\n<th>Distinguishing point</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>True relapse</td>\n<td>Return of the previous symptom pattern</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Withdrawal-associated psychosis</td>\n<td>Within weeks of abrupt stop, alongside cholinergic or histaminergic withdrawal features [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Akathisia</td>\n<td>Inner restlessness, often mistaken for agitation; worsens if dose is increased</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Post-psychotic depression</td>\n<td>Low mood with insight, suicide risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Substance-induced psychosis</td>\n<td>Timing with use; urine toxicology</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Medical/organic</td>\n<td>Delirium, endocrine (hypothyroidism), drug interaction lowering levels (e.g., smoking restarted with clozapine/olanzapine)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Management details</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dose:</strong> acute-effective dose generally continued; the 5 mg risperidone-equivalent threshold [1]. Use the lowest effective dose in the elderly and in women after menopause (oestrogen loss reduces CYP activity and raises drug need) [2].</li>\n<li><strong>Women of childbearing age:</strong> avoid prolactin-raising drugs where possible (risperidone, paliperidone, amisulpride, FGAs). Aripiprazole is useful; consider contraception and folate; monitor bone density [2].</li>\n<li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> continue the effective antipsychotic rather than switching; relapse risk is high postpartum.</li>\n<li><strong>LAI practicalities:</strong> test dose for FGA depots; olanzapine pamoate needs 3 hours of observation for post-injection delirium/sedation syndrome; paliperidone 3-monthly only after ≥4 months stable on monthly [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Clozapine:</strong> blood monitoring; plasma level &gt;350 µg/L target; LAI cannot replace it; continue long-term in responders.</li>\n<li><strong>Polypharmacy:</strong> generally avoid; clozapine + aripiprazole has observational support (lowest rehospitalisation in Tiihonen 2019).</li>\n<li><strong>Stopping:</strong> hyperbolic taper (for example 25% reduction of the current dose every 3–6 months), tapering over a year or more reduced relapse hazard 3.5-fold compared with over days [1]. Weigh symptom-free duration, adverse effects, past pattern of illness, previous reduction attempts, social stability, cost of relapse, and capacity to monitor [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Evidence table</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Study</th>\n<th>Design</th>\n<th>n</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Leucht et al., 2012</td>\n<td>Meta-analysis</td>\n<td>65 RCTs, 6,493</td>\n<td>Relapse 27% vs 64% at 1 year</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gitlin et al., 2001 [1]</td>\n<td>Discontinuation, first episode</td>\n<td>53</td>\n<td>78% relapse at 1 year, 96–98% at 2 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wunderink et al., 2013</td>\n<td>7-year follow-up of RCT</td>\n<td>128</td>\n<td>Recovery 40.4% vs 17.6% in reduction arm [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tiihonen et al., 2017</td>\n<td>Swedish national cohort</td>\n<td>29,823</td>\n<td>Clozapine and LAIs lowest rehospitalisation [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kishimoto et al., 2014</td>\n<td>Meta-analysis LAI vs oral RCTs</td>\n<td>21 RCTs</td>\n<td>No difference in RCTs; mirror-image studies favour LAIs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CATIE (Lieberman 2005)</td>\n<td>Pragmatic RCT</td>\n<td>1,493</td>\n<td>74% discontinued by 18 months; olanzapine longest [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Moncrieff et al., 2023 (RADAR)</td>\n<td>RCT dose reduction</td>\n<td>253</td>\n<td>More severe relapse with reduction; no social benefit at 2 years</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Critical appraisal</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Discontinuation trials may overstate drug benefit.</strong> Most stopped drugs quickly. Withdrawal-associated relapse may inflate placebo-arm relapse [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Relapse is not outcome.</strong> Functional recovery, cognition and negative symptoms change little with maintenance. Wunderink&#39;s data suggest lower long-term exposure may favour function, but the study had flaws [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Long-term harms:</strong> TD (~5% per year with FGAs), metabolic syndrome, cardiovascular mortality, hyperprolactinaemia and bone loss, and possible brain volume loss [1,2].</li>\n<li><strong>Heterogeneity:</strong> 18–30% of first-episode patients may not need long-term drugs, and no biomarker identifies them; cannabis use is the only factor linked to outcome after discontinuation [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Developing-country outcomes</strong> (Madras: high employment and marriage, few hospitalisations despite many off treatment) suggest that social context shapes maintenance needs [3].</li>\n<li><strong>Ethics:</strong> the patient&#39;s right to decline long-term medication (MHCA 2017, advance directives) must be balanced against relapse risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Indian context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>MHCA 2017:</strong> right to community living, advance directives, and supported admission. Maintenance treatment depends on consent; a nominated representative can support decisions.</li>\n<li><strong>District Mental Health Programme</strong> and free essential drugs (including LAIs such as fluphenazine decanoate and haloperidol decanoate) improve access. Newer LAIs are costly.</li>\n<li>Family involvement is central in India; family psychoeducation is feasible and effective.</li>\n<li>Indian studies: the Madras 20-year outcome [3]; the Chennai SCARF community care model; COPSI (Community care for People with Schizophrenia in India, Chatterjee 2014), where collaborative community care plus facility care modestly improved outcomes.</li>\n<li>Concerns: irregular supply, out-of-pocket costs, stigma, and limited physical health monitoring.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Recent advances</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Established:</strong> paliperidone palmitate 6-monthly; aripiprazole lauroxil and 2-monthly aripiprazole; risperidone subcutaneous monthly formulations (in some markets).</li>\n<li><strong>Emerging:</strong> muscarinic agonist xanomeline–trospium (approved 2024) offers maintenance options without D2 blockade; long-term extension data so far show sustained effect and no weight gain.</li>\n<li><strong>Investigational:</strong> prediction models for safe discontinuation; digital relapse prediction using smartphone data; hyperbolic tapering trials.</li>\n</ul>\n","refs":"<ol>\n<li>Taylor DM, Barnes TRE, Young AH. <em>The Maudsley Prescribing Guidelines in Psychiatry</em>. 15th ed. Wiley, 2025; Chapter 1 (Antipsychotic prophylaxis, pp. 28–33; Stopping antipsychotics, pp. 119–125; Antipsychotic depots/long-acting injections, pp. 74–80).</li>\n<li>Mu E, Gurvich C, Kulkarni J. Estrogen and psychosis — a review and future directions. <em>Arch Womens Ment Health</em> 2024; 27:877–885.</li>\n<li>Thara R. Twenty-year course of schizophrenia: the Madras Longitudinal Study. <em>Can J Psychiatry</em> 2004; 49(8):564–569.</li>\n<li>Leucht S, Tardy M, Komossa K, et al. Antipsychotic drugs versus placebo for relapse prevention in schizophrenia: a systematic review and meta-analysis. <em>Lancet</em> 2012; 379:2063–2071.</li>\n<li>Wunderink L, Nieboer RM, Wiersma D, et al. Recovery in remitted first-episode psychosis at 7 years of follow-up of an early dose reduction/discontinuation or maintenance treatment strategy. <em>JAMA Psychiatry</em> 2013; 70:913–920.</li>\n<li>Tiihonen J, Mittendorfer-Rutz E, Majak M, et al. Real-world effectiveness of antipsychotic treatments in a nationwide cohort of 29,823 patients with schizophrenia. <em>JAMA Psychiatry</em> 2017; 74:686–693.</li>\n<li>Chatterjee S, Naik S, John S, et al. Effectiveness of a community-based intervention for people with schizophrenia and their caregivers in India (COPSI). <em>Lancet</em> 2014; 383:1385–1394.</li>\n<li>Government of India. <em>The Mental Healthcare Act, 2017</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n"},"neuromodulation-in-psychiatry":{"title":"Neuromodulation (brain stimulation) techniques in psychiatry — critical evaluation","status":"draft","updated":"2026-10-20","reviewer":[],"covers":["p1-79004221","p1-9c113dab","p1-445a7a09"],"exam":"<p><strong>Definition.</strong> Neuromodulation means changing neural activity by delivering electrical, magnetic or other energy to specific brain targets or networks. Unlike drugs, it acts on a defined circuit. In psychiatry the techniques fall into three groups:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Convulsive:</strong> ECT, magnetic seizure therapy (MST).</li>\n<li><strong>Non-invasive, non-convulsive:</strong> rTMS/theta burst stimulation, deep TMS, tDCS, cranial electrotherapy, trigeminal nerve stimulation.</li>\n<li><strong>Invasive/surgical:</strong> vagus nerve stimulation (VNS), deep brain stimulation (DBS), ablative surgery (anterior capsulotomy, cingulotomy) [1,2].</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The statement &quot;current neuromodulation approaches hold much promise&quot; is <strong>true for ECT and rTMS in depression, partly true for OCD and schizophrenia, and not yet true for most invasive methods</strong>. Promise must be weighed against effect sizes, durability, blinding problems and access.</p>\n<h3>Mechanism</h3>\n<p>Energy delivery → depolarisation (TMS, ECT) or a shift in resting membrane potential (tDCS) → synaptic plasticity (LTP/LTD-like), neurotrophic change (BDNF), neurotransmitter release → <strong>normalisation of dysfunctional networks</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Depression: hypoactive left DLPFC, hyperactive subgenual cingulate.</li>\n<li>OCD: cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical (CSTC) hyperactivity.</li>\n<li>Hallucinations: hyperactive left temporoparietal cortex.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>ECT adds a generalised seizure → hippocampal neurogenesis, increased GABA, raised seizure threshold (anticonvulsant hypothesis) and HPA normalisation [1,2].</p>\n<h3>Techniques compared</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Technique</th>\n<th>How</th>\n<th>Main indication / evidence</th>\n<th>Key limits</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>ECT</strong></td>\n<td>Brief-pulse current under GA, induces 20–25 s seizure; RUL ultrabrief or bitemporal; 2–3/week, 6–12 sessions</td>\n<td>TRD, psychotic depression, catatonia, suicidality, mania, NMS; response 60–80%, remission ~50% (UK ECT Review Group 2003)</td>\n<td>Anaesthesia; retrograde amnesia; relapse ~50% at 6 months without continuation; stigma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>rTMS / iTBS</strong></td>\n<td>Coil over scalp; 10 Hz left DLPFC or 1 Hz right; iTBS 3 min</td>\n<td>Unipolar depression (strong), TRD, peripartum, post-stroke; OCD, PTSD, GAD, negative symptoms (moderate) — IPS CPG 2023</td>\n<td>Daily visits 4–6 weeks; response 30–50%; large sham effect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Deep TMS</strong></td>\n<td>H-coil</td>\n<td>MDD (H1), OCD (H7), smoking (H4)</td>\n<td>Less focal; more headache</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>tDCS</strong></td>\n<td>1–2 mA anodal over left DLPFC, 20–30 min</td>\n<td>Depression (modest), negative symptoms, hallucinations</td>\n<td>Small, inconsistent effects; inferior to escitalopram (ELECT-TDCS)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>MST</strong></td>\n<td>Magnetic seizure, more focal</td>\n<td>Depression; ECT-like efficacy with fewer cognitive effects in early trials</td>\n<td>Investigational</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>VNS</strong></td>\n<td>Implanted pulse generator on left vagus</td>\n<td>Chronic TRD (FDA 2005); slow, cumulative response over 1–2 years</td>\n<td>Surgery, cost; hoarseness; weak RCT evidence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>DBS</strong></td>\n<td>Electrodes in sgACC (Cg25), ventral capsule/striatum, MFB, NAcc</td>\n<td>Refractory OCD (FDA humanitarian exemption 2009), TRD</td>\n<td>Two large TRD RCTs failed; surgical risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ablative surgery</strong></td>\n<td>Gamma-knife capsulotomy, cingulotomy</td>\n<td>Highly refractory OCD/depression</td>\n<td>Irreversible; ethical oversight</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Assessment before neuromodulation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Confirm diagnosis and <strong>true</strong> treatment resistance (adequate dose, duration, adherence).</li>\n<li>Severity scales (HAM-D/MADRS, Y-BOCS, PANSS).</li>\n<li>Medical work-up: seizure history, metal implants (TMS), cardiac and anaesthetic fitness (ECT), baseline cognition (MoCA/MMSE).</li>\n<li>Capacity and informed consent.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Management — how to place them (for depression)</h3>\n<p>Two adequate antidepressant trials fail → optimise or augment (lithium, aripiprazole, T3) <strong>+ psychotherapy</strong> → <strong>rTMS/iTBS</strong> if non-psychotic, not acutely suicidal, outpatient → <strong>ECT</strong> (first-line if psychotic, catatonic, acutely suicidal, not eating, or prior ECT response) → <strong>ketamine/esketamine</strong> for rapid response → continuation/maintenance (pharmacotherapy ± maintenance ECT/rTMS) → <strong>VNS/DBS</strong> only in specialist centres for chronic, multiply-resistant illness [1–3].</p>\n<h3>Key evidence</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>UK ECT Review Group 2003, Lancet:</strong> real ECT &gt; sham (effect size about 0.9); bilateral &gt; unilateral.</li>\n<li><strong>Mutz 2019 network meta-analysis:</strong> rTMS SMD 0.83 vs sham; bitemporal ECT had higher response than HF rTMS [1].</li>\n<li><strong>THREE-D 2018:</strong> iTBS non-inferior to 10 Hz rTMS.</li>\n<li><strong>ELECT-TDCS 2017</strong> (n=245): tDCS &gt; placebo but inferior to escitalopram.</li>\n<li><strong>BROADEN (sgACC) and RECLAIM (VC/VS) DBS trials:</strong> stopped for futility. Long-term open follow-up shows sustained response in some patients.</li>\n<li><strong>SAINT 2022:</strong> 78.6% remission with accelerated fMRI-guided iTBS in a small RCT.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conclusion.</strong> The promise is real but uneven. <strong>ECT</strong> remains the most effective acute antidepressant treatment. <strong>rTMS</strong> has moved from experimental to established second-line care for depression. tDCS, VNS and DBS remain adjuncts or research tools. The best use of neuromodulation now is targeted, personalised and combined with drugs and psychotherapy, not as a replacement for them. Wider claims should wait for larger, well-blinded and longer trials.</p>\n","depth":"<h3>Background</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>1938:</strong> Cerletti and Bini introduced ECT in Rome, after Meduna&#39;s chemical convulsive therapy (1934). It was refined by anaesthesia and muscle relaxants (1950s), brief-pulse stimulation (1970s–80s), and ultrabrief pulse right-unilateral ECT (2000s).</li>\n<li><strong>1985:</strong> Barker&#39;s TMS. <strong>1990s:</strong> antidepressant rTMS. <strong>2008:</strong> FDA clearance [1].</li>\n<li><strong>VNS:</strong> developed for epilepsy (1997 approval); mood improvement noted; FDA approval for chronic TRD in 2005.</li>\n<li><strong>DBS:</strong> from movement disorders. Mayberg (2005) reported sgACC (area 25) DBS in 6 TRD patients; Nuttin (1999) described capsular stimulation in OCD.</li>\n<li><strong>tDCS:</strong> galvanic stimulation of the 19th century, revived by Nitsche and Paulus (2000).</li>\n<li><strong>Psychosurgery:</strong> the discredited prefrontal leucotomy era (Moniz, Freeman) left a legacy of caution that still shapes ethics review for DBS and ablation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Epidemiology of use</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>About 30% of patients with depression do not respond to two antidepressant trials (STAR*D: cumulative remission about 67% after four steps). This is the population for which neuromodulation is considered.</li>\n<li>The IPS guideline notes that 20–60% of patients across psychiatric disorders do not respond to standard treatment, which drives interest in rTMS [1].</li>\n<li>ECT use in India is higher than in the West, often in acute psychosis and catatonia. Unmodified ECT was banned by the MHCA 2017.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Classification and criteria</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>By invasiveness:</strong> non-invasive (TMS, tDCS, tACS, transcutaneous VNS, focused ultrasound) vs invasive (DBS, implanted VNS, epidural cortical stimulation).</li>\n<li><strong>By seizure:</strong> convulsive (ECT, MST) vs non-convulsive.</li>\n<li><strong>By target strategy:</strong> scalp-based (5 cm rule, Beam F3), neuronavigated, connectivity-guided, closed-loop/responsive.</li>\n<li>Regulatory status differs. The FDA &quot;clears&quot; devices (510(k)) rather than approving them like drugs. Clearance for rTMS (MDD, OCD, smoking, anxious depression) differs from evidence grades in guidelines.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Mechanism in detail</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Technique</th>\n<th>Proposed mechanism</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>ECT</td>\n<td>Generalised seizure → increased GABA and seizure threshold (anticonvulsant theory); hippocampal neurogenesis and BDNF; HPA normalisation; increased functional connectivity changes in DMN; dopaminergic sensitisation (relevant to catatonia, Parkinson&#39;s)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>rTMS</td>\n<td>Frequency-dependent LTP/LTD; network propagation from DLPFC to sgACC; dopamine release in caudate; BDNF</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>tDCS</td>\n<td>Subthreshold: anodal depolarises (raises excitability), cathodal hyperpolarises; after-effects NMDA-dependent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>VNS</td>\n<td>Afferent vagal fibres → nucleus tractus solitarius → locus coeruleus (noradrenaline) and dorsal raphe (serotonin) → limbic regions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DBS</td>\n<td>High-frequency stimulation → functional inhibition of the local node and modulation of white-matter tracts (sgACC, VC/VS, MFB)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Clinical features in detail — indications beyond depression</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>OCD:</strong> rTMS (LF right DLPFC moderate; LF SMA, HF bilateral DLPFC low); deep TMS H7 coil FDA-cleared; DBS of VC/VS or STN for severe refractory OCD, with about 50–60% response in open series [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Schizophrenia:</strong> LF rTMS over left temporoparietal cortex for persistent auditory hallucinations (low grade); HF left DLPFC for negative symptoms (moderate); ECT augmentation of clozapine in clozapine-resistant illness; tDCS (fronto-temporal montage) for hallucinations is promising [1,2].</li>\n<li><strong>Catatonia:</strong> benzodiazepines then ECT; ECT is still the treatment of choice when lorazepam fails [2].</li>\n<li><strong>Substance use:</strong> HF left DLPFC rTMS reduces craving and smoking (low grade) [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Dementia:</strong> HF DLPFC rTMS improves global cognition and BPSD in Alzheimer&#39;s (moderate) but not in MCI [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Pain, tinnitus, migraine, insomnia:</strong> motor cortex rTMS for fibromyalgia and migraine; LF right DLPFC for insomnia [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Assessment in detail</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Treatment resistance staging:</strong> Thase–Rush staging, Maudsley Staging Method (MSM), Antidepressant Treatment History Form (ATHF).</li>\n<li><strong>Cognition:</strong> for ECT, baseline and serial MoCA plus an autobiographical memory measure (AMI-SF).</li>\n<li><strong>Pre-ECT:</strong> anaesthetic assessment, ECG, electrolytes, review of drugs that alter seizure threshold (benzodiazepines, anticonvulsants, lithium — neurotoxicity risk). Fundoscopy or CT only if raised ICP is suspected.</li>\n<li><strong>Pre-TMS:</strong> TASS/Rossi questionnaire, motor threshold [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Pre-DBS/VNS:</strong> neurosurgical and ethics committee review; MRI; neuropsychological testing.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Differential diagnosis of non-response to neuromodulation</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Cause</th>\n<th>Clue</th>\n<th>Action</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Pseudo-resistance</td>\n<td>Poor adherence, sub-therapeutic doses</td>\n<td>Optimise first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wrong diagnosis</td>\n<td>Bipolar depression, personality disorder, organic cause</td>\n<td>Re-evaluate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inadequate dose</td>\n<td>Low pulses or intensity, too few sessions, short ECT seizures</td>\n<td>Adjust parameters</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Drug interference</td>\n<td>Benzodiazepines, anticonvulsants raise seizure threshold and MT</td>\n<td>Reduce before treatment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ongoing stressors</td>\n<td>Psychosocial maintaining factors</td>\n<td>Add psychotherapy and social input</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Management details</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>ECT in special groups:</strong> safe in pregnancy (with obstetric monitoring), elderly (consider RUL ultrabrief), adolescents (MHCA 2017 restricts ECT in minors to consent of guardian plus Mental Health Review Board permission).</li>\n<li><strong>rTMS in pregnancy:</strong> minimal fetal exposure; a good option for antenatal depression [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Drug–ECT interactions:</strong> lithium (risk of delirium; consider holding), benzodiazepines (raise threshold; use flumazenil if needed), clozapine (ECT augmentation effective; monitor for tachycardia) [2].</li>\n<li><strong>Continuation:</strong> after successful ECT, continuation pharmacotherapy (lithium + nortriptyline in Sackeim 2001) or continuation ECT (CORE trial: comparable to drugs) is mandatory because relapse is high.</li>\n<li><strong>rTMS maintenance:</strong> tapered sessions for responders with relapse history; evidence still limited [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Evidence table</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Study</th>\n<th>Design</th>\n<th>n</th>\n<th>Main result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>UK ECT Review Group, 2003</td>\n<td>Meta-analysis</td>\n<td>73 RCTs</td>\n<td>ECT &gt; sham and &gt; drugs; bilateral &gt; unilateral</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kellner et al., CORE 2006</td>\n<td>RCT continuation ECT vs lithium+nortriptyline</td>\n<td>201</td>\n<td>Similar relapse (~37% vs 32%)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mutz et al., 2019</td>\n<td>Network meta-analysis</td>\n<td>113 trials (incl. ECT)</td>\n<td>rTMS effective; bitemporal ECT highest response</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Blumberger et al., 2018 (THREE-D)</td>\n<td>Non-inferiority RCT</td>\n<td>414</td>\n<td>iTBS = rTMS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Brunoni et al., 2017 (ELECT-TDCS)</td>\n<td>RCT</td>\n<td>245</td>\n<td>tDCS &gt; placebo, inferior to escitalopram</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Holtzheimer et al., 2017 (BROADEN)</td>\n<td>Sham-controlled DBS sgACC</td>\n<td>90</td>\n<td>No difference at 6 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dougherty et al., 2015 (RECLAIM)</td>\n<td>DBS VC/VS</td>\n<td>30</td>\n<td>No difference at 16 weeks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Aaronson et al., 2017</td>\n<td>VNS registry</td>\n<td>795</td>\n<td>Higher cumulative 5-year response than usual care</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cole et al., 2022 (SAINT)</td>\n<td>RCT</td>\n<td>29</td>\n<td>Remission 78.6% vs 13.3%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tikka et al., 2023 (IPS CPG)</td>\n<td>Umbrella review</td>\n<td>97 meta-analyses</td>\n<td>Grades across 15 disorders</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Critical appraisal</h3>\n<p><strong>Arguments supporting the statement</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Circuit-based, mechanism-informed treatment that bypasses drug side effects (weight gain, sexual dysfunction).</li>\n<li>rTMS is well tolerated (serious adverse events equal to sham) and safe in pregnancy and the elderly [1].</li>\n<li>Rapid-acting protocols (SAINT) and ECT can save lives in severe depression.</li>\n<li>Biomarkers (connectivity, EEG) promise personalised targeting.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Arguments against, or for caution</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Effect sizes outside depression are modest</strong> and come from small, heterogeneous trials. Many IPS recommendations are graded &quot;low&quot; [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Blinding:</strong> sham TMS and sham tDCS are imperfect; placebo response in rTMS trials is large (g ≈ 0.8) [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Durability:</strong> about half of rTMS responders relapse by 12 months; ECT relapse is about 50% at 6 months without continuation.</li>\n<li><strong>Invasive methods failed pivotal RCTs</strong> (BROADEN, RECLAIM). Open-label successes may reflect selection, expectation and programming adjustments.</li>\n<li><strong>Cognitive side effects of ECT</strong> and stigma limit acceptance. Ultrabrief RUL reduces but does not abolish them.</li>\n<li><strong>Access and cost:</strong> daily rTMS sessions, anaesthesia for ECT, and neurosurgery for DBS are concentrated in urban tertiary centres.</li>\n<li><strong>Publication bias and industry funding</strong> in device trials.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Verdict:</strong> &quot;Much promise&quot; is justified for depression and reasonable for OCD. For other disorders it is premature. The field&#39;s priority is replication with active sham, longer follow-up and cost-effectiveness data from low- and middle-income settings.</p>\n<h3>Indian context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>MHCA 2017, Section 95:</strong> prohibits ECT without muscle relaxant and anaesthesia (unmodified ECT). ECT in minors needs guardian consent and Mental Health Review Board permission. Advance directives may refuse ECT.</li>\n<li>IPS clinical practice guidelines exist for rTMS (2023) and ECT, and NIMHANS has a large neuromodulation programme (rTMS, tDCS, ECT research) [1].</li>\n<li>India has a large published trial base: a meta-analysis of 52 Indian studies showed moderate–large rTMS effects [1].</li>\n<li>Barriers: cost of devices, uneven training, rural access. Home-based tDCS and teleconsultation could widen reach, but evidence is still limited.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Recent advances</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Established:</strong> iTBS; deep TMS for OCD; ultrabrief RUL ECT.</li>\n<li><strong>Emerging:</strong> accelerated, connectivity-guided iTBS (SAINT/SNT); EEG-synchronised and closed-loop TMS; ketamine anaesthesia for ECT.</li>\n<li><strong>Investigational:</strong> transcranial focused ultrasound (deep targets non-invasively); adaptive (closed-loop) DBS using local field potential biomarkers; transcutaneous auricular VNS; temporal interference stimulation; MST.</li>\n<li><strong>Combination:</strong> pharmacological enhancement of plasticity (D-cycloserine with rTMS); psychotherapy paired with stimulation (e.g., exposure with deep TMS in OCD).</li>\n</ul>\n","refs":"<ol>\n<li>Tikka SK, Siddiqui MA, Garg S, Pattojoshi A, Gautam M. Clinical practice guidelines for the therapeutic use of repetitive transcranial magnetic stimulation in neuropsychiatric disorders. <em>Indian J Psychiatry</em> 2023; 65(2):270–288.</li>\n<li>Taylor DM, Barnes TRE, Young AH. <em>The Maudsley Prescribing Guidelines in Psychiatry</em>. 15th ed. Wiley, 2025; Chapter 1 (Electroconvulsive therapy and psychosis; Catatonia) and Chapter 3 (Treatment-resistant depression; Electroconvulsive therapy and psychotropic drugs).</li>\n<li>UK ECT Review Group. Efficacy and safety of electroconvulsive therapy in depressive disorders: a systematic review and meta-analysis. <em>Lancet</em> 2003; 361:799–808.</li>\n<li>Blumberger DM, et al. Effectiveness of theta burst versus high-frequency rTMS in patients with depression (THREE-D). <em>Lancet</em> 2018; 391:1683–1692.</li>\n<li>Brunoni AR, et al. Trial of electrical direct-current therapy versus escitalopram for depression. <em>N Engl J Med</em> 2017; 376:2523–2533.</li>\n<li>Holtzheimer PE, et al. Subcallosal cingulate deep brain stimulation for treatment-resistant depression: a multisite, randomised, sham-controlled trial. <em>Lancet Psychiatry</em> 2017; 4:839–849.</li>\n<li>Dougherty DD, et al. A randomized sham-controlled trial of deep brain stimulation of the ventral capsule/ventral striatum for chronic treatment-resistant depression. <em>Biol Psychiatry</em> 2015; 78:240–248.</li>\n<li>Kellner CH, et al. Continuation electroconvulsive therapy vs pharmacotherapy for relapse prevention in major depression (CORE). <em>Arch Gen Psychiatry</em> 2006; 63:1337–1344.</li>\n<li>Cole EJ, et al. Stanford Neuromodulation Therapy (SNT): a double-blind randomized controlled trial. <em>Am J Psychiatry</em> 2022; 179:132–141.</li>\n<li>Government of India. <em>The Mental Healthcare Act, 2017</em>; Section 95.</li>\n</ol>\n"},"newer-antipsychotics-harm-vs-help":{"title":"Newer antipsychotics — more harm than help? (critical evaluation)","status":"draft","updated":"2026-10-20","reviewer":[],"covers":["p1-ff12dcb5"],"exam":"<p><strong>Definition.</strong> &quot;Newer antipsychotics&quot; here means:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Second-generation (atypical) antipsychotics (SGAs)</strong> introduced from the 1990s: clozapine (re-introduced 1990), risperidone, olanzapine, quetiapine, ziprasidone, amisulpride, aripiprazole, paliperidone, asenapine, lurasidone, iloperidone.</li>\n<li><strong>Third-generation/novel agents:</strong> D2/D3 partial agonists (brexpiprazole, cariprazine), lumateperone, and non-D2 drugs such as the <strong>M1/M4 muscarinic agonist xanomeline–trospium</strong> (FDA approval 26 September 2024) [1].</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The question asks whether their gains (fewer extrapyramidal side effects, broader efficacy) are outweighed by harms (metabolic syndrome, cost, overuse).</p>\n<h3>Mechanism</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>FGAs:</strong> potent D2 blockade (&gt;80% occupancy) → antipsychotic effect, plus EPS, TD and hyperprolactinaemia.</li>\n<li><strong>SGAs:</strong> D2 blockade with 5-HT2A antagonism, fast D2 dissociation (Kapur &amp; Seeman), or partial agonism → fewer EPS. H1 and 5-HT2C blockade and muscarinic M3 effects → weight gain and insulin resistance (olanzapine, clozapine).</li>\n<li><strong>Muscarinic agonists:</strong> M4 agonism reduces presynaptic dopamine release in the associative striatum, sparing the sensorimotor striatum (so no EPS); M1 agonism improves cortical E/I balance and may help cognition. Trospium blocks peripheral cholinergic effects [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Benefits vs harms</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Domain</th>\n<th>Help</th>\n<th>Harm</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Efficacy</td>\n<td>Clozapine is uniquely effective in treatment-resistant schizophrenia (~40% respond) and reduces suicide (InterSePT); amisulpride, olanzapine and risperidone slightly more effective than many FGAs (Leucht 2009, Huhn 2019)</td>\n<td>Most SGAs no better than FGAs (CATIE, CUtLASS)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Neurological</td>\n<td>Fewer EPS, akathisia and TD (TD ~1%/year vs ~5%/year with FGAs)</td>\n<td>Not zero: risperidone EPS at higher doses; aripiprazole akathisia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metabolic</td>\n<td>Aripiprazole, lurasidone, ziprasidone, cariprazine and xanomeline–trospium are weight-neutral</td>\n<td>Olanzapine and clozapine: weight gain, type 2 diabetes, dyslipidaemia, metabolic syndrome in ~30–40%; higher cardiovascular mortality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Endocrine</td>\n<td>Aripiprazole and quetiapine are prolactin-sparing</td>\n<td>Risperidone, paliperidone and amisulpride cause marked hyperprolactinaemia (galactorrhoea, amenorrhoea, osteoporosis) [2]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cognition and negative symptoms</td>\n<td>Xanomeline–trospium improved negative symptoms (SMD −0.33) and cognition post hoc in impaired patients [1]; cariprazine for predominant negative symptoms</td>\n<td>Claimed cognitive advantage of SGAs was largely a trial artefact</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Other</td>\n<td>LAIs (paliperidone 6-monthly); mood stabilisation; bipolar depression (quetiapine, lurasidone)</td>\n<td>Off-label overuse (insomnia, dementia, personality disorder); stroke and death in dementia (black-box warning); QTc (ziprasidone); cost</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Assessment (before and during treatment)</h3>\n<p>Baseline weight, BMI, waist, BP, fasting glucose/HbA1c, lipids, prolactin, ECG; repeat at 12 weeks, then yearly. Use AIMS and BARS for movement disorders. Ask about sexual function and menstrual history.</p>\n<h3>Management principles</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Choose the drug by <strong>side-effect profile</strong>, matched to the patient (shared decision-making).</li>\n<li>Prefer metabolically benign drugs first-line in young and first-episode patients.</li>\n<li>Use clozapine early for treatment-resistant schizophrenia.</li>\n<li>Use the lowest effective dose and avoid polypharmacy.</li>\n<li>Co-prescribe metformin for antipsychotic-induced weight gain; add lifestyle interventions.</li>\n<li>Avoid off-label use without clear indication; deprescribe in dementia.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Key evidence</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Leucht 2009 (Lancet):</strong> only four SGAs (clozapine, amisulpride, olanzapine, risperidone) were more efficacious than FGAs; others were not.</li>\n<li><strong>CATIE 2005 (n=1,493)</strong> and <strong>CUtLASS 2006:</strong> no overall effectiveness advantage over perphenazine or FGAs; olanzapine had the longest time to discontinuation but the most weight gain.</li>\n<li><strong>Huhn 2019</strong> network meta-analysis of 32 antipsychotics: small efficacy differences, large side-effect differences.</li>\n<li><strong>Fabiano 2025</strong> meta-analysis of xanomeline–trospium (3 RCTs, n=690, 5 weeks): PANSS total SMD −0.56; response RR 2.13 (NNT 4); no EPS or akathisia; less weight gain (≥7% gain RR 0.46); more nausea (19% vs 4%), vomiting (14% vs 2%), constipation (17% vs 6%), dyspepsia, dry mouth, hypertension and tachycardia [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conclusion.</strong> &quot;More harm than help&quot; is <strong>too sweeping</strong>. The &quot;atypical&quot; class was oversold: most newer drugs are not more effective than older ones, and several cause serious metabolic harm. But clozapine&#39;s unique efficacy, the lower burden of EPS and TD, prolactin-sparing and weight-neutral options, and the first non-D2 mechanism (muscarinic agonism) are real gains. Harm comes mainly from <strong>indiscriminate choice and overuse</strong>, not from the drugs as a class. The answer is individualised prescribing with metabolic vigilance.</p>\n","depth":"<h3>Background</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>1952:</strong> chlorpromazine (Delay and Deniker). <strong>1958:</strong> haloperidol (Janssen).</li>\n<li><strong>1971:</strong> clozapine introduced; withdrawn in 1975 after agranulocytosis deaths in Finland; reintroduced in 1989–90 after Kane&#39;s 1988 trial in treatment-resistant schizophrenia.</li>\n<li><strong>1990s:</strong> risperidone (1993), olanzapine (1996), quetiapine (1997) were marketed as &quot;atypicals&quot; with claims of better efficacy, especially for negative and cognitive symptoms.</li>\n<li><strong>2000s:</strong> aripiprazole (2002), the first D2 partial agonist. Pragmatic trials (CATIE, CUtLASS, EUFEST) tempered enthusiasm. Tyrer and Kendall (2009) called the atypicals &quot;the spurious advance&quot;.</li>\n<li><strong>2010s–2020s:</strong> cariprazine, brexpiprazole, lumateperone; TAAR1 agonists failed in phase 3; xanomeline–trospium (KarXT) became the first non-D2 antipsychotic (2024) [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Epidemiology of use and harm</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>SGAs account for over 80% of antipsychotic prescriptions in most countries, India included. More than half of UK antipsychotic prescriptions are for non-psychotic indications (insomnia, anxiety, personality disorder, dementia) [2].</li>\n<li>Metabolic syndrome prevalence is about 30–35% in people with schizophrenia; antipsychotic-naïve first-episode patients already show some glucose dysregulation.</li>\n<li>The mortality gap is 15–20 years, mostly from cardiovascular disease. However, long-term antipsychotic use is associated with <strong>lower</strong> all-cause mortality than non-use in Finnish and Swedish cohorts (Tiihonen; clozapine lowest).</li>\n<li>In discontinuation trials, rapid stopping may exaggerate apparent benefit [2].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Classification</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Class</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n<th>Distinctive features</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>FGA high potency</td>\n<td>Haloperidol, fluphenazine</td>\n<td>EPS, prolactin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FGA low potency</td>\n<td>Chlorpromazine</td>\n<td>Sedation, anticholinergic, hypotension</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SGA — SDA (serotonin–dopamine antagonist)</td>\n<td>Risperidone, paliperidone, ziprasidone, lurasidone, iloperidone</td>\n<td>Prolactin (risperidone/paliperidone)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SGA — MARTA</td>\n<td>Clozapine, olanzapine, quetiapine, asenapine</td>\n<td>Metabolic, sedation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Substituted benzamides</td>\n<td>Amisulpride, sulpiride</td>\n<td>Prolactin; renally excreted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D2/D3 partial agonists</td>\n<td>Aripiprazole, brexpiprazole, cariprazine</td>\n<td>Akathisia; low metabolic risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Multimodal</td>\n<td>Lumateperone</td>\n<td>Low EPS and metabolic risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Muscarinic agonist</td>\n<td>Xanomeline–trospium</td>\n<td>No D2 blockade; cholinergic GI effects [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Neuroscience-based Nomenclature (NbN)</td>\n<td>Names by mechanism, not &quot;generation&quot;</td>\n<td>Preferred by ECNP and other bodies</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Mechanism in detail — why harms differ</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Receptor</th>\n<th>Effect of blockade</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>D2 (striatal)</td>\n<td>EPS, TD (supersensitivity)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D2 (tuberoinfundibular)</td>\n<td>Hyperprolactinaemia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>H1</td>\n<td>Sedation, appetite, weight gain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5-HT2C</td>\n<td>Appetite, weight gain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M3 (pancreatic β-cells)</td>\n<td>Impaired insulin secretion (olanzapine, clozapine)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>α1</td>\n<td>Postural hypotension</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M1 (central)</td>\n<td>Cognitive impairment, constipation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>hERG channel</td>\n<td>QTc prolongation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Xanomeline acts as an <strong>agonist</strong> at M1/M4; trospium (a quaternary amine that does not cross the BBB) blocks peripheral M2/M3, but nausea, vomiting, constipation, dyspepsia, raised heart rate and BP still occur [1].</p>\n<h3>Clinical features in detail — the harms</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Metabolic:</strong> weight gain (olanzapine and clozapine about 4–10 kg in the first year), insulin resistance, new-onset diabetes (including DKA early in treatment), dyslipidaemia, hypertension.</li>\n<li><strong>Cardiac:</strong> QTc prolongation; myocarditis and cardiomyopathy with clozapine.</li>\n<li><strong>Neurological:</strong> EPS, akathisia (aripiprazole, cariprazine), TD, NMS (rare), seizures (clozapine dose-related).</li>\n<li><strong>Endocrine and sexual:</strong> hyperprolactinaemia leads to galactorrhoea, amenorrhoea, infertility, low oestrogen and bone loss, especially in premenopausal women [2].</li>\n<li><strong>Haematological:</strong> clozapine neutropenia and agranulocytosis (~0.8%).</li>\n<li><strong>GI:</strong> clozapine hypomotility (potentially fatal ileus).</li>\n<li><strong>Dementia:</strong> about 1.6–1.7 times higher mortality and higher stroke risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Assessment in detail</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Metabolic monitoring:</strong> weight weekly for 6 weeks, then at 12 weeks and yearly; BP; HbA1c or fasting glucose and lipids at baseline, 12 weeks and yearly. Prioritise lifestyle and metformin when weight gain exceeds 5–7%.</li>\n<li><strong>Prolactin:</strong> at baseline and if symptomatic; DEXA in long-term hyperprolactinaemia [2].</li>\n<li><strong>ECG:</strong> at baseline when cardiac risk or a QT-prolonging drug is involved.</li>\n<li><strong>Rating scales:</strong> GASS, LUNSERS, AIMS, BARS, SAS.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Differential — choosing between agents (examples)</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Patient priority</th>\n<th>Reasonable choice</th>\n<th>Avoid</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Young, first episode, weight concern</td>\n<td>Aripiprazole, lurasidone, cariprazine</td>\n<td>Olanzapine, clozapine early</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Woman planning pregnancy, menstrual problems</td>\n<td>Aripiprazole, quetiapine</td>\n<td>Risperidone, amisulpride</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prominent EPS history</td>\n<td>Quetiapine, aripiprazole, clozapine</td>\n<td>Haloperidol, high-dose risperidone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Treatment resistance</td>\n<td><strong>Clozapine</strong></td>\n<td>Polypharmacy without clozapine trial</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Predominant negative symptoms</td>\n<td>Cariprazine; consider xanomeline–trospium where available</td>\n<td>High-dose D2 blockers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Elderly with dementia</td>\n<td>Non-drug first; lowest dose, short duration</td>\n<td>Long-term antipsychotics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Poor adherence</td>\n<td>LAI (aripiprazole, paliperidone)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Management details</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Weight gain:</strong> metformin 500 mg twice daily titrated to 1 g twice daily (prevents and reverses weight gain; recommended at initiation with high-risk drugs such as olanzapine or clozapine). Consider switching to aripiprazole, or adjunctive aripiprazole. GLP-1 agonists (semaglutide, liraglutide) for BMI ≥30 or ≥27 with risk factors.</li>\n<li><strong>Hyperprolactinaemia:</strong> reduce dose; switch to aripiprazole; or add aripiprazole 5 mg [2].</li>\n<li><strong>Akathisia:</strong> reduce dose; propranolol; mirtazapine 15 mg.</li>\n<li><strong>TD:</strong> VMAT2 inhibitors (valbenazine, deutetrabenazine); switch to clozapine.</li>\n<li><strong>Xanomeline–trospium practicalities:</strong> 50/20 mg twice daily → 100/20 mg → 125/30 mg twice daily, taken 1 hour before or 2 hours after food. Avoid in urinary retention, moderate–severe hepatic impairment, gastric retention, narrow-angle glaucoma. Monitor heart rate and BP [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Evidence table</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Study</th>\n<th>Design</th>\n<th>n</th>\n<th>Main finding</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Kane et al., 1988</td>\n<td>RCT clozapine vs chlorpromazine in TRS</td>\n<td>268</td>\n<td>30% vs 4% response</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lieberman et al., 2005 (CATIE)</td>\n<td>Pragmatic RCT</td>\n<td>1,493</td>\n<td>74% discontinued by 18 months; perphenazine comparable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Jones et al., 2006 (CUtLASS 1)</td>\n<td>RCT FGA vs SGA</td>\n<td>227</td>\n<td>No QoL advantage for SGAs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Leucht et al., 2009</td>\n<td>Meta-analysis</td>\n<td>150 trials</td>\n<td>Only 4 SGAs more efficacious than FGAs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Huhn et al., 2019</td>\n<td>Network meta-analysis</td>\n<td>402 trials, 53,463</td>\n<td>Small efficacy differences; big side-effect differences</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pillinger et al., 2020</td>\n<td>Network meta-analysis, metabolic</td>\n<td>100 trials</td>\n<td>Olanzapine/clozapine worst; aripiprazole, brexpiprazole, cariprazine, lurasidone best</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fabiano et al., 2025 [1]</td>\n<td>Meta-analysis, xanomeline–trospium</td>\n<td>3 RCTs, 690</td>\n<td>SMD −0.56; no EPS; cholinergic GI effects</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Taipale et al., 2020</td>\n<td>Swedish/Finnish cohorts</td>\n<td>&gt;60,000</td>\n<td>Clozapine and LAIs lowest mortality and rehospitalisation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Critical appraisal</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Harm side:</strong><ul>\n<li>Marketing exaggerated benefits; short trials with high-dose haloperidol comparators created a &quot;straw man&quot;.</li>\n<li>The metabolic epidemic.</li>\n<li>Expanding off-label use.</li>\n<li>High costs before generics.</li>\n<li>Industry funding of trials.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Help side:</strong><ul>\n<li>Clozapine is lifesaving.</li>\n<li>Less EPS and TD improves adherence and quality of life.</li>\n<li>Real-world cohorts show lower mortality on treatment than off it.</li>\n<li>Wider choice allows personalisation.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Xanomeline–trospium:</strong><ul>\n<li><strong>Promising but early</strong>: only 5-week trials, three studies of mostly Black and male US participants, all industry-funded.</li>\n<li>No head-to-head comparisons with standard antipsychotics.</li>\n<li>All-cause discontinuation was not better than placebo.</li>\n<li>An earlier network meta-analysis found the highest discontinuation risk with xanomeline–trospium [1].</li>\n<li>Long-term, adjunctive (ARISE trial negative) and real-world data are still limited.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>The &quot;generation&quot; label is unhelpful</strong>: drugs differ more within classes than between them. Prescribe by receptor profile, not by generation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Indian context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Generic SGAs are cheap in India (olanzapine and risperidone cost a few rupees per day), so cost no longer argues against them, but metabolic monitoring is often neglected.</li>\n<li>South Asians have higher cardiometabolic risk at lower BMI. Use lower BMI thresholds (overweight ≥23, obese ≥25) and a waist threshold of ≥90 cm in men and ≥80 cm in women.</li>\n<li>Olanzapine is widely prescribed first-line in India; this needs reconsideration in young and first-episode patients.</li>\n<li>Clozapine is under-used despite low cost; absolute neutrophil count (ANC) monitoring logistics are a barrier.</li>\n<li>Xanomeline–trospium is <strong>not approved in India</strong> at the time of writing (check current status).</li>\n<li>National List of Essential Medicines (2022): includes haloperidol, chlorpromazine, fluphenazine decanoate, risperidone, olanzapine and clozapine.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Recent advances</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Established:</strong> paliperidone 6-monthly LAI; aripiprazole 2-monthly; metformin co-prescription guidelines (2025 Schizophrenia Bulletin guideline: start metformin at initiation for high-risk antipsychotics such as olanzapine and clozapine, or with medium-risk drugs plus a risk factor).</li>\n<li><strong>Emerging:</strong> xanomeline–trospium (US 2024) [1]; emraclidine (M4 positive allosteric modulator) failed phase 2 in 2024.</li>\n<li><strong>Investigational:</strong> TAAR1 agonists (ulotaront failed phase 3); GlyT1 inhibitors; cannabidiol; personalised prescribing using pharmacogenomics (CYP2D6, CYP1A2).</li>\n</ul>\n","refs":"<ol>\n<li>Fabiano N, Wong S, Zhou C, Correll CU, Højlund M, Solmi M. Efficacy, tolerability, and safety of xanomeline-trospium chloride for schizophrenia: a systematic review and meta-analysis. <em>Eur Neuropsychopharmacol</em> 2025; 92:62–73.</li>\n<li>Taylor DM, Barnes TRE, Young AH. <em>The Maudsley Prescribing Guidelines in Psychiatry</em>. 15th ed. Wiley, 2025; Chapter 1 (Relative adverse effects; Antipsychotic-induced weight gain; Hyperprolactinaemia; First-generation antipsychotics – place in therapy; Stopping antipsychotics).</li>\n<li>Mu E, Gurvich C, Kulkarni J. Estrogen and psychosis — a review and future directions. <em>Arch Womens Ment Health</em> 2024; 27:877–885.</li>\n<li>Leucht S, Corves C, Arbter D, et al. Second-generation versus first-generation antipsychotic drugs for schizophrenia: a meta-analysis. <em>Lancet</em> 2009; 373:31–41.</li>\n<li>Lieberman JA, Stroup TS, McEvoy JP, et al. Effectiveness of antipsychotic drugs in patients with chronic schizophrenia (CATIE). <em>N Engl J Med</em> 2005; 353:1209–1223.</li>\n<li>Huhn M, Nikolakopoulou A, Schneider-Thoma J, et al. Comparative efficacy and tolerability of 32 oral antipsychotics for the acute treatment of adults with multi-episode schizophrenia. <em>Lancet</em> 2019; 394:939–951.</li>\n<li>Pillinger T, McCutcheon RA, Vano L, et al. Comparative effects of 18 antipsychotics on metabolic function in patients with schizophrenia. <em>Lancet Psychiatry</em> 2020; 7:64–77.</li>\n<li>Cooper SJ, Reynolds GP, et al. BAP guidelines on the management of weight gain, metabolic disturbances and cardiovascular risk associated with psychosis and antipsychotic drug treatment. <em>J Psychopharmacol</em> 2016; 30(8):717–748.</li>\n</ol>\n"},"parkinsons-disease-psychiatric-aspects":{"title":"Psychiatric manifestations of Parkinson's disease and their management","status":"draft","updated":"2026-10-15","reviewer":[],"covers":["p3-303f02bd","p3-fdf72d0c","p3-f4a09b39","p3-a0ea515e","p3-dc17344a","p3-868b6f08"],"exam":"<p><strong>Definition.</strong> Parkinson&#39;s disease (PD) is a progressive α-synucleinopathy in which neuropsychiatric syndromes — depression, anxiety, apathy, psychosis, impulse-control disorders, cognitive impairment and sleep disorders — are common, often precede the motor signs, and arise from three sources: the disease itself, its treatment (dopaminergic drugs, deep-brain stimulation), and the psychological response to a chronic disabling illness.</p>\n<h3>Mechanism</h3>\n<p><strong>Braak staging:</strong> α-synuclein pathology spreads in a predictable sequence.</p>\n<p>Stage 1–2: dorsal vagal nucleus, olfactory bulb, <strong>locus coeruleus (noradrenaline), raphe (serotonin)</strong> → hyposmia, constipation, RBD, depression, anxiety (prodromal)\n→ Stage 3–4: <strong>substantia nigra</strong>, basal forebrain, amygdala → motor syndrome, early emotional and cognitive change\n→ Stage 5–6: limbic and neocortical spread → dementia, psychosis.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dopamine loss</strong> (nigrostriatal, mesolimbic, mesocortical) → apathy, anhedonia, executive dysfunction.</li>\n<li><strong>Dopaminergic drugs</strong> over-stimulating the relatively preserved ventral striatum → impulse-control disorders, psychosis.</li>\n<li><strong>Noradrenaline and serotonin loss</strong> → depression, anxiety.</li>\n<li><strong>Cholinergic loss</strong> (nucleus basalis of Meynert) → dementia, visual hallucinations.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Clinical syndromes</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Syndrome</th>\n<th>Frequency</th>\n<th>Key features</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Depression</td>\n<td>MDD ~17%; clinically significant depressive symptoms ~35%</td>\n<td>Anhedonia, pessimism, less guilt than primary depression; may precede motor signs; mood dips in OFF periods</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Anxiety</td>\n<td>~31%</td>\n<td>GAD, panic, social anxiety, fear of falling; <strong>OFF-period panic</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Apathy</td>\n<td>~40%</td>\n<td>Loss of motivation <strong>without sadness</strong>; worse after agonist withdrawal or STN-DBS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Psychosis</td>\n<td>Hallucinations in up to 50% over the illness</td>\n<td>Minor phenomena (presence, passage, illusions) → well-formed visual hallucinations with insight → delusions (persecutory, jealousy) without insight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Impulse-control disorders</td>\n<td>~14%</td>\n<td>Gambling, hypersexuality, compulsive buying, binge eating; <strong>punding</strong>; <strong>dopamine dysregulation syndrome</strong>; linked to dopamine agonists</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cognitive impairment</td>\n<td>PD-MCI ~25%; dementia in ~80% after 20 years</td>\n<td>Executive, attention, visuospatial; retrieval more than storage memory deficit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sleep</td>\n<td>RBD ~40–50%</td>\n<td>Dream enactment; insomnia; daytime sleepiness and sleep attacks with agonists</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Iatrogenic syndromes</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dopamine agonist withdrawal syndrome (DAWS):</strong> anxiety, panic, dysphoria, pain and craving on tapering an agonist; does not respond to levodopa.</li>\n<li><strong>After STN-DBS:</strong> transient hypomania or impulsivity early; later apathy and depression as drugs are reduced; increased suicide risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Assessment</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>History from <strong>patient and caregiver</strong> (patients hide ICDs); relate each symptom to motor onset, drug changes and <strong>ON/OFF timing</strong>; ask about suicide.</li>\n<li>Scales: MDS-UPDRS Part I; GDS-15 or HADS (depression); Parkinson Anxiety Scale; Starkstein Apathy Scale; <strong>QUIP</strong> (impulse-control disorders); <strong>MoCA</strong> (cognition); SCOPA-PC (psychosis).</li>\n<li>Investigations to exclude delirium and mimics: CBC, sodium and electrolytes, renal and liver function, TSH, B12, glucose, urine; imaging, EEG or polysomnography when indicated.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Management</h3>\n<p><strong>General principles:</strong> optimise motor fluctuations first → rationalise drugs before adding new ones → choose psychotropics that do not worsen parkinsonism, falls or cognition → involve the neurologist and the family.</p>\n<p><strong>Depression</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>SSRIs (sertraline, escitalopram, paroxetine) or SNRI (venlafaxine) — effective without motor worsening in the <strong>SAD-PD</strong> trial.</li>\n<li>Nortriptyline effective but anticholinergic; pramipexole has an antidepressant effect but raises ICD risk.</li>\n<li><strong>CBT</strong> effective; <strong>ECT</strong> for severe, psychotic or suicidal depression (often transiently improves motor symptoms).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Anxiety:</strong> smooth dopaminergic delivery for OFF-period anxiety; SSRIs/SNRIs; CBT; avoid long-term benzodiazepines.</p>\n<p><strong>Apathy:</strong> exclude depression and dementia; optimise dopamine; rivastigmine; behavioural activation.</p>\n<p><strong>Psychosis — stepwise</strong></p>\n<p>Treat <strong>delirium</strong> precipitants (infection, dehydration, hyponatraemia, new drugs)\n→ withdraw drugs in this order: anticholinergics → amantadine → MAO-B inhibitors → dopamine agonists → COMT inhibitors → reduce levodopa last\n→ if still distressing: <strong>clozapine 6.25–50 mg/day</strong> (best evidence; blood monitoring) or <strong>pimavanserin 34 mg/day</strong> (5-HT2A inverse agonist); quetiapine widely used but weak evidence\n→ add <strong>rivastigmine</strong> if cognitive impairment.</p>\n<p><strong>Never</strong> use haloperidol, risperidone or olanzapine — they worsen parkinsonism and can cause severe sensitivity reactions.</p>\n<p><strong>Impulse-control disorders:</strong> <strong>reduce or stop the dopamine agonist</strong> (most effective step), taper slowly and watch for DAWS; compensate with levodopa; CBT, financial safeguards, spouse involvement; supervise levodopa supply in DDS.</p>\n<p><strong>Cognitive impairment:</strong> <strong>rivastigmine</strong> (EXPRESS trial) or donepezil; stop anticholinergics; treat vascular risk and sleep; capacity and driving assessment.</p>\n<p><strong>RBD:</strong> bedroom safety; melatonin first; clonazepam with caution.</p>\n<p><strong>DBS:</strong> psychiatric screening before surgery; monitor mood, apathy, impulsivity and suicide afterwards.</p>\n<h3>Key evidence</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SAD-PD (2012):</strong> paroxetine and venlafaxine XR improved depression in PD.</li>\n<li><strong>Parkinson Study Group (1999):</strong> low-dose clozapine improved psychosis without motor worsening.</li>\n<li><strong>Pimavanserin phase 3 (2014):</strong> improved PD psychosis; FDA approved 2016.</li>\n<li><strong>EXPRESS (2004):</strong> rivastigmine modestly improved cognition in PD dementia.</li>\n<li><strong>DOMINION (2010):</strong> ICDs in about 14%; dopamine agonists raise the odds 2–3.5-fold.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conclusion.</strong> Psychiatric syndromes are intrinsic to Parkinson&#39;s disease and its treatment, not incidental complications. Good management links each symptom to disease stage, motor fluctuations and medication, rationalises drugs before adding new ones, and uses motor-sparing treatments — SSRIs/SNRIs and CBT for depression, clozapine or pimavanserin for psychosis, agonist reduction for impulse-control disorders, and cholinesterase inhibitors for dementia.</p>\n","depth":"<h3>Background</h3>\n<p>James Parkinson&#39;s 1817 <em>Essay on the Shaking Palsy</em> described the senses and intellect as uninjured — a view now known to be wrong. Recognition of PD as a multisystem disorder with a long non-motor prodrome grew from Braak&#39;s neuropathological staging (2003) and from cohort studies showing that depression, anxiety, RBD, hyposmia and constipation often precede motor diagnosis by years. Non-motor symptoms are now recognised as the main determinants of quality of life, caregiver strain and institutional care, yet they are missed in a large proportion of routine neurology consultations.</p>\n<h3>Epidemiology</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>PD affects roughly 1% of people over 60 and over 6 million people worldwide (Global Burden of Disease 2016); numbers are rising with population ageing.</li>\n<li>In India, community prevalence estimates vary widely; the highest reported was among the Parsi community of Mumbai (Bharucha et al., 1988). Most Indian estimates are lower than Western figures, partly because of methodology and under-ascertainment.</li>\n<li>Sources of the frequencies in the exam answer: depression — Reijnders et al., 2008 (systematic review); anxiety — Broen et al., 2016 (meta-analysis); apathy — den Brok et al., 2015 (meta-analysis); ICDs — DOMINION, 2010; PD-MCI — Aarsland et al., 2010 (pooled analysis); dementia — Sydney multicentre study, Hely et al., 2008; psychosis — Forsaa et al., 2010 (cross-sectional prevalence 20–40%, cumulative up to ~60% over long follow-up).</li>\n<li>Risk factors for ICDs: younger age at onset, male sex (gambling, hypersexuality), personal or family history of addiction, novelty-seeking traits, depression, higher agonist dose.</li>\n<li>Risk factors for psychosis: older age, longer illness, cognitive impairment, visual impairment, RBD, higher dopaminergic load.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Classification and criteria</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>ICD-11</strong> codes these as <em>secondary</em> mental or behavioural syndromes (e.g. secondary mood syndrome, secondary psychotic syndrome) alongside the PD code. <strong>DSM-5-TR</strong> uses &quot;depressive disorder due to another medical condition&quot;, &quot;psychotic disorder due to another medical condition&quot;, and &quot;major or mild neurocognitive disorder due to Parkinson&#39;s disease&quot;.</li>\n<li><strong>PD psychosis criteria</strong> (NINDS/NIMH workgroup, Ravina et al., 2007): illusions, false sense of presence, hallucinations or delusions; onset after PD diagnosis; recurrent or continuous for at least one month; not better explained by delirium, schizophrenia, DLB or another cause.</li>\n<li><strong>One-year rule:</strong> dementia beginning more than a year after established parkinsonism = PD dementia; before or within a year = dementia with Lewy bodies. It is a clinical convention — both are Lewy body diseases.</li>\n<li><strong>MDS criteria</strong> exist for PD-MCI (Litvan et al., 2012) and PD dementia (Emre et al., 2007).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Mechanism in detail</h3>\n<p><strong>Neurotransmitter systems</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>System</th>\n<th>Lesion</th>\n<th>Psychiatric consequence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Dopamine (disease)</td>\n<td>Nigrostriatal, mesolimbic, mesocortical loss</td>\n<td>Apathy, anhedonia, executive dysfunction, OFF-period dysphoria</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dopamine (drugs)</td>\n<td>Preserved ventral striatum over-stimulated</td>\n<td>ICDs, punding, psychosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Noradrenaline</td>\n<td>Locus coeruleus</td>\n<td>Anxiety, depression, attention deficits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Serotonin</td>\n<td>Raphe nuclei</td>\n<td>Depression, anxiety</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Acetylcholine</td>\n<td>Nucleus basalis of Meynert, pedunculopontine nucleus</td>\n<td>Dementia, visual hallucinations, falls</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Visual hallucinations — Perception and Attention Deficit model (Collerton et al., 2005).</strong> Impaired visual processing (retinal dopamine loss, ventral visual stream dysfunction) combined with impaired attentional control (cholinergic deficit) allows an incorrect &quot;proto-object&quot; to be perceived as real; intrusion of REM-type imagery into wakefulness contributes. Dopaminergic drugs lower the threshold but are not the sole cause, which explains why hallucinations persist in some patients despite drug reduction.</p>\n<p><strong>Impulse-control disorders — the &quot;overdose hypothesis&quot;.</strong> The dose of dopaminergic drug needed to replace dopamine in the depleted dorsal striatum over-doses the relatively spared ventral striatum, which drives reward and motivation. Agonists with high D3 affinity (pramipexole, ropinirole) carry the highest risk; continuous, non-physiological stimulation and individual vulnerability (addiction traits) add to it.</p>\n<p><strong>Psychological factors.</strong> Loss of role and independence, visible disability, uncertainty about progression and stigma add to the biological vulnerability to depression and anxiety.</p>\n<h3>Clinical features in detail</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Depression:</strong> suicidal ideation is common; completed suicide is not clearly increased overall but is increased in the first years after STN-DBS. Irritability and pessimism are prominent; anhedonia overlaps with apathy.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-motor fluctuations:</strong> dysphoria, tearfulness, panic and pain can appear as each levodopa dose wears off and disappear when the next takes effect — ask the patient to keep a diary of symptoms against dose times.</li>\n<li><strong>Psychosis phenomenology:</strong> passage hallucinations are fleeting figures or animals in peripheral vision; presence hallucinations are a vivid sense that someone is nearby; pareidolia (faces in patterns) is common. Well-formed hallucinations are often silent people, children or animals, typically in the evening. <strong>Delusional jealousy (Othello syndrome)</strong> is characteristic. Auditory, tactile and olfactory hallucinations are less common. Psychosis predicts dementia, nursing-home placement and mortality.</li>\n<li><strong>Impulse-control and related behaviours:</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Behaviour</th>\n<th>Description</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Pathological gambling</td>\n<td>Including online gambling and lotteries; debt often hidden</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hypersexuality</td>\n<td>Increased demands, pornography, paraphilic behaviour, infidelity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compulsive buying</td>\n<td>Repeated unneeded purchases</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Binge eating</td>\n<td>Weight gain, nocturnal eating, sweet craving</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Punding</td>\n<td>Repetitive, purposeless handling of objects — sorting, dismantling, collecting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hobbyism, walkabout</td>\n<td>Excessive hobbies; aimless wandering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dopamine dysregulation syndrome</td>\n<td>Compulsive overuse of levodopa or apomorphine beyond motor need, with hypomania, irritability and denial</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cognition:</strong> posterior cortical deficits (semantic fluency, figure copying) predict progression to dementia better than frontal-executive deficits (CamPaIGN cohort). PD dementia brings fluctuating attention, hallucinations and apathy.</li>\n<li><strong>RBD:</strong> isolated RBD converts to a synucleinopathy at about 6% per year and in over 70% by 12 years (Postuma et al., 2019) — a key prodromal marker.</li>\n<li><strong>DAWS:</strong> occurs in roughly 15–20% of patients tapering an agonist; symptoms resemble drug withdrawal in addiction and may persist for months, sometimes forcing a slower taper.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Assessment in detail</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Domain</th>\n<th>Instrument</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Global non-motor</td>\n<td>MDS-UPDRS Part I; Non-Motor Symptoms Scale/Questionnaire</td>\n<td>Quick screen across domains</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Depression</td>\n<td>GDS-15, HADS, BDI, HAM-D</td>\n<td>Use PD-validated cut-offs; somatic items inflate scores</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Anxiety</td>\n<td>Parkinson Anxiety Scale</td>\n<td>Captures episodic and avoidance anxiety</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Apathy</td>\n<td>Starkstein Apathy Scale, Lille Apathy Rating Scale</td>\n<td>Separate from depression</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Psychosis</td>\n<td>SAPS-PD, SCOPA-PC, NPI</td>\n<td>Include minor phenomena</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ICDs</td>\n<td>QUIP / QUIP-RS</td>\n<td>Completed by patient <strong>and</strong> informant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cognition</td>\n<td>MoCA, SCOPA-COG, ACE-III</td>\n<td>MoCA more sensitive than MMSE for PD-MCI</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sleep</td>\n<td>RBD Screening Questionnaire, Epworth Sleepiness Scale</td>\n<td>Polysomnography to confirm RBD</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Examine in a known motor state (ON or OFF) and note it; check orthostatic BP (falls, syncope with psychotropics) and vision (hallucination risk). Investigate rapidly progressive or atypical cognitive decline with neuroimaging; use EEG when fluctuating consciousness suggests seizures or delirium.</p>\n<h3>Differential diagnosis</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Presentation</th>\n<th>Consider</th>\n<th>Distinguishing point</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Low mood and slowing</td>\n<td>Apathy; hypomimia/bradyphrenia; hypothyroidism</td>\n<td>Apathy lacks sadness; motor signs without negative cognitions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fluctuating confusion + hallucinations</td>\n<td>Delirium</td>\n<td>Acute onset, inattention, identifiable precipitant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Early dementia + hallucinations</td>\n<td>Dementia with Lewy bodies</td>\n<td>Dementia before or within one year of parkinsonism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Parkinsonism with early falls, gaze palsy, autonomic failure</td>\n<td>PSP, MSA</td>\n<td>Poor levodopa response, red flags</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Parkinsonism in a patient on antipsychotics</td>\n<td>Drug-induced parkinsonism</td>\n<td>Symmetric onset, temporal link to D2 blocker, normal DAT-SPECT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Episodic anxiety or dysphoria</td>\n<td>Non-motor OFF fluctuation</td>\n<td>Linked to dosing timetable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spending, hypersexuality</td>\n<td>Mania; frontotemporal dementia</td>\n<td>Temporal link to agonist; resolves on withdrawal</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Management details</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Antidepressant cautions:</strong> serotonin syndrome with SSRIs plus MAO-B inhibitors is rare at usual doses but monitor; SSRIs can occasionally worsen tremor or restless legs; check sodium in the elderly (SIADH); mirtazapine helps insomnia and appetite but sedates; bupropion may help fatigue and apathy but can cause agitation.</li>\n<li><strong>ECT:</strong> risk of post-ECT delirium in patients with cognitive impairment; motor improvement is usually temporary.</li>\n<li><strong>rTMS:</strong> modest antidepressant effect in trials.</li>\n<li><strong>Clozapine practicalities:</strong> start 6.25–12.5 mg at night; most patients respond below 50 mg; blood-count monitoring as per local protocol; watch sedation, orthostasis and sialorrhoea.</li>\n<li><strong>Pimavanserin:</strong> no D2 blockade; onset over several weeks; QT prolongation; not widely available in India.</li>\n<li><strong>Quetiapine:</strong> 12.5–150 mg/day; several RCTs failed to separate from placebo; sedation and orthostasis.</li>\n<li><strong>Avoid in all Lewy body disease:</strong> typical antipsychotics, risperidone, olanzapine, aripiprazole — motor worsening and potentially fatal neuroleptic sensitivity reactions.</li>\n<li><strong>ICDs:</strong> amantadine has conflicting evidence and may itself be associated with ICDs; naltrexone is unproven; CBT has RCT support (Okai et al., 2013).</li>\n<li><strong>Rivastigmine:</strong> can transiently worsen tremor; nausea and weight loss; patch formulation better tolerated. Memantine has smaller, inconsistent effects.</li>\n<li><strong>RBD drugs:</strong> melatonin 3–12 mg at night; clonazepam 0.25–1 mg (caution: falls, sleep apnoea, cognition).</li>\n<li><strong>Insomnia:</strong> treat nocturnal akinesia, nocturia and pain; CBT-I.</li>\n<li><strong>Palliative care:</strong> advance care planning while capacity is intact; caregiver respite and support groups, especially with psychosis, ICDs or dementia.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Evidence table</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Study</th>\n<th>Design</th>\n<th>Main finding</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Reijnders et al., 2008</td>\n<td>Systematic review</td>\n<td>MDD ~17%; clinically significant depressive symptoms ~35%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SAD-PD (Richard et al., 2012)</td>\n<td>RCT, n = 115</td>\n<td>Paroxetine and venlafaxine XR improved depression; no motor worsening</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Menza et al., 2009</td>\n<td>RCT, n = 52</td>\n<td>Nortriptyline superior to placebo; paroxetine CR not</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Barone et al., 2010</td>\n<td>RCT, n ≈ 300</td>\n<td>Pramipexole improved depressive symptoms vs placebo</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dobkin et al., 2011</td>\n<td>RCT, n = 80</td>\n<td>CBT superior to clinical monitoring for depression</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Parkinson Study Group, 1999</td>\n<td>RCT, n = 60</td>\n<td>Low-dose clozapine improved psychosis; no motor worsening</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cummings et al., 2014</td>\n<td>Phase 3 RCT, n = 199</td>\n<td>Pimavanserin improved PD psychosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>EXPRESS (Emre et al., 2004)</td>\n<td>RCT, n = 541</td>\n<td>Rivastigmine modestly improved cognition in PD dementia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DOMINION (Weintraub et al., 2010)</td>\n<td>Cross-sectional, n = 3,090</td>\n<td>ICDs in 13.6%; agonists raised odds 2–3.5-fold</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Devos et al., 2014</td>\n<td>RCT</td>\n<td>Rivastigmine improved apathy in non-demented, non-depressed patients</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hely et al., 2008 (Sydney)</td>\n<td>20-year cohort</td>\n<td>~80% of survivors developed dementia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Postuma et al., 2019</td>\n<td>Multicentre cohort, n = 1,280</td>\n<td>Isolated RBD converted at ~6% per year</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Critical appraisal</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Under-recognition:</strong> structured screening substantially increases detection compared with routine consultation.</li>\n<li><strong>Measurement problem:</strong> depression scales with somatic items inflate scores in PD; PD-validated cut-offs or core mood items are needed.</li>\n<li><strong>Thin evidence base:</strong> antidepressant evidence rests on a few small, short RCTs with high placebo response.</li>\n<li><strong>Quetiapine paradox:</strong> the most widely used antipsychotic for PD psychosis has the weakest trial support, while clozapine — the best supported — is underused because of monitoring burden.</li>\n<li><strong>Pimavanserin:</strong> approval rested on one positive phase 3 trial; questions remain about effect size, QT prolongation and the mortality warning shared by all antipsychotics in elderly patients with dementia.</li>\n<li><strong>Agonist trade-off:</strong> dopamine agonists delay levodopa motor complications at a behavioural cost that often falls on families; informed consent about ICD risk before prescribing is a clinical and ethical duty.</li>\n<li><strong>DBS:</strong> excellent motor results can coexist with poor psychosocial adjustment, showing the need for psychiatric input in selection and follow-up.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Indian context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Most patients are managed by neurologists or physicians; psychiatric syndromes are often not screened for, and families may attribute behaviour change to ageing, medication &quot;weakness&quot; or character.</li>\n<li><strong>Cost and access:</strong> clozapine needs reliable laboratory follow-up; pimavanserin is not widely available; generic SSRIs, rivastigmine and levodopa are affordable.</li>\n<li>ICD consequences (gambling, debt, sexual behaviour) carry particular stigma and marital consequences; enquire sensitively with the spouse.</li>\n<li><strong>Mental Healthcare Act 2017:</strong> capacity is decision-specific — a patient with PD dementia may retain capacity for some decisions; advance directives and nominated representatives are useful before cognition declines; admission for severe psychosis or suicidality follows MHCA provisions.</li>\n<li><strong>RPwD Act 2016:</strong> Parkinson&#39;s disease is listed among chronic neurological conditions for disability certification and benefits.</li>\n<li>DBS is available at a limited number of tertiary centres; psychiatric screening should be built into these programmes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Recent advances</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>α-synuclein seed amplification assays</strong> (CSF, skin) detect pathological synuclein with high accuracy, including in prodromal disease (Siderowf et al., 2023), leading to proposed <strong>biological staging</strong> of PD (2024).</li>\n<li><strong>Pimavanserin</strong> as a non-dopaminergic antipsychotic (established in the US; limited availability elsewhere).</li>\n<li><strong>Telehealth and caregiver-inclusive CBT</strong> for depression and anxiety.</li>\n<li><strong>Continuous dopaminergic delivery</strong> — levodopa–carbidopa intestinal gel, subcutaneous foslevodopa–foscarbidopa, apomorphine infusion — smooths fluctuations and may reduce OFF-period anxiety and dysphoria.</li>\n<li><strong>rTMS and other neuromodulation</strong> for depression in PD (modest evidence).</li>\n<li><strong>Prodromal trials</strong> of potential disease-modifying agents in people with isolated RBD.</li>\n</ul>\n","refs":"<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Draft note:</strong> written from the author&#39;s knowledge of the literature, without uploaded source chapters. Check every figure and dose against the sources below before marking this answer <code>reviewed</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<ol>\n<li>Weintraub D, Aarsland D, Biundo R, et al. Management of psychiatric and cognitive complications in Parkinson&#39;s disease. <em>BMJ</em>, 2022;379:e068718.</li>\n<li>Reijnders JS, Ehrt U, Weber WE, Aarsland D, Leentjens AF. A systematic review of prevalence studies of depression in Parkinson&#39;s disease. <em>Movement Disorders</em>, 2008;23:183–189.</li>\n<li>Broen MP, Narayen NE, Kuijf ML, et al. Prevalence of anxiety in Parkinson&#39;s disease: a systematic review and meta-analysis. <em>Movement Disorders</em>, 2016;31:1125–1133.</li>\n<li>den Brok MG, van Dalen JW, van Gool WA, et al. Apathy in Parkinson&#39;s disease: a systematic review and meta-analysis. <em>Movement Disorders</em>, 2015;30:759–769.</li>\n<li>Ravina B, Marder K, Fernandez HH, et al. Diagnostic criteria for psychosis in Parkinson&#39;s disease: report of an NINDS/NIMH work group. <em>Movement Disorders</em>, 2007;22:1061–1068.</li>\n<li>Weintraub D, Koester J, Potenza MN, et al. Impulse control disorders in Parkinson disease: a cross-sectional study of 3090 patients (DOMINION). <em>Archives of Neurology</em>, 2010;67:589–595.</li>\n<li>Richard IH, McDermott MP, Kurlan R, et al. A randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial of antidepressants in Parkinson disease (SAD-PD). <em>Neurology</em>, 2012;78:1229–1236.</li>\n<li>Menza M, Dobkin RD, Marin H, et al. A controlled trial of antidepressants in patients with Parkinson disease and depression. <em>Neurology</em>, 2009;72:886–892.</li>\n<li>Barone P, Poewe W, Albrecht S, et al. Pramipexole for the treatment of depressive symptoms in patients with Parkinson&#39;s disease. <em>Lancet Neurology</em>, 2010;9:573–580.</li>\n<li>Dobkin RD, Menza M, Allen LA, et al. Cognitive-behavioral therapy for depression in Parkinson&#39;s disease: a randomized, controlled trial. <em>American Journal of Psychiatry</em>, 2011;168:1066–1074.</li>\n<li>Parkinson Study Group. Low-dose clozapine for the treatment of drug-induced psychosis in Parkinson&#39;s disease. <em>New England Journal of Medicine</em>, 1999;340:757–763.</li>\n<li>Cummings J, Isaacson S, Mills R, et al. Pimavanserin for patients with Parkinson&#39;s disease psychosis. <em>Lancet</em>, 2014;383:533–540.</li>\n<li>Emre M, Aarsland D, Albanese A, et al. Rivastigmine for dementia associated with Parkinson&#39;s disease (EXPRESS). <em>New England Journal of Medicine</em>, 2004;351:2509–2518.</li>\n<li>Devos D, Moreau C, Maltête D, et al. Rivastigmine in apathetic but dementia and depression-free patients with Parkinson&#39;s disease. <em>Journal of Neurology, Neurosurgery and Psychiatry</em>, 2014;85:668–674.</li>\n<li>Hely MA, Reid WG, Adena MA, et al. The Sydney multicenter study of Parkinson&#39;s disease: the inevitability of dementia at 20 years. <em>Movement Disorders</em>, 2008;23:837–844.</li>\n<li>Postuma RB, Iranzo A, Hu M, et al. Risk and predictors of dementia and parkinsonism in idiopathic REM sleep behaviour disorder. <em>Brain</em>, 2019;142:744–759.</li>\n<li>Braak H, Del Tredici K, Rüb U, et al. Staging of brain pathology related to sporadic Parkinson&#39;s disease. <em>Neurobiology of Aging</em>, 2003;24:197–211.</li>\n<li>Collerton D, Perry E, McKeith I. Why people see things that are not there: a novel Perception and Attention Deficit model for recurrent complex visual hallucinations. <em>Behavioral and Brain Sciences</em>, 2005;28:737–757.</li>\n<li>Siderowf A, Concha-Marambio L, Lafontant DE, et al. Assessment of heterogeneity among participants in the Parkinson&#39;s Progression Markers Initiative cohort using α-synuclein seed amplification. <em>Lancet Neurology</em>, 2023;22:407–417.</li>\n<li>Bharucha NE, Bharucha EP, Bharucha AE, et al. Prevalence of Parkinson&#39;s disease in the Parsi community of Bombay, India. <em>Archives of Neurology</em>, 1988;45:1321–1323.</li>\n<li>David AS, Fleminger S, Kopelman MD, et al. (eds). <em>Lishman&#39;s Organic Psychiatry</em>, 4th edition — chapter on movement disorders.</li>\n<li>Government of India. Mental Healthcare Act, 2017; Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, 2016.</li>\n</ol>\n"},"psychotropic-induced-metabolic-syndrome":{"title":"Assessment and management of psychotropic-induced metabolic syndrome","status":"draft","updated":"2026-10-20","reviewer":[],"covers":["p1-c764876f"],"exam":"<p><strong>Definition.</strong> Metabolic syndrome is a cluster of cardiometabolic risk factors: central obesity, raised triglycerides, low HDL, hypertension and dysglycaemia. Together they raise the risk of type 2 diabetes about fivefold and cardiovascular disease about twofold. <strong>Psychotropic-induced</strong> metabolic syndrome is caused or worsened by medication, mainly antipsychotics (especially olanzapine and clozapine), but also valproate, lithium, mirtazapine and some TCAs. On top of the illness-related risk in psychosis (smoking, inactivity, diet, possible intrinsic insulin resistance), it drives the 15–20-year mortality gap [1].</p>\n<p><strong>Criteria (IDF 2005 / harmonised 2009, with South Asian cut-offs):</strong> waist ≥90 cm in men or ≥80 cm in women, <strong>plus</strong> any two of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>triglycerides ≥150 mg/dL (1.7 mmol/L) or on treatment;</li>\n<li>HDL &lt;40 mg/dL in men or &lt;50 mg/dL in women;</li>\n<li>BP ≥130/85 mmHg or on treatment;</li>\n<li>fasting glucose ≥100 mg/dL (5.6 mmol/L) or diagnosed diabetes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(NCEP ATP-III: any three of five, with waist &gt;102/88 cm.)</p>\n<h3>Mechanism</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Receptor effects:</strong> H1 and 5-HT2C blockade → increased appetite and reduced satiety (hypothalamic AMPK, leptin resistance) → weight gain.</li>\n<li><strong>Direct metabolic effects:</strong> M3 blockade on β-cells → reduced insulin secretion; direct hepatic and muscle insulin resistance, dyslipidaemia (raised triglycerides) — <strong>can occur before weight gain</strong> (olanzapine and clozapine can cause DKA early).</li>\n<li><strong>Other contributors:</strong> sedation and inactivity; illness factors (negative symptoms, poor diet, smoking, alcohol, poverty) [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Liability by drug</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Risk</th>\n<th>Antipsychotics</th>\n<th>Other psychotropics</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Clozapine, olanzapine</td>\n<td>Valproate, mirtazapine, lithium (weight), TCAs (amitriptyline)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Quetiapine, risperidone, paliperidone, iloperidone, asenapine</td>\n<td>Pregabalin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Aripiprazole, brexpiprazole, cariprazine, lurasidone, ziprasidone, amisulpride, haloperidol, xanomeline–trospium</td>\n<td>Lamotrigine, bupropion, SSRIs (most; paroxetine more)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Sources: BAP 2016 clusters [1]; xanomeline–trospium [3].</p>\n<p>8 out of 10 patients gain weight on antipsychotics, and drug choice is the strongest baseline predictor. Low and normal BMI individuals gain the most [2].</p>\n<h3>Assessment (monitoring schedule)</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Parameter</th>\n<th>Baseline</th>\n<th>Follow-up</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Personal and family history of diabetes and CVD, smoking, alcohol, diet, activity</td>\n<td>✓</td>\n<td>Yearly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weight, BMI</td>\n<td>✓</td>\n<td>Weekly for 6 weeks, at 12 weeks, then 3-monthly in year 1, then yearly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Waist circumference</td>\n<td>✓</td>\n<td>12 weeks, yearly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>BP, pulse</td>\n<td>✓</td>\n<td>12 weeks, yearly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fasting glucose / HbA1c</td>\n<td>✓</td>\n<td>12 weeks, yearly (more often if high risk)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fasting lipids</td>\n<td>✓</td>\n<td>12 weeks, yearly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10-year CVD risk (QRISK3, WHO/ISH charts)</td>\n<td>✓</td>\n<td>Yearly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Also: prolactin, ECG, LFT, renal function (before metformin/lithium)</td>\n<td>✓</td>\n<td>As indicated</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>A gain of more than 7% from baseline weight is clinically significant [1].</p>\n<h3>Management (stepwise)</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Prevention at initiation:</strong> choose a low-risk drug, especially in young and first-episode patients; give lifestyle advice from day one.</li>\n<li><strong>Lifestyle:</strong> dietary advice plus exercise (150 minutes/week); structured behavioural programmes reduce weight by about 3 kg vs control, and benefits fade without booster sessions [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Review the drug:</strong> lower the dose; <strong>switch</strong> to a lower-liability drug (aripiprazole, lurasidone, amisulpride, haloperidol), weighing relapse risk [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Metformin:</strong> with or after food; 500 mg once daily → 500 mg twice daily (week 2) → 1 g + 500 mg (week 4) → 1 g twice daily (week 6). Target 1–2 g/day; maximum 1 g/day if eGFR 30–44. <strong>Co-commence</strong> with olanzapine or clozapine, or with medium-risk drugs plus a risk factor (age 10–25, BMI &gt;25, pre-diabetes, hypertension, dyslipidaemia, diabetes). Start in anyone gaining &gt;3% in the first year [2]. It reverses about 3 kg of weight gain vs placebo [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Adjunctive aripiprazole</strong> with clozapine or olanzapine: about 2 kg weight loss [1].</li>\n<li><strong>GLP-1 receptor agonists</strong> (liraglutide, semaglutide, exenatide) if BMI ≥30, or 27–30 with a comorbidity, or if metformin is unavailable or ineffective [2].</li>\n<li><strong>Treat each component:</strong> statins, antihypertensives, diabetes treatment per standard guidelines; smoking cessation (varenicline, NRT, bupropion); alcohol interventions [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Bariatric surgery</strong> in extreme cases [1].</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Key evidence</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pillinger 2020</strong> network meta-analysis: clozapine and olanzapine have the worst metabolic effects; aripiprazole, brexpiprazole, cariprazine and lurasidone the least.</li>\n<li><strong>BAP 2016:</strong> lifestyle programmes about −3 kg; metformin about −3 kg; adjunctive aripiprazole about −2 kg; switching can help; topiramate −2.8 kg but cognitive side effects limit use [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Schizophrenia Bulletin 2025 metformin guideline:</strong> co-commence metformin with high-risk antipsychotics [2].</li>\n<li><strong>STEP-type trials:</strong> semaglutide and liraglutide reduce clozapine- and olanzapine-induced weight gain (liraglutide RCT, Larsen 2017: −5.3 kg).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conclusion.</strong> Psychotropic-induced metabolic syndrome is common, predictable and largely preventable. It must be anticipated at the time of prescribing, monitored systematically, and treated early with lifestyle change, drug review, metformin and, where needed, GLP-1 agonists, while the underlying illness stays controlled. Physical health monitoring is part of psychiatric treatment, not an optional add-on.</p>\n","depth":"<h3>Background</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Reaven (1988) described &quot;syndrome X&quot; (insulin resistance). NCEP ATP-III (2001) and IDF (2005) defined criteria; the 2009 harmonised definition followed.</li>\n<li>Diabetes in psychosis was noted before antipsychotics (Maudsley, 1879: &quot;diabetes is a disease which shows itself in families in which insanity prevails&quot;). Interest surged after clozapine and olanzapine.</li>\n<li>Consensus statements followed: ADA/APA (2004); Maudsley guidance; the BAP guidelines (2011, updated 2016); NICE CG178 (2014); the 2025 metformin guideline (Schizophrenia Bulletin) [1,2].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Epidemiology</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Metabolic syndrome affects about 30–35% of people with schizophrenia (about 10% at first episode, rising quickly with treatment) and about 30% with bipolar disorder.</li>\n<li>Diabetes is 2–3 times more common than in the general population.</li>\n<li>About 8 in 10 people gain weight on antipsychotics [2].</li>\n<li>Smoking: about 60–65% of people with schizophrenia smoke; quit rates are about half those of the general population [1].</li>\n<li>Indian studies: metabolic syndrome in about 25–45% of patients on antipsychotics (AIIMS, PGIMER and NIMHANS data). South Asians develop diabetes at a lower BMI.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Classification and criteria</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Criterion</th>\n<th>IDF/harmonised (South Asian)</th>\n<th>NCEP ATP-III</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Waist</td>\n<td>≥90 cm (M), ≥80 cm (F) — mandatory in IDF</td>\n<td>&gt;102 (M), &gt;88 cm (F)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Triglycerides</td>\n<td>≥150 mg/dL</td>\n<td>≥150 mg/dL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HDL</td>\n<td>&lt;40 (M), &lt;50 (F) mg/dL</td>\n<td>Same</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>BP</td>\n<td>≥130/85</td>\n<td>Same</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fasting glucose</td>\n<td>≥100 mg/dL</td>\n<td>≥110 (original) / ≥100 (revised)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Asian BMI thresholds: overweight ≥23, obese ≥25 kg/m² (WHO expert consultation 2004; Indian consensus).</p>\n<h3>Mechanism in detail</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pathway</th>\n<th>Detail</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>H1 antagonism</td>\n<td>Hypothalamic AMPK activation → hunger; strongest correlate of weight gain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5-HT2C antagonism</td>\n<td>Reduced POMC signalling → reduced satiety; HTR2C −759C/T polymorphism modulates risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M3 antagonism</td>\n<td>Impaired glucose-stimulated insulin release</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D2 antagonism</td>\n<td>Reward/feeding effects; prolactin → insulin resistance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Direct tissue effects</td>\n<td>Olanzapine reduces insulin sensitivity in muscle and liver within days; SREBP activation → lipogenesis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Illness-related</td>\n<td>Negative symptoms, sedentary lifestyle, poor diet, smoking, poverty, stress (HPA), possible intrinsic dysglycaemia</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Clinical features in detail</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Weight gain is fastest in the first 6–12 weeks and may plateau after 1–2 years (olanzapine continues to rise for longer).</li>\n<li>Dyslipidaemia (especially triglycerides) and hyperglycaemia can appear early. Diabetic ketoacidosis may occur within months with clozapine or olanzapine.</li>\n<li>Children and adolescents gain more weight (younger age is a risk factor) [2].</li>\n<li>Weight gain causes non-adherence, low self-esteem and stigma [1].</li>\n<li>Complications: NAFLD, obstructive sleep apnoea, hypertension, coronary disease, stroke, osteoarthritis.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Assessment in detail</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Risk scores:</strong> QRISK3 (includes severe mental illness and antipsychotic use as variables); WHO/ISH charts or the Indian (ICMR) CVD risk approach.</li>\n<li><strong>Tests:</strong> HbA1c (unaffected by fasting; useful when adherence to fasting is uncertain); fasting glucose; OGTT if borderline; fasting lipid profile; LFT (NAFLD); TSH (hypothyroidism, e.g., with lithium); renal function (before metformin); vitamin B12 (on metformin; yearly) [2].</li>\n<li><strong>Who monitors?</strong> Often unclear between psychiatry and primary care, which is one reason monitoring is poor [1]. Use a shared care protocol with a named responsible clinician.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Differential — causes of weight gain or metabolic change in a psychiatric patient</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Cause</th>\n<th>Clue</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Antipsychotic or mood stabiliser</td>\n<td>Temporal relation; drug liability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hypothyroidism</td>\n<td>Lithium; fatigue, TSH raised</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cushing&#39;s syndrome / steroid use</td>\n<td>Striae, proximal myopathy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Depression with hyperphagia / atypical depression</td>\n<td>Mood, appetite, hypersomnia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Binge eating disorder</td>\n<td>Loss-of-control eating episodes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alcohol misuse</td>\n<td>High triglycerides, raised GGT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PCOS (valproate)</td>\n<td>Hirsutism, menstrual irregularity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Smoking cessation</td>\n<td>Weight gain after quitting; raised clozapine levels</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Management details</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Metformin cautions:</strong> avoid in eGFR &lt;30, acute metabolic acidosis, hepatic insufficiency, acute illness, heavy alcohol use. Stop before iodinated contrast and restart ≥48 hours later. &quot;Sick day rule&quot;: stop if dehydrated or systemically unwell. Stop if BMI &lt;20. Side effects: GI (&lt;1 in 10), B12 deficiency (about 7% over 4 years), taste disturbance, rare lactic acidosis [1,2].</li>\n<li><strong>GLP-1 agonists:</strong> semaglutide 0.25 → 2.4 mg weekly; GI side effects. They may reduce alcohol use. Watch for pancreatitis and gallbladder disease.</li>\n<li><strong>Statins:</strong> per CVD risk (atorvastatin 20 mg for primary prevention).</li>\n<li><strong>Antihypertensives:</strong> ACE inhibitors or ARBs (check interaction with lithium, which raises lithium levels).</li>\n<li><strong>Switching:</strong> cross-taper over 2–4 weeks; switching off clozapine is generally inadvisable in treatment-resistant schizophrenia, so add metformin or aripiprazole instead [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Smoking cessation:</strong> controlled reduction with monitoring of clozapine and olanzapine levels (CYP1A2 induction ends, so levels rise by up to 50%) [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Special populations:</strong><ul>\n<li>Pregnancy: gestational diabetes risk with olanzapine and clozapine; screen with OGTT.</li>\n<li>Children: lifestyle measures plus metformin [2].</li>\n<li>Elderly: careful with metformin in renal decline.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Evidence table</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Study</th>\n<th>Design</th>\n<th>n</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Allison et al., 1999</td>\n<td>Meta-analysis</td>\n<td>81 studies</td>\n<td>Weight gain ranking: clozapine &gt; olanzapine &gt; … &gt; ziprasidone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Correll et al., 2009 (SATIETY)</td>\n<td>Cohort, youth first exposure</td>\n<td>272</td>\n<td>Olanzapine +8.5 kg in 11 weeks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wu et al., 2008</td>\n<td>RCT metformin ± lifestyle</td>\n<td>128</td>\n<td>Metformin + lifestyle −4.7 kg vs placebo</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>de Silva et al., 2016</td>\n<td>Meta-analysis metformin</td>\n<td>12 RCTs</td>\n<td>−3.27 kg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Larsen et al., 2017</td>\n<td>RCT liraglutide</td>\n<td>103</td>\n<td>−5.3 kg vs placebo on clozapine/olanzapine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pillinger et al., 2020</td>\n<td>Network meta-analysis</td>\n<td>100 trials</td>\n<td>Metabolic ranking of 18 antipsychotics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>BAP 2016 [1]</td>\n<td>Guideline</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Lifestyle −3 kg; metformin −3 kg; aripiprazole adjunct −2 kg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fabiano et al., 2025 [3]</td>\n<td>Meta-analysis xanomeline–trospium</td>\n<td>690</td>\n<td>≥7% weight gain RR 0.46</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Critical appraisal</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Monitoring gap:</strong> audits repeatedly show less than half of patients get full metabolic monitoring [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence gaps:</strong> many metformin trials are small, Chinese, single-group studies with heterogeneity [1]. GLP-1 data in SMI are emerging. Long-term cardiovascular outcomes have not been shown.</li>\n<li><strong>Switching trade-off:</strong> the metabolic gain must be weighed against the risk of relapse.</li>\n<li><strong>Illness-related risk:</strong> some dysglycaemia exists in drug-naïve first-episode patients, so drugs are not the whole story.</li>\n<li><strong>Responsibility:</strong> psychiatric teams must take ownership of physical health and not leave it to primary care.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Indian context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>High background prevalence of diabetes (ICMR-INDIAB: about 11%) and of abdominal obesity, so the risk is compounded.</li>\n<li>Olanzapine is often first-line because it is cheap and effective; metformin and lifestyle advice are cheap and should be routine.</li>\n<li>Diet: high carbohydrate intake; give culturally tailored advice (portion control, reduce sugar and refined grains, include pulses and vegetables).</li>\n<li>Public health programmes for NCDs (NPCDCS, renamed NP-NCD) offer screening at Ayushman Arogya Mandirs, which can be integrated with the DMHP.</li>\n<li>Indian Psychiatric Society guidance recommends metabolic monitoring for antipsychotic users.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Recent advances</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Established:</strong> metformin co-prescription at initiation (2025 guideline) [2].</li>\n<li><strong>Emerging:</strong><ul>\n<li>GLP-1 receptor agonists (semaglutide, tirzepatide) for antipsychotic-induced weight gain, with RCTs ongoing.</li>\n<li>Olanzapine–samidorphan (US, 2021) with less weight gain.</li>\n<li>Xanomeline–trospium is weight-neutral [3].</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Investigational:</strong> pharmacogenomic prediction of weight gain (HTR2C, MC4R); digital lifestyle coaching.</li>\n</ul>\n","refs":"<ol>\n<li>Cooper SJ, Reynolds GP, Barnes TRE, et al. BAP guidelines on the management of weight gain, metabolic disturbances and cardiovascular risk associated with psychosis and antipsychotic drug treatment. <em>J Psychopharmacol</em> 2016; 30(8):717–748.</li>\n<li>Guideline on metformin for prevention of antipsychotic-induced weight gain (PROGRESS group, St John of God/St Vincent&#39;s/UCD). <em>Schizophr Bull</em> 2025; 51(5):1201 (guideline algorithm).</li>\n<li>Fabiano N, Wong S, Zhou C, et al. Efficacy, tolerability, and safety of xanomeline-trospium chloride for schizophrenia: a systematic review and meta-analysis. <em>Eur Neuropsychopharmacol</em> 2025; 92:62–73.</li>\n<li>Taylor DM, Barnes TRE, Young AH. <em>The Maudsley Prescribing Guidelines in Psychiatry</em>. 15th ed. Wiley, 2025; Chapter 1 (Antipsychotic-induced weight gain; Treatment of antipsychotic-induced weight gain; Diabetes and impaired glucose tolerance; Effects on plasma lipids); Chapter 2 (Physical monitoring in bipolar disorder).</li>\n<li>Pillinger T, McCutcheon RA, Vano L, et al. Comparative effects of 18 antipsychotics on metabolic function in patients with schizophrenia. <em>Lancet Psychiatry</em> 2020; 7:64–77.</li>\n<li>Alberti KG, Eckel RH, Grundy SM, et al. Harmonizing the metabolic syndrome. <em>Circulation</em> 2009; 120:1640–1645.</li>\n</ol>\n"},"recovery-in-schizophrenia":{"title":"Concept of recovery in schizophrenia","status":"draft","updated":"2026-10-20","reviewer":[],"covers":["p1-43304a4f"],"exam":"<p><strong>Definition.</strong> Recovery in schizophrenia has two meanings:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Clinical (objective, outcome-based) recovery:</strong> sustained remission of symptoms <strong>plus</strong> adequate social and occupational functioning for a defined period. Liberman (2002) required at least 2 years of symptom remission (BPRS items ≤4), work or study at least half-time, independent living, and regular social contact.</li>\n<li><strong>Personal (subjective, process-based) recovery:</strong> in Anthony&#39;s (1993) words, &quot;a deeply personal, unique process of changing one&#39;s attitudes, values, feelings, goals, skills and roles … a way of living a satisfying, hopeful and contributing life even with limitations caused by illness&quot;.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The two overlap but are not the same. A person can recover personally while still hearing voices.</p>\n<h3>Mechanism (how recovery happens)</h3>\n<p>Symptom control (medication, early treatment) + psychosocial rehabilitation (skills, work, family) + personal factors (hope, agency, identity) + social context (acceptance, roles, reduced stigma) → functional and personal recovery. <strong>CHIME</strong> framework (Leamy 2011): <strong>C</strong>onnectedness, <strong>H</strong>ope and optimism, <strong>I</strong>dentity, <strong>M</strong>eaning in life, <strong>E</strong>mpowerment.</p>\n<h3>Clinical vs personal recovery</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>Clinical recovery</th>\n<th>Personal recovery</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Origin</td>\n<td>Psychiatric research, outcome studies</td>\n<td>Service-user/consumer movement (1980s–90s)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Defined by</td>\n<td>Clinician</td>\n<td>Person</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nature</td>\n<td>Outcome, binary, time-bound</td>\n<td>Process, continuous, non-linear</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Symptoms</td>\n<td>Must be absent or minimal</td>\n<td>May persist</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Measures</td>\n<td>Andreasen remission criteria, BPRS/PANSS, GAF, PSP</td>\n<td>QPR, RAS, Stages of Recovery Instrument, CHIME-based tools</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Focus</td>\n<td>Deficits</td>\n<td>Strengths, meaning, citizenship</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Related terms:</strong> <em>Response</em> (≥20–50% PANSS fall) → <em>remission</em> (Andreasen 2005: 8 PANSS items ≤3 for ≥6 months) → <em>recovery</em> (remission + functioning for ≥2 years).</p>\n<h3>How common is recovery?</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Jääskeläinen 2013</strong> meta-analysis (50 studies): median recovery 13.5%, with no improvement over decades.</li>\n<li><strong>Harrison 2001, ISOS:</strong> 15–25-year follow-up; about half of the incidence cohort had favourable outcome; about 16% of initially poor-course cases recovered late.</li>\n<li><strong>Madras Longitudinal Study (Thara 2004)</strong> at 20 years (n=61): only 5 were continuously ill and 5 fully recovered; most had relapses with remissions; 77% had GAF symptom scores and 74% GAF function scores above 60; 76% of men were employed; 73.7% were married [1].</li>\n<li>The WHO IPSS and DOSMeD studies found better outcomes in developing countries [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Assessment</h3>\n<p>Remission criteria (PANSS), functioning (PSP, SOFAS), cognition, quality of life (WHOQOL-BREF), personal recovery (Questionnaire about the Process of Recovery, QPR; Recovery Assessment Scale, RAS), and the patient&#39;s own goals.</p>\n<h3>Management — recovery-oriented care</h3>\n<p>Early detection and reduced DUP → effective treatment at the lowest effective dose, shared decision-making → relapse prevention (adherence, LAI, family psychoeducation) → CBT for psychosis, cognitive remediation → <strong>Individual Placement and Support (IPS)</strong> supported employment, supported housing → peer support workers, Wellness Recovery Action Plan (WRAP), Illness Management and Recovery (IMR) → anti-stigma work, rights-based care (MHCA 2017, RPwD Act 2016).</p>\n<h3>Key evidence</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Harding 1987 (Vermont):</strong> after 20–25 years, one-half to two-thirds of long-stay patients were improved or recovered, which challenged Kraepelinian pessimism.</li>\n<li><strong>Liberman 2002:</strong> operational criteria for recovery.</li>\n<li><strong>Jääskeläinen 2013:</strong> 1 in 7 meet strict recovery criteria.</li>\n<li><strong>Thara 2004 (Madras):</strong> high work and marriage rates and few hospitalisations despite relapses [1].</li>\n<li><strong>IPS supported employment</strong> roughly doubles competitive employment compared with traditional vocational rehabilitation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conclusion.</strong> Recovery is a realistic goal for many, not a rare exception. Strict clinical recovery is uncommon (about 14%), but favourable outcomes are frequent, especially in Indian and other developing-country cohorts. Services should measure both clinical and personal recovery and organise care around the person&#39;s goals, not symptom counts alone.</p>\n","depth":"<h3>Background</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Kraepelin&#39;s dementia praecox (1896)</strong> implied inevitable deterioration. <strong>Bleuler (1911)</strong> noted variable courses, and <strong>Manfred Bleuler (1972)</strong> found that about 20% of his 208 patients recovered fully and a further 33% had mild residual states.</li>\n<li>Long-term follow-ups in the 1970s–80s (Huber&#39;s Bonn study, Ciompi&#39;s Lausanne study, the Vermont and Maine studies, Tsuang&#39;s Iowa 500) found heterogeneous, often favourable courses [1].</li>\n<li>The <strong>consumer/survivor movement</strong> of the 1980s–90s (Deegan 1988, &quot;Recovery: the lived experience of rehabilitation&quot;) reframed recovery as a personal journey.</li>\n<li>Recovery became policy: the US New Freedom Commission (2003), the UK&#39;s ImROC programme, and in India the National Mental Health Policy (2014) and the MHCA 2017 emphasise rights and community living.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Epidemiology</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>WHO IPSS (1973)</strong> and <strong>DOSMeD (1992):</strong> better 2- and 5-year outcomes in developing centres (Agra, Chandigarh, Ibadan) than in developed ones [1].</li>\n<li><strong>ISOS</strong> (13–26-year reassessment) confirmed better outcomes in developing centres, while noting possible biases [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Chandigarh studies:</strong> good outcomes in rural cohorts. <strong>Mojtabai 2001:</strong> a poor 2-year course in an Indian cohort predicted a poor 15-year course and high mortality, with 47% dead at 15 years [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Madras:</strong> 17% mortality over 20 years (mean age at death 34.2 years), seven suicides; a shadow over favourable outcomes [1].</li>\n<li>Proposed reasons for better outcomes in developing countries: family support and tolerance, less rigid occupational demands (unorganised sector jobs), no welfare disincentive, and biological differences. These remain postulates [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Classification and criteria</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Construct</th>\n<th>Criteria</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Response</td>\n<td>≥20% (minimal) or ≥50% (good) reduction in PANSS from baseline (corrected for minimum score)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Remission (RSWG, Andreasen 2005)</td>\n<td>P1 delusions, P2 conceptual disorganisation, P3 hallucinations, N1 blunted affect, N4 social withdrawal, N6 lack of spontaneity, G5 mannerisms, G9 unusual thought content — all ≤3 for ≥6 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recovery (Liberman 2002)</td>\n<td>≥2 years: remission, half-time work/study, independent living (or with family without supervision), weekly social activity with peers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Personal recovery</td>\n<td>Self-defined; CHIME</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>ICD-11 course specifiers include &quot;in full remission&quot; and &quot;in partial remission&quot;.</p>\n<h3>Mechanism in detail — determinants of recovery</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Favourable</th>\n<th>Unfavourable</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Acute onset, clear precipitant</td>\n<td>Insidious onset (54.5% in Madras cohort) [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Short DUP</td>\n<td>Long DUP</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Good premorbid adjustment</td>\n<td>Poor premorbid function, developmental delays</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Female sex, married</td>\n<td>Male, single (in Madras, 15 ill men refusing treatment, 10 single and living alone) [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Affective symptoms, positive symptoms</td>\n<td>Prominent negative and cognitive symptoms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Family support, low EE</td>\n<td>High EE, social isolation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Adherence, early response</td>\n<td>Substance misuse, non-adherence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Employment opportunity</td>\n<td>Stigma, unemployment</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Clinical features in detail — the stages of personal recovery (Andresen 2006)</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Moratorium:</strong> denial, confusion, hopelessness.</li>\n<li><strong>Awareness:</strong> realising a fulfilling life is possible.</li>\n<li><strong>Preparation:</strong> taking stock of strengths, learning about the illness.</li>\n<li><strong>Rebuilding:</strong> setting goals, taking responsibility.</li>\n<li><strong>Growth:</strong> living a meaningful life, resilience.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Patterns of long-term course (Madras 20-year data) [1]:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Complete remission (n=5).</li>\n<li>Relapses with complete remission between them.</li>\n<li>Relapses with partial remission between them.</li>\n<li>Continuous illness (n=5).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The middle two patterns were the majority.</p>\n<h3>Assessment in detail</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Clinical:</strong> PANSS (remission items), PSP (four domains: socially useful activities, personal and social relationships, self-care, disturbing behaviour), SOFAS, GAF (the Madras study used GAF symptoms and GAF function) [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Cognition:</strong> MATRICS battery; strong predictor of functional recovery.</li>\n<li><strong>Personal recovery:</strong> QPR (15-item), RAS (24-item), Stages of Recovery Instrument (STORI), Maryland Assessment of Recovery.</li>\n<li><strong>Disability:</strong> <strong>IDEAS</strong> (Indian Disability Evaluation and Assessment Scale) for disability certification under the RPwD Act 2016.</li>\n<li><strong>Quality of life:</strong> WHOQOL-BREF.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Differential — what recovery is not</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Difference</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Cure</td>\n<td>Recovery allows ongoing vulnerability or symptoms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Remission</td>\n<td>Symptom-only; no functional or personal element</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rehabilitation</td>\n<td>A service intervention; recovery is the person&#39;s process</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deinstitutionalisation</td>\n<td>A policy; may or may not promote recovery</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Management details</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Medication:</strong> shared decision-making, lowest effective dose, attention to adverse effects that block recovery (sedation, weight gain, sexual dysfunction, hyperprolactinaemia).</li>\n<li><strong>Psychosocial:</strong> family interventions; CBTp; cognitive remediation combined with vocational rehabilitation; social skills training; IPS (rapid job search, competitive jobs, integration with the clinical team).</li>\n<li><strong>Peer support:</strong> peer workers and self-help groups.</li>\n<li><strong>Women:</strong> consider the hormonal course (perimenstrual worsening, perimenopausal relapse), contraception and parenting support [2].</li>\n<li><strong>Older adults:</strong> physical comorbidity and loneliness.</li>\n<li><strong>Physical health:</strong> the mortality gap of 15–20 years, cardiovascular and metabolic care.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Evidence table</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Study</th>\n<th>Design</th>\n<th>n / follow-up</th>\n<th>Main finding</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>M. Bleuler, 1972</td>\n<td>Clinical cohort</td>\n<td>208 / 20+ years</td>\n<td>~20% full recovery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Harding et al., 1987 (Vermont)</td>\n<td>Long-stay patients</td>\n<td>269 / ~32 years</td>\n<td>50–68% improved or recovered</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Harrison et al., 2001 (ISOS)</td>\n<td>International cohorts</td>\n<td>1,633 / 15–25 years</td>\n<td>Half favourable; late recovery common</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thara, 2004 (Madras) [1]</td>\n<td>First-episode cohort</td>\n<td>90 (61 at follow-up) / 20 years</td>\n<td>5 recovered, 5 continuously ill; 76% of men employed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Jääskeläinen et al., 2013</td>\n<td>Meta-analysis</td>\n<td>50 studies</td>\n<td>Median recovery 13.5%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lally et al., 2017</td>\n<td>Meta-analysis, first episode</td>\n<td>79 studies</td>\n<td>Remission 58%, recovery 38%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Leamy et al., 2011</td>\n<td>Systematic review</td>\n<td>97 papers</td>\n<td>CHIME framework</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Critical appraisal</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definitions drive rates:</strong> strict Liberman criteria give about 14%; broader definitions give 40–60%.</li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Developing country advantage&quot;:</strong> may reflect sampling (urban, acute-onset cases), loss to follow-up, different expectations, and higher mortality removing poor-outcome cases [1]. Madras had 17% mortality.</li>\n<li><strong>Personal recovery is hard to measure</strong> and can be co-opted to justify service cuts (&quot;recovery&quot; as discharge).</li>\n<li><strong>Recovery and medication:</strong> some service-user narratives emphasise recovery without medication; trial data show higher relapse but possibly better function with dose reduction in a subgroup. This is still contested.</li>\n<li>Employment in Madras reflected informal-sector jobs and no welfare safety net, not necessarily better symptom outcomes [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Indian context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>MHCA 2017:</strong> right to community living (Sec 19), right to access care, advance directives, and supported decision-making all support recovery-oriented care.</li>\n<li><strong>RPwD Act 2016:</strong> mental illness is a benchmark disability (≥40% on IDEAS); reservations in jobs and education; disability pension.</li>\n<li>NGOs: SCARF Chennai, The Banyan, Anjali, and Atmiyata (community volunteers).</li>\n<li><strong>COPSI</strong> (Chatterjee 2014): community care added to facility care modestly reduced disability [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Tele-MANAS</strong> (2022) and the DMHP improve continuity.</li>\n<li>Cultural strengths: family involvement, spirituality, and accepted social roles (marriage, informal work). Risks include concealing illness at marriage (seen in the Madras follow-up) [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Recent advances</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Established:</strong> IPS supported employment; family psychoeducation; peer support.</li>\n<li><strong>Emerging:</strong> digital recovery tools (apps, WRAP online); open dialogue approaches; recovery colleges.</li>\n<li><strong>Investigational:</strong> prediction of recovery from early cognitive and neuroimaging markers; personalised dose reduction trials.</li>\n</ul>\n","refs":"<ol>\n<li>Thara R. Twenty-year course of schizophrenia: the Madras Longitudinal Study. <em>Can J Psychiatry</em> 2004; 49(8):564–569.</li>\n<li>Mu E, Gurvich C, Kulkarni J. Estrogen and psychosis — a review and future directions. <em>Arch Womens Ment Health</em> 2024; 27:877–885.</li>\n<li>Liberman RP, Kopelowicz A, Ventura J, Gutkind D. Operational criteria and factors related to recovery from schizophrenia. <em>Int Rev Psychiatry</em> 2002; 14:256–272.</li>\n<li>Anthony WA. Recovery from mental illness: the guiding vision of the mental health service system in the 1990s. <em>Psychosoc Rehabil J</em> 1993; 16:11–23.</li>\n<li>Leamy M, Bird V, Le Boutillier C, Williams J, Slade M. Conceptual framework for personal recovery in mental health: systematic review and narrative synthesis. <em>Br J Psychiatry</em> 2011; 199:445–452.</li>\n<li>Andreasen NC, Carpenter WT, Kane JM, et al. Remission in schizophrenia: proposed criteria and rationale for consensus. <em>Am J Psychiatry</em> 2005; 162:441–449.</li>\n<li>Jääskeläinen E, Juola P, Hirvonen N, et al. A systematic review and meta-analysis of recovery in schizophrenia. <em>Schizophr Bull</em> 2013; 39:1296–1306.</li>\n<li>Harrison G, Hopper K, Craig T, et al. Recovery from psychotic illness: a 15- and 25-year international follow-up study. <em>Br J Psychiatry</em> 2001; 178:506–517.</li>\n<li>Government of India. <em>Mental Healthcare Act 2017</em>; <em>Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act 2016</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n"},"rtms-transcranial-magnetic-stimulation":{"title":"Repetitive transcranial magnetic stimulation (rTMS) and its role in psychiatry","status":"draft","updated":"2026-10-20","reviewer":[],"covers":["p1-87119a2b","p1-3ea44b90"],"exam":"<p><strong>Definition.</strong> Transcranial magnetic stimulation (TMS) is a non-invasive, non-convulsive brain stimulation technique. A rapidly changing current in a coil held against the scalp produces a magnetic field that induces an electric current in the underlying cortex and depolarises neurons. Given repeatedly over a course of sessions (repetitive TMS, rTMS), it changes cortical excitability and network activity beyond the session itself. Barker and colleagues described it in 1985; it entered clinical psychiatry in the 1990s, and the US FDA first cleared a device for major depression in 2008 [1].</p>\n<h3>Mechanism</h3>\n<p>Coil current → time-varying magnetic field (about 1.5–2 tesla, passes through skull without attenuation) → induced electric field in cortex (depth ~2–3 cm with a figure-of-eight coil) → neuronal depolarisation → repeated trains produce LTP-like (excitatory) or LTD-like (inhibitory) plasticity → effects spread trans-synaptically along networks (e.g., left DLPFC → subgenual anterior cingulate cortex, sgACC) [1,2].</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>High frequency (≥5 Hz, usually 10 Hz)</strong> → increases cortical excitability.</li>\n<li><strong>Low frequency (≤1 Hz)</strong> → decreases excitability.</li>\n<li><strong>Theta burst stimulation (TBS)</strong>: bursts of three pulses at 50 Hz repeated at 5 Hz. <strong>Intermittent TBS (iTBS)</strong> is excitatory and takes about 3 minutes instead of about 37; <strong>continuous TBS (cTBS)</strong> is inhibitory [1].</li>\n<li>In depression, the best-supported model is <strong>network rebalancing</strong>: the left DLPFC is hypoactive and the sgACC hyperactive. Stimulating the DLPFC subregion most <em>anticorrelated</em> with the sgACC predicts better response [2,3].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Procedure (IPS guideline) [1]</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Key points</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Screening</td>\n<td>Psychiatric, medical, neurological and drug history; epilepsy (self/family), head injury, stroke, tumour, seizure-threshold-lowering drugs; TMS Adult Safety Screen or Rossi questionnaire</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Consent</td>\n<td>Explain side effects and their likelihood</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Motor threshold (MT)</td>\n<td>Lowest intensity giving a response in contralateral abductor pollicis brevis/first dorsal interosseous in ≥5 of 10 single pulses; re-measure weekly, and after dose changes, heavy alcohol or substance use</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Targeting</td>\n<td>&quot;5-cm rule&quot;, International 10–20 EEG system (Beam F3 for left DLPFC) — cost-effective; neuronavigation (MRI/fMRI) more accurate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Session</td>\n<td>Earplugs for patient and operator; operator &gt;40 cm from coil</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Course</td>\n<td>Typically 20–30 sessions over 4–6 weeks for depression</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Contraindications:</strong> ferromagnetic or magnet-sensitive implants or metal in head/neck; cochlear implants; DBS leads, pacemakers, pumps or ferromagnetic tattoo ink within 10 cm of the coil. Implants below the neck (hip, knee) are safe [1].</p>\n<h3>Clinical uses — IPS CPG 2023 grades [1]</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Condition</th>\n<th>Recommended protocol</th>\n<th>Strength</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Unipolar depression</td>\n<td>HF-rTMS left DLPFC</td>\n<td><strong>Strong</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>LF-rTMS right DLPFC</td>\n<td>Moderate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>Bilateral, iTBS, bilateral TBS, deep TMS, priming</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bipolar depression</td>\n<td>HF left DLPFC</td>\n<td>Moderate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Treatment-resistant depression</td>\n<td>HF left DLPFC (moderate); LF right DLPFC (low)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Peripartum, post-stroke, post-TBI, Parkinson&#39;s depression</td>\n<td>HF left DLPFC</td>\n<td>Moderate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>OCD</td>\n<td>LF right DLPFC (moderate); HF bilateral DLPFC, LF SMA (low); 800–1,200 pulses, 10–30 sessions</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GAD</td>\n<td>LF right DLPFC</td>\n<td>Moderate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PTSD</td>\n<td>HF or LF right DLPFC</td>\n<td>Moderate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Schizophrenia — negative symptoms</td>\n<td>HF left DLPFC, &gt;10 sessions, &gt;100% MT</td>\n<td>Moderate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Schizophrenia — resistant auditory hallucinations</td>\n<td>LF (1 Hz) left temporoparietal cortex (T3P3)</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alzheimer&#39;s dementia (cognition, BPSD)</td>\n<td>HF bilateral/left DLPFC, &gt;5–10 sessions</td>\n<td>Moderate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Smoking cessation</td>\n<td>HF left DLPFC</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Not effective / no recommendation</td>\n<td>Mania, panic disorder, clozapine-resistant schizophrenia, cognition in schizophrenia, eating disorders, ADHD</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>For depression, ≥1,800 pulses/session, subthreshold intensity and ≤2-week duration predicted response in TBS studies; accelerated (several sessions/day) protocols were not better than once-daily treatment in meta-analysis [1].</p>\n<h3>Adverse effects</h3>\n<p>Headache (22.6% vs 10.9% sham), scalp discomfort (23.8% vs 16.2%) and site pain (16.2% vs 5.0%) are common but mild. Serious adverse events (0.9% vs 1.5%) and drop-outs (3.3% vs 2.3%) do not differ from sham [1]. <strong>Seizure</strong> is rare with conventional parameters (well under 1 per 1,000 sessions [8]); 62% occur in the first session and 75% in the first three, so the first sessions need the most vigilance [1]. Others: syncope, transient hearing change (earplugs), facial twitching, and a hypomanic switch that meta-analysis did not find above sham [1].</p>\n<h3>Key evidence</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>O&#39;Reardon 2007</strong> (n=301) and <strong>George 2010 OPT-TMS</strong> (n=190): HF left DLPFC rTMS superior to sham in medication-resistant MDD [4,5].</li>\n<li><strong>Mutz 2018 network meta-analysis</strong> (56 trials): response OR 3.75 for HF left DLPFC; LF right, bilateral, iTBS and deep TMS also effective [1].</li>\n<li><strong>THREE-D, Blumberger 2018</strong> (n=414): iTBS (3 min) non-inferior to 10 Hz rTMS (37.5 min) [6].</li>\n<li><strong>SAINT, Cole 2020/2022</strong>: fMRI-targeted accelerated iTBS, 10 sessions/day for 5 days; 86.4% remission open-label and 78.6% vs 13.3% sham remission in an RCT (n=29) [3,7].</li>\n<li>Durability: of responders, 66.5% still respond at 3 months, 52.9% at 6 months and 46.3% at 12 months [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conclusion.</strong> rTMS is an established, well-tolerated second-line treatment for unipolar depression after one or more failed antidepressants. Strong evidence supports high-frequency left DLPFC stimulation, and iTBS cuts session time without losing efficacy. It is less effective than ECT and should not replace ECT for psychotic, catatonic or acutely suicidal depression. Its place in OCD, PTSD, negative symptoms and dementia is real but rests on moderate evidence from small trials with large sham responses.</p>\n","depth":"<h3>Background</h3>\n<p>Faraday&#39;s law of electromagnetic induction (1831) is the physical basis. d&#39;Arsonval (1896) produced phosphenes with a head coil. Barker, Jalinous and Freeston at Sheffield (1985) built the first practical stimulator for the motor cortex, initially as a diagnostic tool for central motor conduction. In the 1990s, George and Pascual-Leone showed antidepressant effects of left prefrontal stimulation. FDA clearances followed: NeuroStar for MDD (2008), Brainsway H1 deep TMS for MDD (2013), H7 coil for OCD (2017), iTBS (2018), H4 coil for smoking cessation (2020), anxious depression (2021), and SAINT/neuronavigated accelerated iTBS (2022) [1,3].</p>\n<h3>Mechanism in detail</h3>\n<p><strong>Physics.</strong> A capacitor discharges through the coil in about 100–300 µs → magnetic field → induced E-field parallel to the scalp → preferentially activates horizontally oriented axons and interneurons in superficial cortex. Figure-of-eight (butterfly) coils give a focal field at the junction of the wings. H-coils (deep TMS) reach deeper but less focally. Double-cone coils reach medial areas such as the SMA and ACC [1].</p>\n<p><strong>Neurophysiology.</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Single pulse → motor-evoked potential (MEP); used for MT and research measures of excitability.</li>\n<li>Paired pulse → short-interval intracortical inhibition (GABA-A) and intracortical facilitation (glutamate).</li>\n<li>Repetitive trains → NMDA-receptor-dependent synaptic plasticity, raised BDNF, changed monoamine release (dopamine release in caudate after left DLPFC rTMS), and normalised HPA axis activity.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Network model of depression.</strong> Left DLPFC (top-down control) is hypoactive → limbic sgACC hyperactive → negative bias and rumination. Excitatory left DLPFC stimulation → reduced sgACC activity. Fox et al. (2012) showed that clinical efficacy of different DLPFC sites correlated with their anticorrelation with sgACC; SAINT targets this individually with resting-state fMRI [3]. Placebo response in depression trials also activates left DLPFC and sgACC/ventral striatum, which partly explains the large sham response (g ≈ 0.8) [1].</p>\n<p><strong>Why low frequency on the right?</strong> Inter-hemispheric imbalance model: relative right prefrontal overactivity in depression and anxiety; 1 Hz inhibits it.</p>\n<h3>Parameters explained</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Parameter</th>\n<th>Typical value (depression)</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Frequency</td>\n<td>10 Hz (HF-L); 1 Hz (LF-R); iTBS 50 Hz bursts at 5 Hz</td>\n<td>Excitatory vs inhibitory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Intensity</td>\n<td>110–120% resting MT (TBS often 80–120%)</td>\n<td>Prefrontal cortex lies deeper than the motor strip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Train</td>\n<td>4 s on, 26 s off (10 Hz)</td>\n<td>Inter-train interval limits seizure risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pulses/session</td>\n<td>3,000 (10 Hz); 600 (iTBS)</td>\n<td>≥1,800 better in TBS meta-analysis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sessions</td>\n<td>20–30, 5 days a week</td>\n<td>Accelerated: 2–10 a day</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Assessment in detail</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Before treatment:</strong> diagnosis and severity (HAM-D, MADRS), treatment history (number of adequate trials — the Antidepressant Treatment History Form), substances, sleep, medical illness, pregnancy, metallic implants. Use the TASS or Rossi 15-item questionnaire [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Investigations:</strong> none routinely required. EEG only if seizure history is unclear. MRI when neuronavigation is used. X-ray cannot tell whether metal is ferromagnetic [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Medications:</strong> record seizure-threshold-lowering drugs (clozapine, bupropion, high-dose TCAs, theophylline), alcohol use, sleep loss and infection. Benzodiazepines and anticonvulsants raise MT and may reduce the effect; re-measure MT after dose changes [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Monitoring:</strong> rating scales weekly; ask about hearing, headache and switch symptoms.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Differential choice — rTMS compared with other treatments</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>When preferred over rTMS</th>\n<th>Evidence point</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>ECT</td>\n<td>Psychotic depression, catatonia, high suicide risk, refusal to eat, need for rapid response</td>\n<td>Bitemporal ECT had higher response than HF-L rTMS in network meta-analysis [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ketamine/esketamine</td>\n<td>Rapid relief needed, outpatient access to monitoring</td>\n<td>Response within hours; durability limited</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>tDCS</td>\n<td>Low cost, home use</td>\n<td>Smaller and less consistent effects</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Psychotherapy augmentation</td>\n<td>Mild–moderate depression, patient preference</td>\n<td>Combining with rTMS showed no added benefit in a small meta-analysis [1]</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Management details</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Augment, don&#39;t replace.</strong> Nearly all trials used rTMS as an adjunct to treatment as usual. In TRD, response was better when rTMS was added to antidepressants than when given alone [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Suicidality:</strong> rTMS reduced suicidal ideation (g 0.39) in non-TRD patients. IPS does <strong>not</strong> recommend rTMS for acutely suicidal patients; ECT is preferred [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Maintenance:</strong> evidence is accumulating. Tapering schedules (twice weekly → weekly → monthly) are used for responders with relapse history, but no specific recommendation is made [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> the E-field at the uterus from a figure-of-eight coil over the DLPFC (≈100 mV/m at 60 cm) is far below the threshold for nerve stimulation (800 mV/m), so risk is minimal. This makes rTMS an attractive option in perinatal depression [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Children:</strong> single- and paired-pulse TMS are safe from age 2 with hearing protection. Therapeutic rTMS in adolescents is still investigational [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Elderly:</strong> brain atrophy increases scalp-to-cortex distance. Older age and more sessions predicted greater response in a meta-analysis of patients aged over 50 [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Implants:</strong> pacemakers, VNS and other stimulators are allowed if the coil stays more than 10 cm away. Use DBS only with clear justification and ethics committee oversight [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Staff:</strong> a <em>TMS physician</em> (psychiatrist, neurologist or neurosurgeon trained in rTMS) prescribes; a <em>TMS operator</em> (often a nurse) delivers and must be trained in seizure management [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Evidence table</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Study</th>\n<th>Design</th>\n<th>n</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>O&#39;Reardon et al., 2007 [4]</td>\n<td>Multicentre RCT, HF-L vs sham</td>\n<td>301</td>\n<td>Superior on MADRS at 4–6 weeks; led to FDA clearance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>George et al., 2010 (OPT-TMS) [5]</td>\n<td>NIMH RCT</td>\n<td>190</td>\n<td>Remission 14.1% vs 5.1% sham</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Blumberger et al., 2018 (THREE-D) [6]</td>\n<td>Non-inferiority RCT</td>\n<td>414</td>\n<td>iTBS = 10 Hz (response ~49% vs 47%)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mutz et al., 2019 [1]</td>\n<td>Network meta-analysis</td>\n<td>53 trials</td>\n<td>SMD 0.83; response OR 3.17–6.02 by protocol</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cole et al., 2022 (SAINT) [7]</td>\n<td>Double-blind RCT</td>\n<td>29</td>\n<td>Remission 78.6% vs 13.3% at 4 weeks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Valiengo et al., 2022 [1]</td>\n<td>Meta-analysis, MDD aged &gt;50</td>\n<td>26</td>\n<td>Remission OR 4.63</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hyde et al., 2022 [1]</td>\n<td>Umbrella MA</td>\n<td>46 trials</td>\n<td>Effective in unipolar (0.60), not significant in bipolar depression (0.20)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Indian meta-analysis (52 studies) [1]</td>\n<td>Indian trials</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Moderate–large effects across disorders</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Critical appraisal</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Blinding is imperfect.</strong> Active rTMS causes scalp sensations and twitching that sham coils do not fully mimic. The large placebo response (g ≈ 0.8) narrows the real-minus-sham difference [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Effect size is moderate.</strong> Response rates in clinical trials are 30–50% and remission 15–30%, lower than ECT.</li>\n<li><strong>Heterogeneity:</strong> protocols, targets, intensity and session number vary widely, and most non-depression indications rest on a few small trials.</li>\n<li><strong>Durability:</strong> about half of responders lose benefit by 1 year without maintenance [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Accelerated protocols:</strong> SAINT&#39;s dramatic remission rates come from small samples with individualised fMRI targeting that few centres can provide. Pooled accelerated studies have not beaten once-daily treatment [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Cost and access:</strong> daily attendance for 4–6 weeks is a barrier, especially for rural patients.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Indian context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>IPS Clinical Practice Guideline (Tikka et al., 2023)</strong> is the reference standard in India. It was based on an umbrella review of 97 meta-analyses (2018–2022) [1].</li>\n<li>Training: IPS with NIMHANS and AIIMS New Delhi runs annual workshops; CIP Ranchi and KMC Manipal also train [1].</li>\n<li>NIMHANS and CIP Ranchi have contributed many Indian trials on auditory hallucinations, negative symptoms, OCD and depression.</li>\n<li>Under the <strong>Mental Healthcare Act 2017</strong>, rTMS is not a restricted procedure (unlike ECT, it needs no anaesthesia), but informed consent and documentation remain mandatory. A nominated representative&#39;s consent applies for patients lacking capacity.</li>\n<li>Cost per course and availability outside cities limit use. Twice-daily or iTBS schedules can shorten hospital stay.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Recent advances</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Established:</strong> iTBS (3-minute sessions); deep TMS H-coils for MDD and OCD.</li>\n<li><strong>Emerging:</strong> SAINT / accelerated neuronavigated iTBS (FDA-cleared 2022; replication ongoing) [3,7].</li>\n<li><strong>Investigational:</strong> EEG-synchronised TMS, closed-loop systems, priming protocols, cerebellar iTBS for negative symptoms, orbitofrontal targets for OCD, and connectivity-guided targeting for other disorders [1].</li>\n<li>Combining rTMS with D-cycloserine (NMDA partial agonist) to enhance plasticity is being studied.</li>\n</ul>\n","refs":"<ol>\n<li>Tikka SK, Siddiqui MA, Garg S, Pattojoshi A, Gautam M. Clinical practice guidelines for the therapeutic use of repetitive transcranial magnetic stimulation in neuropsychiatric disorders. <em>Indian J Psychiatry</em> 2023; 65(2):270–288.</li>\n<li>Taylor DM, Barnes TRE, Young AH. <em>The Maudsley Prescribing Guidelines in Psychiatry</em>. 15th ed. Wiley, 2025; Chapter 3 (Introduction to depression; Treatment-resistant depression).</li>\n<li>Cole EJ, Stimpson KH, Bentzley BS, et al. Stanford Accelerated Intelligent Neuromodulation Therapy for treatment-resistant depression. <em>Am J Psychiatry</em> 2020; 177:716–726. (Summarised in user&#39;s TMS notes.)</li>\n<li>O&#39;Reardon JP, Solvason HB, Janicak PG, et al. Efficacy and safety of transcranial magnetic stimulation in the acute treatment of major depression: a multisite randomized controlled trial. <em>Biol Psychiatry</em> 2007; 62:1208–1216.</li>\n<li>George MS, Lisanby SH, Avery D, et al. Daily left prefrontal transcranial magnetic stimulation therapy for major depressive disorder: a sham-controlled randomized trial. <em>Arch Gen Psychiatry</em> 2010; 67:507–516.</li>\n<li>Blumberger DM, Vila-Rodriguez F, Thorpe KE, et al. Effectiveness of theta burst versus high-frequency repetitive transcranial magnetic stimulation in patients with depression (THREE-D). <em>Lancet</em> 2018; 391:1683–1692.</li>\n<li>Cole EJ, Phillips AL, Bentzley BS, et al. Stanford Neuromodulation Therapy (SNT): a double-blind randomized controlled trial. <em>Am J Psychiatry</em> 2022; 179:132–141.</li>\n<li>Rossi S, Antal A, Bestmann S, et al. Safety and recommendations for TMS use in healthy subjects and patient populations. <em>Clin Neurophysiol</em> 2021; 132:269–306.</li>\n</ol>\n"},"schizophrenia-neurodevelopmental-hypothesis":{"title":"Schizophrenia as a neurodevelopmental disorder — critical evaluation","status":"draft","updated":"2026-10-20","reviewer":[],"covers":["p1-25fbc65c"],"exam":"<p><strong>Definition.</strong> A <em>neurodevelopmental disorder</em> is one in which the brain is not formed normally from the start: abnormal genetic programming or early environmental insult disrupts proliferation, migration, differentiation, synapse formation or pruning [1]. The <strong>neurodevelopmental hypothesis of schizophrenia</strong> (Weinberger 1987; Murray &amp; Lewis 1987) says that the illness is the late expression of a brain lesion acquired in fetal or early life, unmasked by normal maturation in adolescence. It competes with the Kraepelinian <strong>neurodegenerative</strong> view (&quot;dementia praecox&quot;). The current consensus is a <strong>progressive neurodevelopmental</strong> model: developmental vulnerability plus processes after onset that can worsen outcome [1].</p>\n<h3>Mechanism</h3>\n<p>Genes (CNVs, many common variants) + early insult (obstetric complications, maternal infection, famine) → abnormal neuronal migration and connectivity (first hit) → premorbid soft signs, cognitive and social deficits → adolescent <strong>excessive synaptic pruning</strong> and dopamine/glutamate maturation (second hit) → stress, cannabis, urbanicity, migration (third hit) → aberrant salience from striatal dopamine dysregulation → psychosis → possible neuroprogression (glutamate excitotoxicity, oxidative stress) in a subgroup [1].</p>\n<h3>Evidence for and against</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Line of evidence</th>\n<th>Supports neurodevelopment</th>\n<th>Against / limitations</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Obstetric complications</td>\n<td>Pooled OR ≈ 2 (Geddes 1999; Cannon 2002): bleeding, pre-eclampsia, hypoxia [1]</td>\n<td>Effect sizes small; publication bias; many exposed never develop illness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prenatal infection, famine</td>\n<td>Influenza, rubella, Dutch Hunger Winter (Susser 1996), winter–spring birth excess [1]</td>\n<td>Influenza findings not replicated (Crow 1992; Susser 1994) [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Minor physical anomalies</td>\n<td>High-arched palate, low-set ears, dermatoglyphic changes (ectodermal origin) [1]</td>\n<td>Non-specific</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Premorbid deficits</td>\n<td>Delayed milestones, low IQ, poor social competence in birth and conscript cohorts (British 1946, Dunedin, Swedish and Israeli conscripts, Northern Finland 1966) [1]</td>\n<td>Most children with these signs never develop schizophrenia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Imaging at first episode</td>\n<td>Ventricular enlargement, smaller hippocampus, cavum septum pellucidum already present before treatment [1]</td>\n<td>Progressive grey matter loss after onset suggests a degenerative element</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Neuropathology</td>\n<td>Neuronal disarray, heterotopic neurons, reduced neuropil and spines; <strong>no gliosis</strong> (insult before third trimester) [1]</td>\n<td>Migration findings inconsistently replicated [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Genetics</td>\n<td>DISC1, NRG1, DTNBP1, 22q11.2 deletion, C4 complement (pruning), synaptic genes in GWAS; overlap with autism and intellectual disability CNVs [1]</td>\n<td>Polygenic, small effects, also overlap with bipolar disorder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Course and treatment</td>\n<td>Stable cognitive deficit after onset (Heaton 2001) [1]</td>\n<td>Treatability, relapses, and DUP effects fit poorly with a static lesion [1]</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Assessment implications</h3>\n<p>Developmental history (birth, milestones, schooling), neurological soft signs, IQ, family history, minor physical anomalies; consider 22q11.2 deletion or other CNV testing in patients with intellectual disability, dysmorphism or congenital anomalies.</p>\n<h3>Management implications</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Prevention: better obstetric care, maternal nutrition and infection control.</li>\n<li>Early detection: clinical high-risk services and shorter duration of untreated psychosis (DUP).</li>\n<li>Cognitive remediation and vocational rehabilitation for the developmental cognitive deficit.</li>\n<li>Treat early and keep relapses low to limit any neuroprogressive component.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Key evidence</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Weinberger 1987; Murray &amp; Lewis 1987:</strong> original statements of the hypothesis.</li>\n<li><strong>Feinberg 1983:</strong> excessive adolescent synaptic pruning [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Jones 1994 (British 1946 cohort):</strong> delayed milestones and speech problems in children who later developed schizophrenia [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Sekar 2016:</strong> C4 complement variants drive synaptic pruning and are associated with schizophrenia risk.</li>\n<li><strong>Gupta &amp; Kulhara (Indian review):</strong> evidence favours neurodevelopment more strongly, but a neurodegenerative component exists in subgroups [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conclusion.</strong> The statement is <strong>largely true but incomplete</strong>. Strong convergent evidence places the origins of schizophrenia in abnormal brain development, but the developmental model alone cannot explain adolescent onset, relapse-linked deterioration, response to treatment, and full recovery in a minority. Schizophrenia is best described as a <strong>progressive neurodevelopmental disorder</strong> with heterogeneous subgroups. This view is also more therapeutically optimistic than either pure model, because it supports early intervention and relapse prevention [1].</p>\n","depth":"<h3>Background</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Clouston (1891)</strong> described &quot;developmental insanity&quot;. <strong>Southard (1915)</strong> reported cortical anomalies [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Kraepelin (1896–1919)</strong> grouped <em>dementia praecox</em> by its deteriorating course; this formed the neurodegenerative tradition. He and <strong>Bleuler</strong> also noted childhood oddities in later patients [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Bender (1947)</strong> and <strong>Watt (1978)</strong> described childhood neurological and social abnormalities [1].</li>\n<li><strong>1987:</strong> Weinberger (&quot;implications of normal brain development for the pathogenesis of schizophrenia&quot;) and Murray &amp; Lewis (&quot;Is schizophrenia a neurodevelopmental disorder?&quot;) formalised the hypothesis.</li>\n<li><strong>Late 1990s:</strong> Woods (1998) proposed the <em>progressive neurodevelopmental</em> synthesis; Keshavan (1999) proposed a unitary &quot;development, disease and degeneration&quot; model [1].</li>\n<li><strong>2010s:</strong> genomics (PGC GWAS, CNVs), C4 and pruning, and the developmental risk-factor model (Murray, Howes 2017).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Epidemiology of risk factors</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Risk factor</th>\n<th>Approximate effect</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Family history: first-degree relative</td>\n<td>Risk ~10% (vs ~1%); MZ concordance ~45%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>22q11.2 deletion</td>\n<td>~25–30% develop psychosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Obstetric complications</td>\n<td>OR ≈ 2 [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prenatal famine (Dutch Hunger Winter)</td>\n<td>About a twofold increase [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Winter–spring birth</td>\n<td>~5–8% excess</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Paternal age &gt;55</td>\n<td>~2–3 fold</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Urban upbringing, migration</td>\n<td>~2 fold</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Childhood adversity</td>\n<td>OR ≈ 2.8 (Varese 2012)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Heavy cannabis use</td>\n<td>~2–4 fold</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Male excess (≈1.4:1) and earlier onset in men (peak 20–29) than women (peak about 5 years later) fit a model in which oestrogen buffers developmental vulnerability [2].</p>\n<h3>Classification and criteria</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>DSM-5-TR places schizophrenia under &quot;Schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorders&quot;, <strong>not</strong> under neurodevelopmental disorders (which include intellectual disability, autism, ADHD, specific learning disorder, motor and communication disorders).</li>\n<li>ICD-11 likewise keeps it in &quot;Schizophrenia or other primary psychotic disorders&quot; (6A20), separate from 6A0 neurodevelopmental disorders.</li>\n<li>The classifications acknowledge developmental antecedents in their text but <strong>do not reclassify</strong>. This is the strongest nosological argument that the statement oversimplifies.</li>\n<li>Childhood-onset schizophrenia (before age 13) is the clearest developmental form. It has more premorbid abnormality, more CNVs and more progressive grey matter loss [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Mechanism in detail</h3>\n<p><strong>Normal development</strong> has critical windows. Neurogenesis and migration (reelin, astrotactin) occur in the first and second trimesters. Synaptogenesis peaks in infancy. Pruning runs from childhood into the third decade, with prefrontal pruning last [1].</p>\n<p><strong>Two-hit/three-hit model:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Early hit:</em> genetic risk + prenatal insult → subtle cortical dysconnectivity, GABA interneuron (parvalbumin) abnormalities, reduced neuropil.</li>\n<li><em>Late hit:</em> adolescent pruning (C4-mediated, NMDA-dependent) removes excess synapses. In vulnerable brains this goes too far, causing cortical &quot;disconnection&quot;, lower prefrontal glutamate drive to midbrain dopamine neurons, low tonic and excess phasic striatal dopamine, and aberrant salience [1].</li>\n<li><em>Environmental hit:</em> stress (HPA activation), cannabis and social defeat sensitise dopamine release.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Glutamate as the bridge:</strong> glutamate guides migration and plasticity in development and pruning in adolescence, and excess glutamate causes excitotoxicity later. This is the proposed &quot;three-hit glutamate&quot; link between development and degeneration [1].</p>\n<p><strong>Why no gliosis?</strong> Gliosis develops only after the third trimester. Its absence suggests the lesion is early, or that cell loss is through apoptosis rather than necrosis (bcl-2 changes) [1].</p>\n<h3>Evidence for neurodegeneration (the counter-case)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Kraepelin&#39;s original concept of deteriorating course.</li>\n<li><strong>Longer DUP</strong> predicts poorer response, more relapse and worse outcome (Perkins 2005), suggesting active &quot;toxicity&quot; of untreated psychosis [1].</li>\n<li>Each relapse brings slower and less complete recovery; about 1 in 6 relapses leads to treatment resistance.</li>\n<li><strong>Longitudinal MRI:</strong> progressive grey matter loss (frontal, temporal) and ventricular enlargement after onset, greatest in childhood-onset cases and with more relapses. Antipsychotic exposure confounds this (Ho 2011; primate studies) [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Oxidative stress:</strong> reduced SOD, glutathione and catalase; positive symptoms correlate inversely with SOD [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Counter-evidence:</strong> absent gliosis, stable cognition after onset (Heaton 2001; Hoff 2005), and full recovery in a minority [1].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Clinical features in detail — the developmental trajectory</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Stage</th>\n<th>Clinical</th>\n<th>Pathological</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Premorbid</td>\n<td>Delayed milestones, clumsiness, lower IQ, solitary play</td>\n<td>Neurodevelopmental lesion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prodromal</td>\n<td>Social withdrawal, decline in functioning, attenuated symptoms</td>\n<td>Maturational events, pruning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Onset/deterioration</td>\n<td>Psychosis, functional decline</td>\n<td>Neurochemical sensitisation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chronic/residual</td>\n<td>Negative and cognitive symptoms</td>\n<td>Neuroprogression in some</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Adapted from Gupta &amp; Kulhara [1].</p>\n<h3>Assessment in detail</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Neurological soft signs:</strong> Neurological Evaluation Scale (sensory integration, motor coordination, sequencing).</li>\n<li><strong>Minor physical anomalies:</strong> Waldrop scale.</li>\n<li><strong>Premorbid adjustment:</strong> Premorbid Adjustment Scale (Cannon-Spoor).</li>\n<li><strong>Neurocognition:</strong> MATRICS Consensus Cognitive Battery; deficits of about 1 SD are present at first episode [1].</li>\n<li><strong>Genetics:</strong> chromosomal microarray in syndromic cases; 22q11.2 FISH.</li>\n<li><strong>Imaging:</strong> structural MRI to exclude organic causes. Findings are not diagnostic.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Differential — other models of schizophrenia</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Model</th>\n<th>Core idea</th>\n<th>How it relates</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Neurodevelopmental</td>\n<td>Early lesion, late expression</td>\n<td>Explains premorbid signs and onset age</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Neurodegenerative</td>\n<td>Progressive neuronal loss</td>\n<td>Explains deterioration in a subgroup</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Progressive neurodevelopmental</td>\n<td>Both</td>\n<td>Best fit to current data [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dopamine hypothesis (v III, Howes &amp; Kapur 2009)</td>\n<td>Final common pathway: presynaptic striatal dopamine</td>\n<td>Downstream of developmental risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Glutamate/NMDA hypofunction</td>\n<td>Ketamine/PCP model</td>\n<td>Bridges development and degeneration [1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Immune/inflammatory</td>\n<td>Maternal infection, microglia, C4</td>\n<td>Links prenatal and pruning hits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Social defeat / developmental risk-factor model</td>\n<td>Adversity sensitises dopamine</td>\n<td>Environment acting on development</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Management details — what the model changes</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary prevention:</strong> antenatal care, prevention of maternal infection (rubella, influenza vaccination), folate and nutrition, reduction of childhood adversity, and public health campaigns about adolescent cannabis.</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary prevention:</strong> clinical high-risk (CHR) services, mainly CBT and monitoring, and reducing DUP.</li>\n<li><strong>Tertiary prevention:</strong> sustained antipsychotic treatment to avoid relapses (if relapse is toxic), cognitive remediation, and supported employment.</li>\n<li><strong>Investigational neuroprotective approaches:</strong> omega-3 fatty acids (positive early trial, Amminger 2010; not replicated in NEURAPRO), N-acetylcysteine, minocycline, sarcosine and other glycine-site agonists [1], and oestrogen/raloxifene in women [2].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Evidence table</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Study</th>\n<th>Design</th>\n<th>n</th>\n<th>Finding</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Geddes et al., 1999 [1]</td>\n<td>Individual-patient meta-analysis</td>\n<td>700 cases</td>\n<td>Obstetric complications OR ≈ 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cannon et al., 2002 [1]</td>\n<td>Meta-analysis</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Pooled effects &lt;2; hypoxia-related factors strongest</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Susser et al., 1996 [1]</td>\n<td>Dutch famine cohort</td>\n<td>~100,000</td>\n<td>Twofold risk after first-trimester famine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Jones et al., 1994 [1]</td>\n<td>British 1946 birth cohort</td>\n<td>5,362</td>\n<td>Delayed milestones, speech problems, solitary play</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Heaton et al., 2001 [1]</td>\n<td>Longitudinal cognition</td>\n<td>142 cases</td>\n<td>Deficits stable after onset</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rapoport et al., 1997–98 [1]</td>\n<td>Childhood-onset MRI</td>\n<td>15–16</td>\n<td>Accelerated grey matter loss</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sekar et al., 2016</td>\n<td>Genetics/neurobiology</td>\n<td>28,799 cases (PGC)</td>\n<td>C4A expression → complement-mediated pruning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Perkins et al., 2005 [1]</td>\n<td>Meta-analysis</td>\n<td>43 studies</td>\n<td>Longer DUP → poorer response</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Critical appraisal</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Strengths:</strong> convergent evidence from epidemiology, genetics, neuropathology, imaging and cohort studies; explains the premorbid phase and onset age; links to autism and intellectual disability through shared CNVs.</li>\n<li><strong>Weaknesses:</strong><ul>\n<li>Most findings are <strong>non-specific</strong> (also in bipolar disorder, autism, depression) and have small effect sizes.</li>\n<li>Many replications failed (migration abnormalities, influenza) [1].</li>\n<li>The model does not explain <strong>late-onset</strong> schizophrenia (after age 40, more common in women) or <strong>very late-onset</strong> schizophrenia-like psychosis.</li>\n<li>It struggles with relapse–remission course, response to treatment and <strong>full recovery</strong> in up to a fifth of patients [1,3].</li>\n<li>A purely developmental label risks <strong>therapeutic nihilism</strong>. Gupta and Kulhara note the combined view is more optimistic [1].</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Overreach:</strong> calling schizophrenia simply &quot;neurodevelopmental&quot; ignores the heterogeneity: subgroups with strong developmental loading (childhood onset, cognitive impairment) and others with better outcomes and affective features.</li>\n<li><strong>Indian outcome data</strong> (Madras Longitudinal Study: only 5 of 61 patients continuously ill over 20 years) argue against inevitable deterioration and against a fixed lesion [3].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Indian context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Indian contributions include Gupta &amp; Kulhara&#39;s critical review (PGIMER), studies of minor physical anomalies and soft signs (NIMHANS, PGIMER), the Madras and Chandigarh long-term outcome studies, and 22q11.2 and genomic work from NIMHANS (e.g., the Accelerator programme for Discovery in Brain disorders using Stem cells (ADBS)) [1,3].</li>\n<li>Public health relevance: maternal undernutrition, perinatal complications and infections are still common in India, so developmental prevention is relevant here.</li>\n<li>Cannabis is culturally accepted in some regions (bhang), so adolescent education matters.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Recent advances</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Established:</strong> large-scale GWAS (PGC 2022: 287 loci) implicate synaptic and neurodevelopmental genes; CNV burden overlaps with autism and intellectual disability.</li>\n<li><strong>Emerging:</strong> brain organoids from patient iPSCs show altered neurogenesis and synaptic pruning (microglia–neuron co-cultures); normative modelling of brain development finds individual-level deviations rather than a uniform lesion.</li>\n<li><strong>Investigational:</strong> developmental &quot;risk calculators&quot; combining polygenic score, premorbid function and environment; early-life interventions trialled in high-risk offspring.</li>\n</ul>\n","refs":"<ol>\n<li>Gupta S, Kulhara P. What is schizophrenia: a neurodevelopmental or neurodegenerative disorder or a combination of both? A critical analysis. <em>Indian J Psychiatry</em> 2010; 52(1):21–27.</li>\n<li>Mu E, Gurvich C, Kulkarni J. Estrogen and psychosis — a review and future directions. <em>Arch Womens Ment Health</em> 2024; 27:877–885.</li>\n<li>Thara R. Twenty-year course of schizophrenia: the Madras Longitudinal Study. <em>Can J Psychiatry</em> 2004; 49(8):564–569.</li>\n<li>Weinberger DR. Implications of normal brain development for the pathogenesis of schizophrenia. <em>Arch Gen Psychiatry</em> 1987; 44:660–669.</li>\n<li>Murray RM, Lewis SW. Is schizophrenia a neurodevelopmental disorder? <em>BMJ</em> 1987; 295:681–682.</li>\n<li>Sekar A, Bialas AR, de Rivera H, et al. Schizophrenia risk from complex variation of complement component 4. <em>Nature</em> 2016; 530:177–183.</li>\n<li>Howes OD, Kapur S. The dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia: version III — the final common pathway. <em>Schizophr Bull</em> 2009; 35:549–562.</li>\n<li>American Psychiatric Association. <em>DSM-5-TR</em>. 2022; World Health Organization. <em>ICD-11 for Mortality and Morbidity Statistics</em>, 2022 (6A0, 6A20).</li>\n</ol>\n"}},"byQuestion":{"p1-cee74827":"acute-transient-psychotic-disorder-course-outcome","p1-ab084c9a":"antipsychotics-in-clinical-high-risk-for-psychosis","p1-7bcebc12":"applied-schizophrenia-olanzapine-metabolic-syndrome","p1-ef058f3c":"applied-schizophrenia-woman-galactorrhoea-refusing-admission","p1-2a7319ea":"cognitive-impairment-in-schizophrenia","p1-90793542":"cognitive-impairment-in-schizophrenia","p1-6d5b01b1":"early-intervention-in-psychosis","p1-19591112":"early-intervention-in-psychosis","p1-40ad35dc":"early-intervention-in-psychosis","p1-3597bec2":"maintenance-treatment-schizophrenia","p1-79004221":"neuromodulation-in-psychiatry","p1-9c113dab":"neuromodulation-in-psychiatry","p1-445a7a09":"neuromodulation-in-psychiatry","p1-ff12dcb5":"newer-antipsychotics-harm-vs-help","p3-303f02bd":"parkinsons-disease-psychiatric-aspects","p3-fdf72d0c":"parkinsons-disease-psychiatric-aspects","p3-f4a09b39":"parkinsons-disease-psychiatric-aspects","p3-a0ea515e":"parkinsons-disease-psychiatric-aspects","p3-dc17344a":"parkinsons-disease-psychiatric-aspects","p3-868b6f08":"parkinsons-disease-psychiatric-aspects","p1-c764876f":"psychotropic-induced-metabolic-syndrome","p1-43304a4f":"recovery-in-schizophrenia","p1-87119a2b":"rtms-transcranial-magnetic-stimulation","p1-3ea44b90":"rtms-transcranial-magnetic-stimulation","p1-25fbc65c":"schizophrenia-neurodevelopmental-hypothesis"}};
